p><strong>Key idea:</strong> DQL has only ONE command: <code>SELECT</code>.=
 Everything else (<code>FROM</code>, <code>WHERE</code>, <code>GROUP BY</co=
de>, etc.) are <strong>clauses</strong> of the <code>SELECT</code> statemen=
t, not separate commands.</p>
</div>
<h4 id=3D"the-select-statement--its-clauses">The SELECT Statement &amp; Its=
 Clauses</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Clause</th>
<th>Purpose</th>
<th>Example</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>SELECT</code></td>
<td>Specifies <em>which columns</em> to retrieve</td>
<td><code>SELECT first_name, salary</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>FROM</code></td>
<td>Specifies <em>which table(s)</em> to read from</td>
<td><code>FROM employees</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>WHERE</code></td>
<td>Filters rows <em>before</em> grouping (row-level filter)</td>
<td><code>WHERE department =3D 'Sales'</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>GROUP BY</code></td>
<td>Groups rows sharing a value, typically for aggregation</td>
<td><code>GROUP BY department</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>HAVING</code></td>
<td>Filters <em>after</em> grouping (group-level filter)</td>
<td><code>HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 5</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>ORDER BY</code></td>
<td>Sorts the result set (ASC by default)</td>
<td><code>ORDER BY salary DESC</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>DISTINCT</code></td>
<td>Removes duplicate rows from the result</td>
<td><code>SELECT DISTINCT department</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>LIMIT</code></td>
<td>Caps the number of rows returned</td>
<td><code>LIMIT 10</code></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
