p><strong>Why does this matter?</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>You <strong>can't</strong> use a column alias defined in <code>SELECT</=
code> inside a <code>WHERE</code> clause =E2=80=94 because <code>WHERE</cod=
e> runs <em>before</em> <code>SELECT</code>.</li>
<li>You <strong>can</strong> use it in <code>ORDER BY</code> =E2=80=94 beca=
use <code>ORDER BY</code> runs <em>after</em> <code>SELECT</code>.</li>
<li><code>WHERE</code> filters individual rows <em>before</em> grouping. <c=
ode>HAVING</code> filters groups <em>after</em> grouping.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<h4 id=3D"example">Example</h4>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> department, <span class=3D=
"hljs-built_in">COUNT</span>(<span class=3D"hljs-operator">*</span>) <span =
class=3D"hljs-keyword">AS</span> emp_count
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> employees
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> hire_date <span class=3D"hljs-ope=
rator">&gt;</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;2023-01-01&#x27;</span=
>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">GROUP</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">BY</=
span> department
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">HAVING</span> <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">CO=
UNT</span>(<span class=3D"hljs-operator">*</span>) <span class=3D"hljs-oper=
ator">&gt;</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">5</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ORDER</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">BY</=
span> emp_count <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">DESC</span>
LIMIT <span class=3D"hljs-number">3</span>;</code></pre></div>
<p><strong>Step-by-step execution:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>FROM</strong> =E2=86=92 look at the <code>employees</code> tabl=
e</li>
<li><strong>WHERE</strong> =E2=86=92 keep only rows where <code>hire_date</=
code> is after Jan 1, 2023</li>
<li><strong>GROUP BY</strong> =E2=86=92 group the remaining rows by <code>d=
epartment</code></li>
<li><strong>HAVING</strong> =E2=86=92 keep only groups with more than 5 emp=
loyees</li>
<li><strong>SELECT</strong> =E2=86=92 pick the <code>department</code> name=
 and the count</li>
<li><strong>ORDER BY</strong> =E2=86=92 sort by count descending</li>
<li><strong>LIMIT</strong> =E2=86=92 return only the top 3 departments</li>
</ol>
<h4 id=3D"where-vs-having">WHERE vs HAVING</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th><code>WHERE</code></th>
<th><code>HAVING</code></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Filters</td>
<td>Individual rows</td>
<td>Groups (after <code>GROUP BY</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Can use aggregate functions?</td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C No</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 Yes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Runs</td>
<td>Before grouping</td>
<td>After grouping</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Example</td>
<td><code>WHERE salary &gt; 50000</code></td>
<td><code>HAVING AVG(salary) &gt; 50000</code></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h5 id=3D"-query-showing-the-difference">=F0=9F=8E=AF Query showing the dif=
ference</h5>
<p>Sample <code>employees</code> data (assume the <code>salary</code> colum=
