ed back if needed.</p>
</div>
<hr>
<hr>
</section><section class=3D"sec"><h2 id=3D"mysql-data-types" data-toc=3D"2.=
 MySQL Data Types"><span class=3D"sec-n">2.</span> MySQL Data Types</h2>
<p>MySQL provides a wide range of data types grouped into three main catego=
ries: <strong>Numeric</strong>, <strong>String (Text)</strong>, and <strong=
>Date/Time</strong>. Choosing the right data type is critical for storage e=
fficiency, query performance, and data integrity.</p>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"numeric-data-types" data-toc=3D"Numeric Data Types"><strong>Numer=
ic Data Types</strong></h3>
<h4 id=3D"integer-types"><strong>Integer Types</strong></h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Type</th>
<th>Storage</th>
<th>Range (Signed)</th>
<th>Range (Unsigned)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>TINYINT</code></td>
<td>1 byte</td>
<td>-128 to 127</td>
<td>0 to 255</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>SMALLINT</code></td>
<td>2 bytes</td>
<td>-32,768 to 32,767</td>
<td>0 to 65,535</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>MEDIUMINT</code></td>
<td>3 bytes</td>
<td>-8,388,608 to 8,388,607</td>
<td>0 to 16,777,215</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>INT</code> (or <code>INTEGER</code>)</td>
<td>4 bytes</td>
<td>-2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647</td>
<td>0 to 4,294,967,295</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>BIGINT</code></td>
<td>8 bytes</td>
<td>-9,223,372,036,854,775,808 to 9,223,372,036,854,775,807</td>
<td>0 to 18,446,744,073,709,551,615</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>BOOL</code> / <code>BOOLEAN</code></td>
<td>1 byte</td>
<td>Synonym for <code>TINYINT(1)</code></td>
<td>0 =3D false, 1 =3D true</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>When to use:</strong> Use <code>TINYINT</code> for small-range v=
alues like status flags, boolean-style columns, or age. Use <code>SMALLINT<=
/code> for moderately small numbers like year or a limited counter. <code>I=
NT</code> is the most commonly used integer type and works well for primary=
 keys, counters, and general-purpose whole numbers. Reach for <code>BIGINT<=
/code> only when you expect values to exceed the ~2 billion limit of <code>=
INT</code>, such as large-scale ID generators or financial transaction coun=
ts. Always pick the smallest type that safely fits your data to save storag=
e.</p>
<p><strong>Signed vs Unsigned:</strong> By default, integer columns are <st=
rong>signed</strong> (allow negative values). Adding <code>UNSIGNED</code> =
after the type doubles the positive range by removing the negative half. Us=
e <code>UNSIGNED</code> when a column can never be negative, like <code>age=
</code>, <code>quantity</code>, or auto-increment IDs. Example: <code>INT U=
NSIGNED</code> gives you 0 to ~4.3 billion instead of -2.1 billion to +2.1 =
billion.</p>
<h4 id=3D"decimal--floating-point-types"><strong>Decimal / Floating-Point T=
ypes</strong></h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Type</th>
<th>Storage</th>
<th>Range / Precision</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>FLOAT</code></td>
<td>4 bytes</td>
<td>-3.402823466E+38 to +3.402823466E+38 (~7 decimal digits precision)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>DOUBLE</code> (or <code>DOUBLE PRECISION</code>, <code>REAL</code=
>)</td>
<td>8 bytes</td>
<td>-1.7976931348623157E+308 to +1.7976931348623157E+308 (~15 decimal digit=
s precision)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>DECIMAL(M,D)</code> (or <code>DEC</code>, <code>NUMERIC</code>)</=
td>
<td>Varies (~4 bytes per 9 digits)</td>
<td>Exact precision, max range same as DOUBLE. M =3D up to 65 total digits,=
 D =3D up to 30 decimal places</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>What does <code>DECIMAL(M,D)</code> mean?</strong> <code>M</code=
> is the <strong>total number of digits</strong> (both sides of the decimal=
 point combined), and <code>D</code> is the <strong>number of digits after =
the decimal point</strong>. So <code>DECIMAL(10,2)</code> means: up to 10 d=
igits total, with 2 after the decimal point. The digits before the decimal =
=3D <code>M - D</code> =3D 8 digits, giving you a range of <code>-99,999,99=
9.99</code> to <code>99,999,999.99</code>. If you insert <code>49.99</code>=
, MySQL stores exactly <code>49.99</code> with no approximation or binary c=
onversion. Storage varies: roughly 4 bytes per 9 digits (e.g., <code>DECIMA=
L(10,2)</code> uses about 5 bytes). <code>M</code> can go up to 65, and <co=
de>D</code> can go up to 30 (but <code>D</code> must always be less than or=
 equal to <code>M</code>).</p>
<p><strong>Common declarations:</strong></p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Declaration</th>
<th>Meaning</th>
<th>Range</th>
<th>Use Case</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>DECIMAL(10,2)</code></td>
<td>8 digits before decimal, 2 after</td>
<td>-99,999,999.99 to 99,999,999.99</td>
<td>Product prices, order totals</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>DECIMAL(15,4)</code></td>
<td>11 digits before, 4 after</td>
<td>-99,999,999,999.9999 to 99,999,999,999.9999</td>
<td>Banking, financial calculations</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>DECIMAL(18,8)</code></td>
<td>10 digits before, 8 after</td>
<td>Up to 9,999,999,999.99999999</td>
<td>Cryptocurrency (satoshi precision)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>DECIMAL(5,2)</code></td>
<td>3 digits before, 2 after</td>
<td>-999.99 to 999.99</td>
<td>Percentages, small amounts</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>How is it different from FLOAT/DOUBLE internally?</strong> <code=
>FLOAT</code> and <code>DOUBLE</code> convert your number to <strong>binary=
 (base-2)</strong> using IEEE 754. The number <code>0.1</code> cannot be re=
presented exactly in binary, just like <code>1/3</code> cannot be represent=
ed exactly in base-10, so you get tiny errors (<code>0.1 + 0.2 =3D 0.300000=
00000000004</code>). <code>DECIMAL</code> skips binary entirely and stores =
each digit <strong>as-is in base-10</strong>, so <code>0.1</code> is stored=
 as exactly <code>0.1</code>. This is why <code>DECIMAL</code> is the only =
safe choice for money.</p>
<p><strong>When to use:</strong> Use <code>DECIMAL</code> (also known as <c=
ode>NUMERIC</code>) whenever you need exact precision, especially for finan=
cial data like prices, salaries, and account balances =E2=80=94 it stores n=
umbers as exact values and avoids rounding errors. Use <code>FLOAT</code> o=
r <code>DOUBLE</code> for scientific calculations, measurements, or any sce=
nario where a small degree of approximation is acceptable and performance m=
atters more than pinpoint accuracy. <code>DOUBLE</code> gives you more prec=
ision than <code>FLOAT</code> at the cost of double the storage.</p>
<hr>
<h4 id=3D"handling-prices-in-production--the-recurring-decimal-problem"><st=
rong>Handling Prices in Production =E2=80=94 The Recurring Decimal Problem<=
/strong></h4>
<p>Storing prices seems simple until you run into cases like:</p>
<ul>
<li>A bill of <strong>$100 split 3 ways</strong> =E2=86=92 $33.333333... (r=
ecurring)</li>
<li><strong>Tax calculation:</strong> $49.99 =C3=97 7.25% =3D $3.624275 (ne=
eds rounding)</li>
<li><strong>Currency conversion:</strong> 100 USD =C3=97 0.8333... EUR/USD =
(recurring)</li>
<li><strong>Discount:</strong> 10% off $9.99 =3D $0.999 (needs rounding)</l=
i>
</ul>
<p>These aren't edge cases =E2=80=94 they happen in every e-commerce system=
, every day. Let's look at how production systems actually handle this.</p>
<h5 id=3D"the-core-problem-why-do-recurring-decimals-exist">The Core Proble=
m: Why Do Recurring Decimals Exist?</h5>
<p>Not every fraction can be represented exactly in decimal. <code>1/3 =3D =
0.333...</code> goes on forever. When you store this in a <code>DECIMAL(10,=
2)</code> column, MySQL <strong>truncates or rounds</strong> to <code>0.33<=
/code>. That missing <code>$0.003333...</code> per person, over millions of=
 transactions, adds up.</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Splitting $100 three ways</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">100<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">/</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">3=
</span>;
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Result: 33.3333  (MySQL default 4 decimal p=
laces)</span>

<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">CA=
ST</span>(<span class=3D"hljs-number">100</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operat=
or">/</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">3</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keywo=
rd">AS</span> <span class=3D"hljs-type">DECIMAL</span>(<span class=3D"hljs-=
number">10</span>,<span class=3D"hljs-number">2</span>));
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Result: 33.33</span>

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- But 33.33 =C3=97 3 =3D 99.99 =E2=80=94 wher=
e did the missing $0.01 go?</span></code></pre></div>
<h5 id=3D"never-use-float-or-double-for-money">Never Use FLOAT or DOUBLE fo=
r Money</h5>
<p>Before we solve recurring decimals, let's understand why <code>FLOAT</co=
de>/<code>DOUBLE</code> are completely unacceptable for financial data:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- FLOAT horror show</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TAB=
LE</span> bad_prices (price <span class=3D"hljs-type">FLOAT</span>);
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">INSERT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">INT=
O</span> bad_prices <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">VALUES</span> (<span class=
=3D"hljs-number">0.1</span>), (<span class=3D"hljs-number">0.2</span>);

<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">SU=
M</span>(price) <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> bad_prices;
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Expected: 0.3</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Actual:   0.30000000447034836  =E2=86=90 WR=
ONG</span>

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- DECIMAL does this correctly</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TAB=
LE</span> good_prices (price <span class=3D"hljs-type">DECIMAL</span>(<span=
 class=3D"hljs-number">10</span>,<span class=3D"hljs-number">2</span>));
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">INSERT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">INT=
O</span> good_prices <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">VALUES</span> (<span clas=
s=3D"hljs-number">0.1</span>), (<span class=3D"hljs-number">0.2</span>);

<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">SU=
M</span>(price) <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> good_prices;
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Result: 0.30  =E2=86=90 CORRECT</span></cod=
e></pre></div>
<p><code>FLOAT</code> and <code>DOUBLE</code> use <strong>binary floating-p=
oint</strong> (IEEE 754), which cannot represent <code>0.1</code> exactly i=
n binary =E2=80=94 just like <code>1/3</code> can't be represented exactly =
in decimal. <code>DECIMAL</code> stores each digit as-is (base-10), so <cod=
e>0.1</code> is stored as exactly <code>0.1</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Rule: Always use <code>DECIMAL</code> for money. No exceptions.<=
/strong></p>
<h5 id=3D"production-strategy-1-store-prices-in-smallest-currency-unit-as-i=
nt-most-common">Production Strategy 1: Store Prices in Smallest Currency Un=
it as INT (Most Common)</h5>
<p>The most widely used approach in production (Stripe, Shopify, Amazon) is=
 to <strong>avoid decimals entirely</strong> by storing amounts in the <str=
ong>smallest currency unit</strong> (cents, paise, etc.) as an integer.</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Instead of this:</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TAB=
LE</span> orders (
    price <span class=3D"hljs-type">DECIMAL</span>(<span class=3D"hljs-numb=
er">10</span>,<span class=3D"hljs-number">2</span>)    <span class=3D"hljs-=
comment">-- $49.99</span>
);

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Do this:</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TAB=
LE</span> orders (
    price_cents <span class=3D"hljs-type">INT</span>         <span class=3D=
"hljs-comment">-- 4999 (meaning $49.99)</span>
);</code></pre></div>
<p><strong>Why this works:</strong></p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Aspect</th>
<th>DECIMAL(10,2)</th>
<th>INT (cents)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Stores $49.99 as</td>
<td><code>49.99</code></td>
<td><code>4999</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Stores $100.00 as</td>
<td><code>100.00</code></td>
<td><code>10000</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Recurring decimal risk</td>
<td>Still possible in calculations</td>
<td>Eliminated =E2=80=94 all values are whole numbers</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Arithmetic precision</td>
<td>Exact for storage, rounding needed for division</td>
<td>Exact =E2=80=94 integer math has no precision loss</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Performance</td>
<td>Slightly slower (variable-length)</td>
<td>Faster =E2=80=94 fixed 4 bytes, native CPU operations</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>How the split-the-bill problem is solved with cents:</strong></p=
>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- $100.00 =3D 10000 cents, split 3 ways=
</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">1000=
0</span> DIV <span class=3D"hljs-number">3</span>;          <span class=3D"=
hljs-comment">-- Result: 3333 (integer division, no decimals)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- 3333 cents =3D $33.33</span>

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- But 3333 * 3 =3D 9999 cents =3D $99.99 =E2=
=80=94 still 1 cent missing!</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Solution: assign the remainder to the last =
person</span></code></pre></div>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">Java</span></div><pre><code class=3D=
"hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-type">int</span> <span class=3D"hljs-variable">t=
otalCents</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hl=
js-number">10000</span>;
<span class=3D"hljs-type">int</span> <span class=3D"hljs-variable">people</=
span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">=
3</span>;
<span class=3D"hljs-type">int</span> <span class=3D"hljs-variable">perPerso=
n</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> totalCents / people;     =
    <span class=3D"hljs-comment">// 3333</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-type">int</span> <span class=3D"hljs-variable">remainde=
r</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> totalCents % people;     =
    <span class=3D"hljs-comment">// 1</span>

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">// Person 1: 3333 cents ($33.33)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">// Person 2: 3333 cents ($33.33)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">// Person 3: 3333 + 1 =3D 3334 cents ($33.34) =
 =E2=86=90 gets the extra cent</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">// Total: 3333 + 3333 + 3334 =3D 10000</span><=
/code></pre></div>
<p>This is called the <strong>&quot;last person absorbs the remainder&quot;=
</strong> pattern. It's simple, deterministic, and accounts for every cent.=
</p>
<p><strong>Display conversion (cents to dollars) happens only in the presen=
tation layer:</strong></p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">Java</span></div><pre><code class=3D=
"hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-type">int</span> <span class=3D"hljs-variable">p=
riceCents</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hl=
js-number">4999</span>;

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">// Only convert for display</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-type">String</span> <span class=3D"hljs-variable">displ=
ay</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> String.format(<span clas=
s=3D"hljs-string">&quot;$%.2f&quot;</span>, priceCents / <span class=3D"hlj=
s-number">100.0</span>);  <span class=3D"hljs-comment">// &quot;$49.99&quot=
;</span>

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">// API response</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">// { &quot;price&quot;: 4999, &quot;currency&q=
uot;: &quot;USD&quot;, &quot;display&quot;: &quot;$49.99&quot; }</span></co=
de></pre></div>
<h5 id=3D"production-strategy-2-decimal-with-controlled-rounding">Productio=
n Strategy 2: DECIMAL with Controlled Rounding</h5>
<p>If you need to store fractional amounts (e.g., crypto prices, unit rates=
), use <code>DECIMAL</code> with <strong>more precision than you display</s=
trong> and round explicitly.</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Store with extra precision (4 decimal=
 places)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TAB=
LE</span> products (
    unit_price <span class=3D"hljs-type">DECIMAL</span>(<span class=3D"hljs=
-number">12</span>,<span class=3D"hljs-number">4</span>)    <span class=3D"=
hljs-comment">-- $33.3333</span>
);

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Round to 2 decimal places only at the final=
 step</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> ROUND(unit_price, <span class=3D=
"hljs-number">2</span>) <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">AS</span> display_pric=
e <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> products;
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- 33.3333 becomes 33.33</span></code></pre></=
div>
<p><strong>The key rule: carry extra precision through calculations, round =
only at the end.</strong></p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Bad: round at each step (error accumu=
lates)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SET</span> <span class=3D"hljs-variable">@subt=
otal</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> ROUND(<span class=3D"h=
ljs-number">33.33</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">2</span>);           =
       <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- 33.33</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SET</span> <span class=3D"hljs-variable">@tax<=
