blocks at T=3D30: 25 seconds of downtime even for an INSTANT ALTER" title=
=3D"MDL trap timeline: slow SELECT T=3D0 to T=3D30, ALTER waits from T=3D5,=
 new queries queue from T=3D5+, everything unblocks at T=3D30: 25 seconds o=
f downtime even for an INSTANT ALTER" loading=3D"lazy"></figure>
<p><strong>Prevention:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Set <code>lock_wait_timeout</code> so the ALTER gives up instead of bloc=
king everything:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SET</span> SESSION lock_wait_timeout <sp=
an class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">5</span>;=
  <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Give up after 5 seconds</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ALTER</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TABL=
E</span> users <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ADD</span> <span class=3D"hljs-=
keyword">COLUMN</span> bio TEXT;
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- If the metadata lock isn&#x27;t available i=
n 5 seconds, the ALTER fails</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- instead of causing a cascading pile-up</spa=
n></code></pre></div>
</li>
<li>
<p>Kill long-running queries before running the ALTER</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Use <code>pt-online-schema-change</code> (Percona) or <code>gh-ost</code=
> (GitHub) for large production tables =E2=80=94 these create a shadow copy=
 and swap tables, avoiding metadata-lock issues entirely (see <a href=3D"#p=
roduction-tools-for-zero-downtime-alter">Production Tools for Zero-Downtime=
 ALTER</a> below)</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Always run ALTER during low-traffic windows</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"advantages-of-alter-table" data-toc=3D"Advantages of ALTER TABLE"=
>Advantages of ALTER TABLE</h3>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Advantage</th>
<th>Explanation</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Schema evolution</strong></td>
<td>Lets you adapt your database schema as requirements change =E2=80=94 ad=
d new features, support new data, without recreating tables</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Data integrity</strong></td>
<td>Adding constraints (<code>NOT NULL</code>, <code>UNIQUE</code>, <code>F=
OREIGN KEY</code>) via ALTER ensures data quality at the database level</td=
>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Index management</strong></td>
<td>You can add or drop indexes to tune query performance based on real-wor=
ld workload analysis</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Online DDL (5.6+)</strong></td>
<td>Most common operations (add column, add index) can run without downtime=
 in modern MySQL</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>INSTANT operations (8.0+)</strong></td>
<td>Adding a column is now instantaneous regardless of table size =E2=80=94=
 just a metadata change</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Standardized</strong></td>
<td>Part of the SQL standard =E2=80=94 every developer and ORM knows how to=
 use it</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"disadvantages-of-alter-table" data-toc=3D"Disadvantages of ALTER =
TABLE">Disadvantages of ALTER TABLE</h3>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Disadvantage</th>
<th>Explanation</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Table lock for some operations</strong></td>
<td>Changing a column's data type, converting charset, or adding a <code>FU=
LLTEXT</code> index still requires a full table copy with a lock =E2=80=94 =
can mean hours of downtime on large tables</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Metadata lock cascading</strong></td>
<td>Even fast operations can cause cascading query pile-ups if a long-runni=
ng query is holding the metadata lock (explained above)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Replication lag</strong></td>
<td>On replicas (in statement-based replication), the <code>ALTER TABLE</co=
de> runs again from scratch =E2=80=94 a 2-hour ALTER on the primary means 2=
 hours of replication lag</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Disk space</strong></td>
<td><code>COPY</code> and <code>INPLACE</code> operations need <strong>extr=
a disk space</strong> equal to the table's size (for the temporary copy or =
rebuilt data). If your disk is 80% full, the ALTER may fail</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Cannot rollback DDL</strong></td>
<td><code>ALTER TABLE</code> is auto-committed =E2=80=94 there's no <code>R=
OLLBACK</code>. If you add a wrong column, you must run another <code>ALTER=
 TABLE</code> to fix it</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Risk of data loss</strong></td>
<td>Changing a column from <code>VARCHAR(200)</code> to <code>VARCHAR(50)</=
code> silently truncates data. Changing <code>BIGINT</code> to <code>INT</c=
ode> can cause overflow errors</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Coordination required</strong></td>
<td>In large teams, uncoordinated schema changes can break application code=
 that assumes the old schema</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Partitioned table overhead</strong></td>
<td><code>ALTER TABLE</code> on partitioned tables is slower because it mus=
t modify every partition's data file</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h4 id=3D"production-tools-for-zero-downtime-alter">Production Tools for Ze=
ro-Downtime ALTER</h4>
<p>For operations that still require a <code>COPY</code>, teams often use e=
xternal tools that avoid locking:</p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Tool</th>
<th>How It Works</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong><code>pt-online-schema-change</code></strong> (Percona Toolkit)=
</td>
<td>Creates a new table with the desired schema, uses triggers to copy data=
 and capture live changes, then swaps atomically</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong><code>gh-ost</code></strong> (GitHub)</td>
<td>Similar concept but uses the binary log instead of triggers =E2=80=94 n=
o trigger overhead, more controllable</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong><code>fb-mysql-osc</code></strong> (Facebook)</td>
<td>Facebook's variant, optimized for their scale</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>These tools let you run even a full table rebuild (<code>COPY</code> ope=
ration) without locking the table =E2=80=94 at the cost of additional compl=
exity and longer total execution time.</p>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"the-future-columns-strategy--pre-allocating-columns-to-avoid-alte=
r" data-toc=3D"The &quot;Future Columns&quot; Strategy =E2=80=94 Pre-Alloca=
ting =E2=80=A6">The &quot;Future Columns&quot; Strategy =E2=80=94 Pre-Alloc=
ating Columns to Avoid ALTER</h3>
<p>One strategy to avoid <code>ALTER TABLE</code> entirely is to <strong>pr=
e-create extra columns</strong> upfront that you don't need yet =E2=80=94 &=
quot;reserving&quot; them for future use.</p>
<h4 id=3D"how-it-works">How It Works</h4>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keywor=
d">TABLE</span> users (
    id           <span class=3D"hljs-type">BIGINT</span> <span class=3D"hlj=
s-keyword">PRIMARY</span> KEY AUTO_INCREMENT,
    name         <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class=3D"hl=
js-number">100</span>),
    email        <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class=3D"hl=
js-number">255</span>),
    created_at   <span class=3D"hljs-type">TIMESTAMP</span> <span class=3D"=
hljs-keyword">DEFAULT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">CURRENT_TIMESTAM=
P</span>,
   =20
    <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Reserved columns for future use</span>
    extra_int_1    <span class=3D"hljs-type">INT</span> <span class=3D"hljs=
-keyword">DEFAULT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">NULL</span>,
    extra_int_2    <span class=3D"hljs-type">INT</span> <span class=3D"hljs=
-keyword">DEFAULT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">NULL</span>,
    extra_int_3    <span class=3D"hljs-type">INT</span> <span class=3D"hljs=
-keyword">DEFAULT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">NULL</span>,
    extra_str_1    <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class=3D"=
hljs-number">255</span>) <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">DEFAULT</span> <span =
class=3D"hljs-keyword">NULL</span>,
    extra_str_2    <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class=3D"=
hljs-number">255</span>) <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">DEFAULT</span> <span =
class=3D"hljs-keyword">NULL</span>,
    extra_str_3    <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class=3D"=
hljs-number">255</span>) <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">DEFAULT</span> <span =
class=3D"hljs-keyword">NULL</span>,
    extra_text_1   TEXT <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">DEFAULT</span> <span c=
lass=3D"hljs-keyword">NULL</span>,
    extra_date_1   <span class=3D"hljs-type">DATE</span> <span class=3D"hlj=
s-keyword">DEFAULT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">NULL</span>,
    extra_json_1   JSON <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">DEFAULT</span> <span c=
lass=3D"hljs-keyword">NULL</span>
);</code></pre></div>
<p>Later, when you need a new column:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Instead of: ALTER TABLE users ADD COL=
UMN phone VARCHAR(20);</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- You just START USING an existing column:</s=
pan>

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Application code simply starts reading/writ=
ing extra_str_1 as &quot;phone&quot;</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Maybe rename it for clarity (INSTANT operat=
ion, no data copy):</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ALTER</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TABL=
E</span> users RENAME <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">COLUMN</span> extra_str_=
1 <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TO</span> phone;</code></pre></div>
<p>You skip the <code>ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN</code> entirely =E2=80=94 the =
column already exists. You just start writing data to it. If the column's e=
xisting type doesn't match what you need exactly, at worst you rename it (w=
hich is <code>INSTANT</code>).</p>
<h4 id=3D"advantages-of-future-columns">Advantages of Future Columns</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Advantage</th>
<th>Explanation</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Zero downtime</strong></td>
<td>No <code>ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN</code> needed =E2=80=94 the column is a=
lready there. Just start using it.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>No metadata lock risk</strong></td>
<td>Since you're not running DDL, there's no metadata lock contention at al=
l</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>No replication lag</strong></td>
<td>Nothing to replicate =E2=80=94 the schema hasn't changed</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>No disk space spike</strong></td>
<td>No temporary table copy, no extra disk needed</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Works on any MySQL version</strong></td>
<td>Doesn't depend on Online DDL or <code>INSTANT</code> algorithm =E2=80=
=94 even MySQL 5.5 works fine</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Predictable schema</strong></td>
<td>DevOps teams know the schema won't change unexpectedly, simplifying mig=
ration and deployment pipelines</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Good for high-frequency schema iterations</strong></td>
<td>If your product is evolving rapidly and you'd otherwise ALTER daily, pr=
e-allocated columns save repeated DDL risk</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h4 id=3D"disadvantages-of-future-columns">Disadvantages of Future Columns<=
/h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Disadvantage</th>
<th>Explanation</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Unclear schema</strong></td>
<td><code>extra_str_1</code>, <code>extra_int_2</code> are meaningless =E2=
=80=94 developers can't understand the table by looking at the schema. Self=
-documenting column names are lost</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Type mismatch</strong></td>
<td>You reserved <code>INT</code> but now need <code>DECIMAL(10,2)</code>? =
You're stuck =E2=80=94 either misuse the column or fall back to <code>ALTER=
 TABLE</code> anyway</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Wasted storage</strong></td>
<td>Every <code>NULL</code> column in InnoDB costs 1 bit in the null bitmap=
 per row, plus the column metadata. For <code>VARCHAR</code>/<code>TEXT</co=
de> columns, the overhead is minimal when <code>NULL</code>, but it still a=
dds up across billions of rows</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Row size bloat</strong></td>
<td>InnoDB has a row size limit (~8 KB for inline data). Pre-allocating man=
y columns eats into this budget, especially if you reserve wide <code>VARCH=
AR</code> columns</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Guessing the future</strong></td>
<td>You can't predict what data types you'll need. Reserve too few columns =
=E2=86=92 still need ALTER. Reserve too many =E2=86=92 waste and confusion.=
 Reserve the wrong types =E2=86=92 useless</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>No constraints</strong></td>
<td>You can't pre-add <code>NOT NULL</code>, <code>UNIQUE</code>, or <code>=
FOREIGN KEY</code> constraints on columns whose purpose you don't know yet.=
 Adding these later requires ALTER anyway</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>ORM and code confusion</strong></td>
<td>JPA/Hibernate entities would have fields like <code>extraStr1</code> an=
d <code>extraInt2</code> =E2=80=94 confusing to develop with. Column rename=
s help but add churn</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Index limitations</strong></td>
<td>You can't pre-create useful indexes on columns whose query patterns are=
 unknown. Adding indexes later still requires ALTER (though <code>ADD INDEX=
</code> is <code>INPLACE</code> and online)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Anti-pattern in relational design</strong></td>
<td>It violates normalization principles. The schema should describe the da=
ta, not anticipate hypothetical future data. This approach trades schema cl=
arity for operational convenience</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h4 id=3D"a-better-alternative-the-json-column-approach">A Better Alternati=
ve: The JSON Column Approach</h4>
<p>Instead of reserving typed columns, a more flexible &quot;future-proof&q=
uot; strategy is using a single <code>JSON</code> column for extensible att=
ributes:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keywor=
d">TABLE</span> users (
    id           <span class=3D"hljs-type">BIGINT</span> <span class=3D"hlj=
s-keyword">PRIMARY</span> KEY AUTO_INCREMENT,
    name         <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class=3D"hl=
js-number">100</span>),
    email        <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class=3D"hl=
js-number">255</span>),
    created_at   <span class=3D"hljs-type">TIMESTAMP</span> <span class=3D"=
hljs-keyword">DEFAULT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">CURRENT_TIMESTAM=
P</span>,
   =20
    metadata     JSON <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">DEFAULT</span> <span cla=
ss=3D"hljs-keyword">NULL</span>    <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Extensib=
le attributes live here</span>
);</code></pre></div>
<p>When you need a new field:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- No ALTER TABLE needed =E2=80=94 just =
store it in the JSON column</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">UPDATE</span> users <span class=3D"hljs-keywor=
d">SET</span> metadata <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> JSON_SET(<s=
pan class=3D"hljs-built_in">COALESCE</span>(metadata, <span class=3D"hljs-s=
tring">&#x27;{}&#x27;</span>), <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;$.phone&#x=
27;</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;+1-555-1234&#x27;</span>)
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> id <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=
=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">42</span>;

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Query it:</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> id, name, metadata<span class=3D=
"hljs-operator">-</span><span class=3D"hljs-operator">&gt;&gt;</span><span =
class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;$.phone&#x27;</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keywor=
d">AS</span> phone <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> users <span cla=
ss=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> id <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span=
> <span class=3D"hljs-number">42</span>;

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- When you&#x27;re sure the field is permanen=
t and heavily queried,</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- THEN promote it to a proper column via ALTE=
R TABLE</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ALTER</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TABL=
E</span> users <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ADD</span> <span class=3D"hljs-=
keyword">COLUMN</span> phone <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span=
 class=3D"hljs-number">20</span>), ALGORITHM<span class=3D"hljs-operator">=
=3D</span>INSTANT;
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">UPDATE</span> users <span class=3D"hljs-keywor=
d">SET</span> phone <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> metadata<span =
class=3D"hljs-operator">-</span><span class=3D"hljs-operator">&gt;&gt;</spa=
n><span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;$.phone&#x27;</span> <span class=3D"hlj=
s-keyword">WHERE</span> metadata<span class=3D"hljs-operator">-</span><span=
 class=3D"hljs-operator">&gt;&gt;</span><span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;$=
.phone&#x27;</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">IS</span> <span class=3D"h=
ljs-keyword">NOT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">NULL</span>;</code></p=
re></div>
<p><strong>Why JSON is better than reserved columns:</strong></p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Aspect</th>
<th>Reserved Columns</th>
<th>JSON Column</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>New field without ALTER?</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 Yes (if matching type exists)</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 Yes (always)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Type flexibility</td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C Fixed =E2=80=94 must match reserved type</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 Any type =E2=80=94 strings, numbers, arrays, nested objects</=
td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Schema clarity</td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C Poor =E2=80=94 <code>extra_int_1</code> is meaningless</td>
<td>=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F Moderate =E2=80=94 field names are in the JSON keys =
(<code>$.phone</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Number of future fields</td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C Limited by how many you reserved</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 Unlimited</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Query performance</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 Regular column =E2=80=94 full index support</td>
<td>=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F Slower =E2=80=94 JSON extraction has overhead; needs=
 generated columns for indexing</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Storage efficiency</td>
<td>=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F Null bitmap overhead per reserved column</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 Single column =E2=80=94 only stores data that exists</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Indexing</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 Standard indexes</td>
<td>=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F Requires virtual generated column + index for fast q=
ueries</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>Indexing JSON fields (when they become hot):</strong></p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Create a virtual generated column fro=
m the JSON field</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ALTER</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TABL=
E</span> users <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ADD</span> <span class=3D"hljs-=
keyword">COLUMN</span> phone <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span=
 class=3D"hljs-number">20</span>)
    GENERATED ALWAYS <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">AS</span> (metadata<span =
class=3D"hljs-operator">-</span><span class=3D"hljs-operator">&gt;&gt;</spa=
n><span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;$.phone&#x27;</span>) VIRTUAL;

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Index it</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> INDEX idx_phone <span class=3D"h=
ljs-keyword">ON</span> users (phone);

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Now this query uses the index:</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> users <span class=3D"hljs-k=
eyword">WHERE</span> phone <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> <span c=
lass=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;+1-555-1234&#x27;</span>;</code></pre></div>
<h4 id=3D"when-to-use-each-strategy">When to Use Each Strategy</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Scenario</th>
<th>Recommended Approach</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>MySQL 8.0+ with small-to-moderate tables</td>
<td>Just use <code>ALTER TABLE</code> with <code>INSTANT</code> =E2=80=94 a=
dding a column is instantaneous</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>MySQL 8.0+ with massive tables (billions of rows)</td>
<td><code>ALTER TABLE</code> with <code>INSTANT</code> for adding columns; =
<code>pt-online-schema-change</code> or <code>gh-ost</code> for type change=
s</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Rapidly evolving schema (startup, MVP phase)</td>
<td><strong>JSON column</strong> for experimental fields =E2=86=92 promote =
to real columns when stable</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Legacy MySQL (5.5 or older) with large tables</td>
<td>Reserved columns may be justified if you truly can't afford the downtim=
e of ALTER</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Very high-traffic tables where even metadata locks are risky</td>
<td><strong>JSON column</strong> avoids DDL entirely; combine with <code>lo=
ck_wait_timeout</code> for any eventual ALTER</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
