<p><strong>Other issues with OFFSET/LIMIT:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Data inconsistency</strong> =E2=80=94 If a row is inserted or d=
eleted while the user is paginating, they may see <strong>duplicate items</=
strong> or <strong>skip entries</strong> entirely</li>
<li><strong>Performance is O(OFFSET + LIMIT)</strong> =E2=80=94 gets linear=
ly worse as you go deeper</li>
</ul>
<h3 id=3D"approach-2-deferred-join-keep-offsetlimit-just-make-it-fast" data=
-toc=3D"Approach 2: Deferred Join (Keep OFFSET/LIMIT, Just =E2=80=A6">Appro=
ach 2: Deferred Join (Keep OFFSET/LIMIT, Just Make It Fast)</h3>
<div class=3D"markdown-alert markdown-alert-note"><p class=3D"markdown-aler=
t-title"><svg class=3D"octicon octicon-info mr-2" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 16" ver=
sion=3D"1.1" width=3D"16" height=3D"16" aria-hidden=3D"true"><path d=3D"M0 =
8a8 8 0 1 1 16 0A8 8 0 0 1 0 8Zm8-6.5a6.5 6.5 0 1 0 0 13 6.5 6.5 0 0 0 0-13=
ZM6.5 7.75A.75.75 0 0 1 7.25 7h1a.75.75 0 0 1 .75.75v2.75h.25a.75.75 0 0 1 =
0 1.5h-2a.75.75 0 0 1 0-1.5h.25v-2h-.25a.75.75 0 0 1-.75-.75ZM8 6a1 1 0 1 1=
 0-2 1 1 0 0 1 0 2Z"></path></svg>Note</p><p>Also known as a <strong>&quot;=
late row lookup&quot;</strong>. Real-world implementations: <a href=3D"http=
s://github.com/planetscale/fast_page" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener">Fa=
stPage</a> (Rails), <a href=3D"https://github.com/hammerstonedev/fast-pagin=
ate" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener">Fast Paginate</a> (Laravel).</p>
</div>
<p>Cursor pagination (Approach 3) is strictly better =E2=80=94 <strong>but =
you can't always use it</strong>. If the product demands numbered pages (&q=
uot;Page 47 of 2,000&quot;), a &quot;jump to last page&quot; button, or a s=
ortable admin grid, you're stuck with <code>OFFSET</code>. A <strong>deferr=
ed join</strong> keeps <code>OFFSET/LIMIT</code> semantics but strips out m=
ost of its cost.</p>
<h4 id=3D"step-1--understand-what-actually-makes-offset-slow">Step 1 =E2=80=
=94 Understand What <em>Actually</em> Makes OFFSET Slow</h4>
<p>It is <strong>not</strong> the counting. Counting to 450,000 is nothing =
for a CPU. The real cost is <strong>what MySQL has to carry while it counts=
</strong>.</p>
<p>Take this query, with an index on <code>(price, id)</code>:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operat=
or">*</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> products <span class=
=3D"hljs-keyword">ORDER</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">BY</span> price=
, id LIMIT <span class=3D"hljs-number">20</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keywor=
d">OFFSET</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">450000</span>;</code></pre></d=
iv>
