</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>SELECT id FROM products ORDER BY price, id</code></td>
<td>The <strong>inner/derived</strong> query. Selects <em>only</em> the pri=
mary key.</td>
<td>Every column it needs (<code>price</code>, <code>id</code>) lives insid=
e the index <code>(price, id)</code> =E2=86=92 this is a <strong>covering i=
ndex scan</strong>. MySQL never touches the table data at all. <code>EXPLAI=
N</code> shows <code>Using index</code>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>LIMIT 20 OFFSET 450000</code></td>
<td>Does the skipping <strong>here</strong>, on index entries.</td>
<td>Skipping 450,000 =C3=97 ~20-byte entries that sit packed and pre-sorted=
 in the same index pages. Sequential reads, no random I/O, no row assembly.=
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>AS page</code></td>
<td>Names the derived table (MySQL requires an alias).</td>
<td>The <code>LIMIT</code> inside also <strong>prevents MySQL from merging<=
/strong> this subquery back into the outer query =E2=80=94 it's forced to m=
aterialize it first. That's exactly what we want.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>INNER JOIN products AS p USING (id)</code></td>
<td>Joins those 20 IDs back to the real table.</td>
<td>20 primary-key lookups. <strong>Not 450,020.</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>ORDER BY p.price, p.id</code> (outer)</td>
<td>Re-sorts the final result.</td>
<td>A <code>JOIN</code> gives <strong>no ordering guarantee</strong> =E2=80=
=94 dropping this returns the right 20 rows in the wrong order. Sorting 20 =
rows is free.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h4 id=3D"step-3--why-this-is-actually-faster-the-numbers">Step 3 =E2=80=94=
 Why This Is <em>Actually</em> Faster (The Numbers)</h4>
<p><code>products</code> =3D 1M rows, avg row =E2=89=88 600 bytes (name, de=
scription, etc.), index entry =E2=89=88 20 bytes.</p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th>=E2=9D=8C Na=C3=AFve <code>OFFSET 450000</code></th>
<th>=E2=9C=85 Deferred Join</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Index entries walked</td>
<td>450,020</td>
<td>450,020 <em>(same!)</em></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Full-row lookups</strong></td>
<td><strong>450,020</strong></td>
<td><strong>20</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Data actually read</td>
<td>~450,020 =C3=97 600 B =E2=89=88 <strong>270 MB</strong></td>
<td>~450,020 =C3=97 20 B =E2=89=88 <strong>9 MB</strong> + 20 rows</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>I/O pattern</td>
<td>Random B+ Tree descents</td>
<td>Sequential scan within index pages</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Rows returned</td>
<td>20</td>
<td>20</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>~30=C3=97 less data, and almost none of it random I/O.</strong> =
PlanetScale's benchmark shows deferred joins staying near-flat across 2,000=
 pages while plain offset degrades badly.</p>
<p>Verify it yourself with <code>EXPLAIN</code>:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- The expensive half</span>
EXPLAIN <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-oper=
ator">*</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> products <span clas=
s=3D"hljs-keyword">ORDER</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">BY</span> pric=
e, id LIMIT <span class=3D"hljs-number">20</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keywo=
rd">OFFSET</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">450000</span>;
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">--   key: idx_price_id | rows: 450020 | Extra:=
 (empty)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">--   =E2=86=91 no &quot;Using index&quot;  =E2=
=86=92 table lookups ARE happening</span>

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- The cheap half (the deferred join&#x27;s su=
bquery)</span>
EXPLAIN <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> id <span class=3D"hljs-k=
eyword">FROM</span> products <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ORDER</span> <spa=
n class=3D"hljs-keyword">BY</span> price, id LIMIT <span class=3D"hljs-numb=
er">20</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">OFFSET</span> <span class=3D"hlj=
s-number">450000</span>;
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">--   key: idx_price_id | rows: 450020 | Extra:=
 Using index</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">--   =E2=86=91 &quot;Using index&quot; =3D COV=
ERING =E2=86=92 never touches the table  =F0=9F=8E=89</span></code></pre></=
div>
<p><code>Using index</code> in the <code>Extra</code> column is the whole t=
rick. If you don't see it in your subquery, the deferred join won't help =
=E2=80=94 your index doesn't cover the <code>ORDER BY</code>.</p>
