GET /api/products?limit=3D20&amp;sort=3Dprice_asc&amp;cursor=3DeyJ2IjoxLCJz=
b3J0Ijoi...</code></pre></div>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Frontend rule</th>
<th>Why</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Omit <code>cursor</code> on the first page</strong></td>
<td>Absence of a cursor <em>is</em> the signal for &quot;start from the beg=
inning&quot;</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Send <code>next_cursor</code> back verbatim</strong> =E2=80=94 =
never decode, edit, or construct it</td>
<td>It's opaque by design. If clients start parsing it, you can never chang=
e the sort key or add a tiebreaker without breaking them</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Resend the same <code>sort</code> + filters with every page</st=
rong></td>
<td>The cursor is only valid for the exact query it was minted under</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Reset to page 1 (drop the cursor) whenever a filter or sort cha=
nges</strong></td>
<td>An old cursor means nothing under a new sort order</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Stop when <code>next_cursor</code> is <code>null</code></strong=
></td>
<td>Don't rely on <code>data.length &lt; limit</code> alone</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
