<p>With 100 authors, this is 101 queries. With 10,000 authors =E2=86=92 10,=
001 queries. <strong>Each query involves a separate network round trip</str=
ong>, so even if each query is fast (1ms), 10,001 queries =3D <strong>10 se=
conds</strong> of just network overhead.</p>
<h3 id=3D"solution-1-use-a-join-best--1-query" data-toc=3D"Solution 1: Use =
a JOIN (Best =E2=80=94 1 Query)">Solution 1: Use a JOIN (Best =E2=80=94 1 Q=
uery)</h3>
<p>Fetch everything in a <strong>single query</strong> using a JOIN:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- =E2=9C=85 1 query =E2=80=94 gets ALL =
authors and ALL their books at once</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> a.name, b.title
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> authors a
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">LEFT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">JOIN<=
/span> books b <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ON</span> a.id <span class=3D"h=
ljs-operator">=3D</span> b.author_id;</code></pre></div>
<p>MySQL fetches everything in one round trip. The database does the heavy =
lifting instead of your application code.</p>
<h3 id=3D"solution-2-batch-with-in-clause-2-queries" data-toc=3D"Solution 2=
: Batch with IN Clause (2 Queries)">Solution 2: Batch with IN Clause (2 Que=
ries)</h3>
<p>If a JOIN creates too many duplicate rows (e.g., authors with many books=
), use two queries with an <code>IN</code> clause:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Query 1: Get all authors</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> authors;

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Query 2: Get ALL books for ALL those author=
s in ONE query</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> books <span class=3D"hljs-k=
eyword">WHERE</span> author_id <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">IN</span> (<spa=
n class=3D"hljs-number">1</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">2</span>, <sp=
an class=3D"hljs-number">3</span>, ..., <span class=3D"hljs-number">100</sp=
an>);</code></pre></div>
<p><strong>Total: 2 queries</strong> instead of 101. Your application code =
then groups the books by <code>author_id</code> in memory.</p>
<h3 id=3D"solution-3-orm-eager-loading" data-toc=3D"Solution 3: ORM Eager L=
oading">Solution 3: ORM Eager Loading</h3>
<p>Most ORMs have built-in solutions that generate the optimized queries fo=
r you:</p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Framework</th>
<th style=3D"text-align:center">Lazy Loading (=E2=9D=8C N+1)</th>
<th style=3D"text-align:center">Eager Loading (=E2=9C=85 Fixed)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Hibernate (Java)</strong></td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center"><code>author.getBooks()</code> in a loop</t=
d>
<td style=3D"text-align:center"><code>JOIN FETCH</code> or <code>@EntityGra=
ph</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Django (Python)</strong></td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center"><code>author.books.all()</code> in a loop</=
td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center"><code>.select_related()</code> / <code>.pre=
fetch_related()</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Rails (Ruby)</strong></td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center"><code>author.books</code> in a loop</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center"><code>.includes(:books)</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Entity Framework (.NET)</strong></td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">Navigation property access</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center"><code>.Include(a =3D&gt; a.Books)</code></t=
d>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Sequelize (Node.js)</strong></td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center"><code>author.getBooks()</code> in a loop</t=
d>
<td style=3D"text-align:center"><code>{ include: [Book] }</code></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
