<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th>Logical Schema</th>
<th>Physical Schema</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Concerned with</strong></td>
<td>Data structure =E2=80=94 tables, columns, types, constraints</td>
<td>Storage =E2=80=94 how data is stored on disk</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Visible to</strong></td>
<td>Users, application developers</td>
<td>Database administrators, DBMS internals</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Can be changed without affecting apps?</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F Often breaks applications, unless views absorb the c=
hange (<a href=3D"#data-independence--logical-vs-physical">logical data ind=
ependence</a>)</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 Can be modified independently (<a href=3D"#data-independence-=
-logical-vs-physical">physical data independence</a>)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<ul>
<li>The DBMS provides <strong>DDL (Data Definition Language)</strong> to de=
fine the logical schema</li>
<li>The <strong>physical schema is hidden</strong> behind the logical schem=
a =E2=80=94 it can be changed (e.g., adding an index, changing storage engi=
ne) without affecting application programs</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"sub-schema-external-schema--view" data-toc=3D"Sub-Schema (Externa=
l Schema / View)">Sub-Schema (External Schema / View)</h3>
<p>A <strong>sub-schema</strong> is a <strong>subset of the schema</strong>=
 =E2=80=94 it defines what portion of the database a specific user or appli=
cation can see.</p>
