Pgo=3D" alt=3D"Three-schema architecture: several external views on top, ma=
pped to one conceptual (logical) schema, mapped to one internal (physical) =
schema stored on disk; physical data independence sits between conceptual a=
nd internal, logical data independence between external and conceptual" tit=
le=3D"Three-schema architecture: several external views on top, mapped to o=
ne conceptual (logical) schema, mapped to one internal (physical) schema st=
ored on disk; physical data independence sits between conceptual and intern=
al, logical data independence between external and conceptual" loading=3D"l=
azy"></figure>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Level</th>
<th>Also called</th>
<th>Describes</th>
<th>How many</th>
<th>In MySQL terms</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>External</strong> (top)</td>
<td>View level, <a href=3D"#sub-schema-external-schema--view">sub-schema</a=
></td>
<td>What <strong>one user group / app</strong> is allowed to see</td>
<td><strong>Many</strong>, one per user group</td>
<td><code>VIEW</code>s, column-level <code>GRANT</code>s</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Conceptual</strong> (middle)</td>
<td>Logical level, <a href=3D"#types-of-schema">logical schema</a></td>
<td><strong>All</strong> entities, attributes, relationships and constraint=
s of the whole database</td>
<td><strong>One</strong></td>
<td><code>CREATE TABLE</code> definitions, keys, FKs, <code>CHECK</code>s</=
td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Internal</strong> (bottom)</td>
<td>Physical level, <a href=3D"#types-of-schema">physical schema</a></td>
<td><strong>How</strong> it's stored: files, pages, record format, indexes,=
 partitions</td>
<td><strong>One</strong></td>
<td>InnoDB clustered B+ tree, <code>.ibd</code> files, 16 KB pages, seconda=
ry indexes, row format, compression</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Between the levels sit two <strong>mappings</strong>, maintained by the =
DBMS:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>External =E2=86=94 Conceptual mapping:</strong> how each view's=
 columns are computed from the real tables (e.g. a view's <code>SELECT =E2=
=80=A6 JOIN =E2=80=A6</code>).</li>
<li><strong>Conceptual =E2=86=94 Internal mapping:</strong> how each table =
and column is laid out in storage (which file, which index, which page form=
at).</li>
</ul>
<p>Users and applications only ever talk to the <strong>external</strong> (=
or conceptual) level. The mappings are what let the lower levels change und=
erneath them.</p>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"data-independence--logical-vs-physical" data-toc=3D"Data Independ=
ence =E2=80=94 Logical vs Physical">Data Independence =E2=80=94 Logical vs =
Physical</h3>
<p><strong>Data independence</strong> =3D you can change the schema at one =
level <strong>without having to change the level above it</strong> (and so =
without rewriting applications). It comes in two kinds:</p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th><strong>Physical data independence</strong></th>
<th><strong>Logical data independence</strong></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Change made at</strong></td>
<td>Internal (physical) level</td>
<td>Conceptual (logical) level</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Must NOT affect</strong></td>
<td>The conceptual schema, i.e. table definitions and queries</td>
<td>External views, i.e. applications</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Absorbed by</strong></td>
<td>Conceptual =E2=86=94 Internal mapping</td>
<td>External =E2=86=94 Conceptual mapping (views)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Examples</strong></td>
<td>Add or drop an index =C2=B7 change row format or compression =C2=B7 par=
tition a table =C2=B7 move data to SSD or another tablespace =C2=B7 rebuild=
 the table</td>
<td>Add a column =C2=B7 split one table into two =C2=B7 merge tables =C2=B7=
 rename a column behind a view</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>What apps notice</strong></td>
<td>Nothing, except speed</td>
<td>Nothing, <strong>if</strong> a view preserves the old shape</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>How easy?</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 Easy, and fully supported. This is what DBMSs are built for</=
td>
<td>=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F Harder: apps depend directly on table and column nam=
es</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>Physical independence in practice:</strong> <code>CREATE INDEX i=
dx_email ON users(email);</code> changes only the internal level. Every exi=
sting query keeps working unchanged. It just gets faster.</p>
<p><strong>Logical independence in practice:</strong> split <code>employees=
</code> for security, and keep the old shape alive with a view so applicati=
ons don't change:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Before: employees(emp_id, name, dept,=
 salary, ssn)</span>
RENAME <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TABLE</span> employees <span class=3D"h=
ljs-keyword">TO</span> employees_core;
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TAB=
LE</span> employees_private (<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">PRIMARY</span> KE=
Y (emp_id))
    <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">AS</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SEL=
ECT</span> emp_id, salary, ssn <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> emp=
loyees_core;
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ALTER</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TABL=
E</span> employees_core <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">DROP</span> <span clas=
s=3D"hljs-keyword">COLUMN</span> salary, <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">DROP<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">COLUMN</span> ssn;

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- The external mapping: a view with the OLD n=
ame and OLD columns</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">VIE=
W</span> employees <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">AS</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> c.emp_id, c.name, c.dept, p.sala=
ry, p.ssn
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span>   employees_core c
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">JOIN</span>   employees_private p <span class=
=3D"hljs-keyword">USING</span> (emp_id);</code></pre></div>
<p>Every <code>SELECT =E2=80=A6 FROM employees</code> keeps working. (Write=
s through a join view are limited; see <a href=3D"#4-rules-for-updatable-vi=
ews">rules for updatable views</a>.)</p>
<div class=3D"markdown-alert markdown-alert-warning"><p class=3D"markdown-a=
lert-title"><svg class=3D"octicon octicon-alert mr-2" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 16"=
 version=3D"1.1" width=3D"16" height=3D"16" aria-hidden=3D"true"><path d=3D=
"M6.457 1.047c.659-1.234 2.427-1.234 3.086 0l6.082 11.378A1.75 1.75 0 0 1 1=
4.082 15H1.918a1.75 1.75 0 0 1-1.543-2.575Zm1.763.707a.25.25 0 0 0-.44 0L1.=
698 13.132a.25.25 0 0 0 .22.368h12.164a.25.25 0 0 0 .22-.368Zm.53 3.996v2.5=
a.75.75 0 0 1-1.5 0v-2.5a.75.75 0 0 1 1.5 0ZM9 11a1 1 0 1 1-2 0 1 1 0 0 1 2=
 0Z"></path></svg>Warning</p><p><strong>Logical independence has limits.</s=
trong> <code>SELECT *</code> silently changes shape when you add a column, =
and <strong>dropping</strong> a column that an app reads can't be hidden by=
 any view. That's why logical independence is &quot;partial&quot; in practi=
ce, while physical independence is close to complete.</p>
</div>
<div class=3D"markdown-alert markdown-alert-tip"><p class=3D"markdown-alert=
-title"><svg class=3D"octicon octicon-light-bulb mr-2" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 16=
" version=3D"1.1" width=3D"16" height=3D"16" aria-hidden=3D"true"><path d=
=3D"M8 1.5c-2.363 0-4 1.69-4 3.75 0 .984.424 1.625.984 2.304l.214.253c.223.=
264.47.556.673.848.284.411.537.896.621 1.49a.75.75 0 0 1-1.484.211c-.04-.28=
2-.163-.547-.37-.847a8.456 8.456 0 0 0-.542-.68c-.084-.1-.173-.205-.268-.32=
C3.201 7.75 2.5 6.766 2.5 5.25 2.5 2.31 4.863 0 8 0s5.5 2.31 5.5 5.25c0 1.5=
16-.701 2.5-1.328 3.259-.095.115-.184.22-.268.319-.207.245-.383.453-.541.68=
1-.208.3-.33.565-.37.847a.751.751 0 0 1-1.485-.212c.084-.593.337-1.078.621-=
1.489.203-.292.45-.584.673-.848.075-.088.147-.173.213-.253.561-.679.985-1.3=
2.985-2.304 0-2.06-1.637-3.75-4-3.75ZM5.75 12h4.5a.75.75 0 0 1 0 1.5h-4.5a.=
75.75 0 0 1 0-1.5ZM6 15.25a.75.75 0 0 1 .75-.75h2.5a.75.75 0 0 1 0 1.5h-2.5=
a.75.75 0 0 1-.75-.75Z"></path></svg>Tip</p><p><strong>Interview answer:</s=
trong> &quot;The three-schema architecture separates external views, the co=
nceptual schema and the internal storage, with a mapping between each pair.=
 That gives <strong>physical data independence</strong>: I can add an index=
 or repartition a table without touching queries. It also gives <strong>log=
ical data independence</strong>: I can restructure tables and keep old view=
s so apps don't change. Physical independence is easy and almost complete; =
logical is harder because apps depend on table and column names.&quot;</p>
</div>
<h4 id=3D"quick-summary">Quick Summary</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Concept</th>
<th>What It Is</th>
<th>Analogy</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Instance</strong></td>
<td>Snapshot of data at a moment in time</td>
<td>A photo of the building right now</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Schema</strong></td>
<td>Structure/blueprint of the database</td>
<td>The architectural plan of the building</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Sub-schema</strong></td>
<td>A user's partial view of the schema</td>
<td>A window showing only some rooms</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Three-schema architecture</strong></td>
<td>External views =E2=86=92 conceptual schema =E2=86=92 internal storage, =
linked by mappings</td>
<td>Windows =E2=86=92 floor plan =E2=86=92 bricks and plumbing</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Data independence</strong></td>
<td>Changing one level without breaking the level above</td>
<td>Rewiring the walls without changing the floor plan (physical), or chang=
ing the floor plan while keeping the windows (logical)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<hr>
<hr>
</section><section class=3D"sec"><h2 id=3D"referential-integrity--foreign-k=
ey-actions-on-delete--on-update" data-toc=3D"9. Referential Integrity &amp;=
 Foreign Key Actions (ON =E2=80=A6"><span class=3D"sec-n">9.</span> Referen=
tial Integrity &amp; Foreign Key Actions (ON DELETE / ON UPDATE)</h2>
<h3 id=3D"what-is-referential-integrity" data-toc=3D"What Is Referential In=
tegrity?">What Is Referential Integrity?</h3>
<p>=F0=9F=94=97 <a href=3D"https://www.tutorialspoint.com/Referential-Integ=
rity-Rule-in-RDBMS" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener">Tutorialspoint =E2=
=80=94 Referential Integrity Rule in RDBMS</a></p>
<p>When you normalize a database, you split data into multiple related tabl=
es connected by <strong>foreign keys</strong>. Referential Integrity is a r=
ule that ensures <strong>relationships between tables remain consistent</st=
rong>. Specifically:</p>
<div class=3D"markdown-alert markdown-alert-note"><p class=3D"markdown-aler=
t-title"><svg class=3D"octicon octicon-info mr-2" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 16" ver=
sion=3D"1.1" width=3D"16" height=3D"16" aria-hidden=3D"true"><path d=3D"M0 =
8a8 8 0 1 1 16 0A8 8 0 0 1 0 8Zm8-6.5a6.5 6.5 0 1 0 0 13 6.5 6.5 0 0 0 0-13=
ZM6.5 7.75A.75.75 0 0 1 7.25 7h1a.75.75 0 0 1 .75.75v2.75h.25a.75.75 0 0 1 =
0 1.5h-2a.75.75 0 0 1 0-1.5h.25v-2h-.25a.75.75 0 0 1-.75-.75ZM8 6a1 1 0 1 1=
 0-2 1 1 0 0 1 0 2Z"></path></svg>Note</p><p><strong>A foreign key value in=
 one table must either match an existing primary key value in the reference=
d table, or be NULL.</strong></p>
</div>
<p>In simple words =E2=80=94 you can't reference something that doesn't exi=
st.</p>
<h4 id=3D"the-two-tables-involved">The Two Tables Involved</h4>
<p>Every referential integrity constraint involves two tables:</p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Term</th>
<th>Role</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Parent table</strong> (Referenced table)</td>
<td>Contains the <strong>primary key</strong> being referenced</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Child table</strong> (Referencing table)</td>
<td>Contains the <strong>foreign key</strong> that points to the parent</td=
>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"example--departments--employees" data-toc=3D"Example =E2=80=94 De=
partments &amp; Employees">Example =E2=80=94 Departments &amp; Employees</h=
3>
<p>This one example is used throughout the section. <code>employees.dept_id=
</code> =E2=86=92 <code>departments.dept_id</code>. Departments is the <str=
ong>parent</strong>, employees is the <strong>child</strong>.</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keywor=
d">TABLE</span> departments (
    dept_id   <span class=3D"hljs-type">INT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyw=
ord">PRIMARY</span> KEY,
    dept_name <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class=3D"hljs-=
number">100</span>)
);

<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TAB=
LE</span> employees (
    emp_id    <span class=3D"hljs-type">INT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyw=
ord">PRIMARY</span> KEY,
    emp_name  <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class=3D"hljs-=
number">100</span>),
    dept_id   <span class=3D"hljs-type">INT</span>,
    <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FOREIGN</span> KEY (dept_id) <span class=
=3D"hljs-keyword">REFERENCES</span> departments(dept_id)
        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ON</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword"=
>DELETE</span> CASCADE
        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ON</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword"=
>UPDATE</span> CASCADE
);</code></pre></div>
<h4 id=3D"parent-table-departments">Parent Table: <code>departments</code><=
/h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th style=3D"text-align:center">dept_id (PK)</th>
<th style=3D"text-align:center">dept_name</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">10</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">Engineering</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">20</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">Marketing</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">30</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">HR</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h4 id=3D"child-table-employees">Child Table: <code>employees</code></h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th style=3D"text-align:center">emp_id (PK)</th>
<th style=3D"text-align:center">emp_name</th>
<th style=3D"text-align:center">dept_id (FK)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">101</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">Alice</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">10 =E2=9C=85</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">102</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">Bob</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">20 =E2=9C=85</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">103</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">Carol</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">30 =E2=9C=85</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">104</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">Dave</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">NULL =E2=9C=85</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">105</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">Eve</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center"><strong>50</strong> =E2=9D=8C</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Let's walk through each row:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Alice =E2=86=92 dept_id 10</strong> =E2=86=92 =E2=9C=85 Valid. =
<code>10</code> exists in <code>departments</code></li>
<li><strong>Bob =E2=86=92 dept_id 20</strong> =E2=86=92 =E2=9C=85 Valid. <c=
ode>20</code> exists in <code>departments</code></li>
<li><strong>Carol =E2=86=92 dept_id 30</strong> =E2=86=92 =E2=9C=85 Valid. =
<code>30</code> exists in <code>departments</code></li>
<li><strong>Dave =E2=86=92 dept_id NULL</strong> =E2=86=92 =E2=9C=85 Valid.=
 NULL is allowed (Dave hasn't been assigned to a department yet)</li>
<li><strong>Eve =E2=86=92 dept_id 50</strong> =E2=86=92 =E2=9D=8C <strong>V=
IOLATION!</strong> <code>50</code> does not exist in <code>departments</cod=
e>. The database will <strong>reject</strong> this insert.</li>
</ul>
