<p><strong>Real example:</strong> Each person has at most one passport; eac=
h passport belongs to at most one person.</p>
<p><strong>How to implement:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Put a foreign key on either side with a <strong>UNIQUE constraint</stro=
ng></li>
<li>Or share the same primary key across both tables</li>
</ul>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keywor=
d">TABLE</span> passport (
    passport_id   <span class=3D"hljs-type">INT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-=
keyword">PRIMARY</span> KEY,
    passport_no   <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class=3D"h=
ljs-number">20</span>) <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">NOT</span> <span class=
=3D"hljs-keyword">NULL</span>,
    person_id     <span class=3D"hljs-type">INT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-=
keyword">UNIQUE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">NOT</span> <span class=
=3D"hljs-keyword">NULL</span>,        <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- FK + =
UNIQUE =3D 1:1</span>
    <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FOREIGN</span> KEY (person_id) <span class=
=3D"hljs-keyword">REFERENCES</span> person(person_id)
);</code></pre></div>
