<p><strong>Real example:</strong> One department has many employees; each e=
mployee belongs to one department.</p>
<p><strong>How to implement:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Put a <strong>foreign key on the &quot;many&quot; side</strong> (Employ=
ee) referencing the &quot;one&quot; side (Department)</li>
</ul>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keywor=
d">TABLE</span> employee (
    emp_id     <span class=3D"hljs-type">INT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-key=
word">PRIMARY</span> KEY,
    emp_name   <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class=3D"hljs=
-number">50</span>),
    dept_id    <span class=3D"hljs-type">INT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-key=
word">NOT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">NULL</span>,                 =
 <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- FK on the N-side</span>
    <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FOREIGN</span> KEY (dept_id) <span class=
=3D"hljs-keyword">REFERENCES</span> department(dept_id)
);</code></pre></div>
