<hr>
<h3 id=3D"3-primary-key" data-toc=3D"3. Primary Key">3. Primary Key</h3>
<p>The <strong>primary key</strong> is the <strong>one candidate key chosen=
</strong> by the designer to be the main identifier for the table.</p>
<h4 id=3D"rules-for-primary-key">Rules for Primary Key:</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Rule</th>
<th>Explanation</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Unique</strong></td>
<td>No two rows can have the same PK value</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>NOT NULL</strong></td>
<td>PK can never be empty/null</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>One per table</strong></td>
<td>Only one primary key allowed per table</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Immutable</strong> (best practice)</td>
<td>Should rarely change once set</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h4 id=3D"choosing-the-primary-key-for-students">Choosing the Primary Key f=
or <code>students</code>:</h4>
<p>We have three candidate keys: <code>{roll_no}</code>, <code>{email}</cod=
e>, <code>{phone}</code>. Let's compare:</p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Candidate</th>
<th style=3D"text-align:center">Stable?</th>
<th style=3D"text-align:center">Short?</th>
<th style=3D"text-align:center">Meaningful?</th>
<th style=3D"text-align:center">Choice</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>roll_no</code></td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9C=85 Never changes</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9C=85 Integer</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9C=85 Clear identifier</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9C=85 <strong>Best choice</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>email</code></td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9D=8C Students may change email</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9D=8C Long string</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9C=85 Readable</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9D=8C</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>phone</code></td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9D=8C Students may change phone</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9D=8C Long</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9D=8C Not meaningful for students</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9D=8C</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keywor=
d">TABLE</span> students (
    roll_no   <span class=3D"hljs-type">INT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyw=
ord">PRIMARY</span> KEY,     <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- =E2=86=90 Chos=
en as PK</span>
    name      <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class=3D"hljs-=
number">50</span>),
    email     <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class=3D"hljs-=
number">100</span>) <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">UNIQUE</span>,  <span clas=
s=3D"hljs-comment">-- =E2=86=90 Alternate key (enforced)</span>
    phone     <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class=3D"hljs-=
number">15</span>) <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">UNIQUE</span>,   <span clas=
s=3D"hljs-comment">-- =E2=86=90 Alternate key (enforced)</span>
    dept      <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class=3D"hljs-=
number">10</span>)
);</code></pre></div>
<h4 id=3D"composite-primary-key">Composite Primary Key</h4>
<p>Sometimes a single column isn't enough. A <strong>composite PK</strong> =
uses multiple columns together:</p>
<p><strong><code>enrollment</code> table:</strong></p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th style=3D"text-align:center">student_id</th>
<th style=3D"text-align:center">course_id</th>
<th style=3D"text-align:center">grade</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">1</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">101</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">A</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">1</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">102</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">B+</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">2</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">101</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">A-</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Neither <code>student_id</code> alone nor <code>course_id</code> alone i=
s unique. But <code>{student_id, course_id}</code> together is unique =E2=
=86=92 composite PK.</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">PRIMARY</span> KEY (student_id, course_i=
d)</code></pre></div>
<h4 id=3D"why-uuid-is-not-preferred-as-primary-key">Why UUID is Not Preferr=
ed as Primary Key</h4>
<p>A random UUID is wide and non-sequential, which hurts a clustered index.=
 The full breakdown is in <a href=3D"#uuid-vs-auto-increment-integer-as-pri=
mary-key">UUID vs Auto-Increment Integer as Primary Key</a>.</p>
<hr>
<h4 id=3D"-is-declaring-a-primary-key-mandatory-in-mysql">=E2=9D=93 Is Decl=
aring a PRIMARY KEY Mandatory in MySQL?</h4>
<p><strong>Technically no. Practically, always yes.</strong></p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keywor=
d">TABLE</span> logs (message <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<spa=
n class=3D"hljs-number">255</span>));   <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- per=
fectly legal, MySQL accepts it</span></code></pre></div>
<p>But InnoDB builds a clustered index <strong>anyway</strong> =E2=80=94 it=
 falls back to a hidden <code>GEN_CLUST_INDEX</code> (see <a href=3D"#-does=
-declaring-a-key-in-mysql-automatically-create-an-index">the fallback chain=
</a>). So skipping the PK doesn't avoid the cost. It just means you don't g=
et to choose it, and you can never reference what it built.</p>
<h5 id=3D"what-actually-goes-wrong">What Actually Goes Wrong</h5>
<p><strong>1. Replication falls over</strong> =E2=80=94 this is the big one=
</p>
