<hr>
<h3 id=3D"-does-declaring-a-key-in-mysql-automatically-create-an-index" dat=
a-toc=3D"Does Declaring a KEY in MySQL Automatically Create =E2=80=A6">=E2=
=9D=93 Does Declaring a KEY in MySQL Automatically Create an Index?</h3>
<p><strong>Yes =E2=80=94 for <code>PRIMARY KEY</code>, <code>UNIQUE</code>,=
 and <code>FOREIGN KEY</code>. Not for anything else.</strong></p>
<h4 id=3D"key-vs-index--not-the-same-thing">Key vs Index =E2=80=94 Not the =
Same Thing</h4>
<p>Conceptually they're different. MySQL just <strong>enforces</strong> mos=
t keys by building an index, which is why they get confused.</p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th><strong>Key</strong></th>
<th><strong>Index</strong></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>What it is</strong></td>
<td>A <strong>logical rule</strong>: which column(s) identify a row or link=
 tables</td>
<td>A <strong>physical data structure</strong> (a B+ tree) that makes looku=
ps fast</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Purpose</strong></td>
<td>Correctness: uniqueness, identity, relationships</td>
<td>Speed: avoid scanning the whole table</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Level</strong></td>
<td>Logical / <a href=3D"#three-schema-architecture-ansisparc">conceptual s=
chema</a></td>
<td>Physical / internal schema</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Examples</strong></td>
<td>Primary, unique, foreign, candidate, super key</td>
<td><code>CREATE INDEX idx_email ON users(email)</code></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>First, a naming trap: in MySQL DDL, <strong><code>KEY</code> is literall=
y a synonym for <code>INDEX</code></strong>. These two lines are the same s=
tatement:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs">KEY   idx_name (name)
INDEX idx_name (name)   <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- identical</span></c=
ode></pre></div>
<p>So &quot;key&quot; inside <code>CREATE TABLE</code> <em>is</em> an index=
 declaration =E2=80=94 there's nothing to build separately.</p>
<h4 id=3D"what-creates-an-index-vs-what-doesnt">What Creates an Index vs Wh=
at Doesn't</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Declaration</th>
<th style=3D"text-align:center">Index?</th>
<th>What you actually get</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>PRIMARY KEY</code></td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9C=85</td>
<td>The <strong>clustered index</strong> in InnoDB =E2=80=94 not a separate=
 structure. The table <em>is</em> the index, physically stored in PK order<=
/td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>UNIQUE</code> / <code>UNIQUE KEY</code></td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9C=85</td>
<td>A secondary index. MySQL has <strong>no</strong> unique constraint with=
out an index =E2=80=94 the index is <em>how</em> uniqueness is enforced</td=
>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>FOREIGN KEY</code></td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9C=85</td>
<td>InnoDB <strong>auto-creates</strong> an index on the child column if no=
ne exists. The parent column must already have one (usually its PK), or MyS=
QL rejects the constraint</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>KEY</code> / <code>INDEX</code></td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9C=85</td>
<td>The explicit form</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Candidate / super / alternate key</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9D=8C</td>
<td>Design concepts only. A column gets an index only if you actually decla=
re it <code>PRIMARY KEY</code> or <code>UNIQUE</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>CHECK</code></td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9D=8C</td>
<td>Constraint only, no structure</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>NOT NULL</code>, <code>DEFAULT</code></td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9D=8C</td>
<td>Column attributes, no structure</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>A plain column</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9D=8C</td>
<td>Nothing</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h4 id=3D"see-it-yourself">See It Yourself</h4>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keywor=
d">TABLE</span> students (
    roll_no  <span class=3D"hljs-type">INT</span>          <span class=3D"h=
ljs-keyword">PRIMARY</span> KEY,          <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- =
=E2=86=92 clustered index</span>
    email    <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class=3D"hljs-n=
umber">100</span>) <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">UNIQUE</span>,             =
  <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- =E2=86=92 unique secondary index</span>
    phone    <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class=3D"hljs-n=
umber">15</span>)  <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">UNIQUE</span>,             =
  <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- =E2=86=92 unique secondary index</span>
    name     <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class=3D"hljs-n=
umber">50</span>)  <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">NOT</span> <span class=3D"h=
ljs-keyword">NULL</span>,             <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- =E2=
=86=92 nothing</span>
    age      <span class=3D"hljs-type">INT</span>          <span class=3D"h=
ljs-keyword">CHECK</span> (age <span class=3D"hljs-operator">&gt;=3D</span>=
 <span class=3D"hljs-number">18</span>),    <span class=3D"hljs-comment">--=
 =E2=86=92 nothing</span>
    dept_id  <span class=3D"hljs-type">INT</span>,
    <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FOREIGN</span> KEY (dept_id) <span class=
=3D"hljs-keyword">REFERENCES</span> departments(dept_id)  <span class=3D"hl=
js-comment">-- =E2=86=92 auto-index on dept_id</span>
);</code></pre></div>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">text</span></div><pre><code class=3D=
"hljs">SHOW INDEX FROM students;

+----------+------------+----------+-------------+
| Table    | Non_unique | Key_name | Column_name |
+----------+------------+----------+-------------+
| students |          0 | PRIMARY  | roll_no     |  =E2=86=90 clustered ind=
ex
| students |          0 | email    | email       |  =E2=86=90 unique second=
ary
| students |          0 | phone    | phone       |  =E2=86=90 unique second=
ary
| students |          1 | dept_id  | dept_id     |  =E2=86=90 auto-created =
by the FK
+----------+------------+----------+-------------+

4 indexes =E2=80=94 and we never typed the word INDEX once.
(Non_unique =3D 0 means the index enforces uniqueness.)</code></pre></div>
<p>Note what's <strong>missing</strong>: <code>name</code>. The summary tab=
le above calls it a <em>secondary key</em>, but that's relational-theory vo=
cabulary =E2=80=94 MySQL creates nothing for it. If you want <code>WHERE na=
me =3D ?</code> to be fast, you must declare <code>INDEX(name)</code> yours=
elf.</p>
