a.75.75 0 0 1-.75-.75Z"></path></svg>Tip</p><p><strong>Interview tip:</stro=
ng> &quot;Is a key the same as an index?&quot; =E2=80=94 <strong>No.</stron=
g> A <em>key</em> is a logical constraint (this identifies a row / this mus=
t be unique / this references another table). An <em>index</em> is a physic=
al B+ Tree structure. MySQL <strong>enforces</strong> <code>PRIMARY</code>,=
 <code>UNIQUE</code> and <code>FOREIGN</code> keys by building indexes, whi=
ch is why they get confused, and in MySQL syntax the word <code>KEY</code> =
is literally a synonym for <code>INDEX</code>. MySQL happens to implement m=
ost key constraints <em>using</em> an index, which is why they're conflated=
 =E2=80=94 but you can have an index with no constraint (<code>INDEX(name)<=
/code>), and in relational theory a key with no index (<code>name</code> as=
 a secondary key).</p>
