<h3 id=3D"insert-performance--the-b-tree-problem" data-toc=3D"Insert Perfor=
mance =E2=80=94 The B+ Tree Problem">Insert Performance =E2=80=94 The B+ Tr=
ee Problem</h3>
<p>This is the <strong>biggest</strong> performance difference between the =
two, and it comes down to how InnoDB's clustered index (B+ Tree) handles in=
serts. (For the general page-split mechanics, meaning what a split writes a=
nd InnoDB's right-hand split for ascending keys, see <a href=3D"#page-split=
s--the-hidden-cost-of-a-random-primary-key">Page Splits =E2=80=94 The Hidde=
n Cost of a Random Primary Key</a>. This section focuses on how the PK choi=
ce triggers them.)</p>
<h4 id=3D"auto-increment-sequential-inserts-fast">Auto-Increment: Sequentia=
l Inserts (Fast)</h4>
<p>Auto-increment IDs are <strong>monotonically increasing</strong>: 1, 2, =
3, 4, 5, ... Each new row has a higher key than the last. In the B+ Tree, t=
his means every insert goes to the <strong>rightmost leaf page</strong> =E2=
=80=94 the same page, over and over, until it fills up and splits.</p>
