<h3 id=3D"quick-decision-guide-2" data-toc=3D"Quick Decision Guide">Quick D=
ecision Guide</h3>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Scenario</th>
<th>Recommended Primary Key</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Single database, internal system</td>
<td><code>BIGINT AUTO_INCREMENT</code> =E2=80=94 simplest and fastest</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Single database, public-facing API</td>
<td><code>BIGINT</code> PK + <code>UUID</code> public column (hybrid)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Distributed database / multi-master</td>
<td><code>UUID v7</code> as <code>BINARY(16)</code> =E2=80=94 sequential + =
no coordination</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Microservices generating IDs independently</td>
<td><code>UUID v7</code> or <code>ULID</code> as <code>BINARY(16)</code></t=
d>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Range-sharded distributed DB (CockroachDB, Spanner)</td>
<td>Random <code>UUID v4</code> (or a hash-sharded index) =E2=80=94 time-or=
dered keys like <code>UUID v7</code> all land in the newest range and creat=
e a write hot-spot</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Hash-sharded DB (e.g. Vitess with a hash vindex)</td>
<td><code>UUID v7</code> or <code>BIGINT</code> =E2=80=94 the shard is pick=
ed by hash, so key order doesn't cause hot-spots, and v7 keeps each shard's=
 B+ Tree inserts sequential</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>High-volume analytics / time-series</td>
<td><code>BIGINT AUTO_INCREMENT</code> =E2=80=94 maximize insert throughput=
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Merging data from multiple sources</td>
<td><code>UUID</code> =E2=80=94 guaranteed unique across all sources</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Legacy system, can't change PK</td>
<td>Keep <code>BIGINT</code> PK, add a <code>UUID</code> column for new int=
egrations</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<div class=3D"markdown-alert markdown-alert-important"><p class=3D"markdown=
-alert-title"><svg class=3D"octicon octicon-report mr-2" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 =
16" version=3D"1.1" width=3D"16" height=3D"16" aria-hidden=3D"true"><path d=
=3D"M0 1.75C0 .784.784 0 1.75 0h12.5C15.216 0 16 .784 16 1.75v9.5A1.75 1.75=
 0 0 1 14.25 13H8.06l-2.573 2.573A1.458 1.458 0 0 1 3 14.543V13H1.75A1.75 1=
.75 0 0 1 0 11.25Zm1.75-.25a.25.25 0 0 0-.25.25v9.5c0 .138.112.25.25.25h2a.=
75.75 0 0 1 .75.75v2.19l2.72-2.72a.749.749 0 0 1 .53-.22h6.5a.25.25 0 0 0 .=
25-.25v-9.5a.25.25 0 0 0-.25-.25Zm7 2.25v2.5a.75.75 0 0 1-1.5 0v-2.5a.75.75=
 0 0 1 1.5 0ZM9 9a1 1 0 1 1-2 0 1 1 0 0 1 2 0Z"></path></svg>Important</p><=
p><strong>The golden rule:</strong> Use <code>BIGINT AUTO_INCREMENT</code> =
as your primary key for maximum InnoDB performance. If you need non-enumera=
ble external identifiers, add a separate <code>UUID</code> column (preferab=
ly v7, stored as <code>BINARY(16)</code>). If you're in a distributed syste=
m where centralized ID generation is impossible, use <code>UUID v7</code> a=
s your primary key =E2=80=94 it gives you global uniqueness with near-seque=
ntial insert performance.</p>
</div>
<hr>
<hr>
</section><section class=3D"sec"><h2 id=3D"sql-injection" data-toc=3D"18. S=
QL Injection"><span class=3D"sec-n">18.</span> SQL Injection</h2>
<p>SQL Injection is a <strong>code injection attack</strong> where a hacker=
 inserts malicious SQL code through user input fields (login forms, search =
boxes, URLs) to manipulate the database. It's one of the <strong>most commo=
n and dangerous</strong> web vulnerabilities.</p>
<div class=3D"markdown-alert markdown-alert-note"><p class=3D"markdown-aler=
t-title"><svg class=3D"octicon octicon-info mr-2" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 16" ver=
sion=3D"1.1" width=3D"16" height=3D"16" aria-hidden=3D"true"><path d=3D"M0 =
8a8 8 0 1 1 16 0A8 8 0 0 1 0 8Zm8-6.5a6.5 6.5 0 1 0 0 13 6.5 6.5 0 0 0 0-13=
ZM6.5 7.75A.75.75 0 0 1 7.25 7h1a.75.75 0 0 1 .75.75v2.75h.25a.75.75 0 0 1 =
0 1.5h-2a.75.75 0 0 1 0-1.5h.25v-2h-.25a.75.75 0 0 1-.75-.75ZM8 6a1 1 0 1 1=
 0-2 1 1 0 0 1 0 2Z"></path></svg>Note</p><p><strong>Think of it this way:<=
/strong> Imagine a bank teller who blindly follows any instruction written =
on a withdrawal slip. A normal customer writes &quot;Withdraw =E2=82=B91000=
 from Account #123.&quot; An attacker writes &quot;Withdraw =E2=82=B91000 f=
rom Account #123; <strong>also transfer all money from every account to min=
e</strong>.&quot; If the teller doesn't verify, the attack succeeds. SQL In=
jection works the same way.</p>
</div>
