<h3 id=3D"how-a-b-tree-works--the-engine-behind-mysql-indexes" data-toc=3D"=
How a B+ Tree Works =E2=80=94 The Engine Behind MySQL =E2=80=A6">How a B+ T=
ree Works =E2=80=94 The Engine Behind MySQL Indexes</h3>
<p>Almost every index in InnoDB is a <strong>B+ Tree</strong> (a balanced, =
sorted tree optimized for disk-based storage). Understanding this structure=
 is essential because it explains <em>why</em> indexes are fast for some qu=
eries and useless for others.</p>
<p>Both the clustered (primary key) index and every secondary index use thi=
s same structure.</p>
<p>=E2=96=B6=EF=B8=8F <a href=3D"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3DaZjYr87r=
1b8&amp;t=3D1657s" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener">B-Tree (Abdul Bari)</=
a><br>
=E2=96=B6=EF=B8=8F <a href=3D"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3DownO77M4SWI=
" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener">B Tree Insert and Delete</a><br>
=F0=9F=93=9A <a href=3D"https://planetscale.com/blog/btrees-and-database-in=
dexes" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener">B-Trees &amp; B+ Tree &amp; DB In=
dexes (PlanetScale)</a><br>
=F0=9F=95=B9=EF=B8=8F <a href=3D"https://bplustree.app/" target=3D"_blank" =
rel=3D"noopener">Interactive B+ Tree Visualizer</a><br>
=F0=9F=95=B9=EF=B8=8F <a href=3D"https://btree.app/" target=3D"_blank" rel=
=3D"noopener">Interactive B Tree Visualizer</a></p>
<p>=F0=9F=94=97 <a href=3D"https://medium.com/@akashsdas_dev/b-trees-and-b-=
trees-682d363df1f7" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener">B-Trees and B+ Trees=
 =E2=80=94 Explained</a></p>
<h4 id=3D"why-not-a-simple-binary-search-tree">Why Not a Simple Binary Sear=
ch Tree?</h4>
<p>A binary search tree (BST) stores one key per node and has two children.=
 For a table with 1 million rows, a balanced BST would be ~20 levels deep (=
<code>log=E2=82=82(1,000,000) =E2=89=88 20</code>). Each level means a sepa=
rate <strong>disk read</strong> (since nodes are scattered across disk), so=
 finding one row =3D 20 disk I/Os. Disk seeks take ~5-10ms each, so a singl=
e lookup could take 100-200ms =E2=80=94 unacceptable.</p>
<p>A B+ Tree solves this by being <strong>wide and short</strong>:</p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Property</th>
<th>Binary Search Tree</th>
<th>B+ Tree (InnoDB)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Keys per node</td>
<td>1</td>
<td>Hundreds (fills a 16 KB page)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Children per node</td>
<td>2</td>
<td>Hundreds</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Height for 1M rows</td>
<td>~20</td>
<td><strong>3-4</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Disk reads per lookup</td>
<td>~20</td>
<td><strong>3-4</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Data stored in</td>
<td>Every node</td>
<td><strong>Leaf nodes only</strong></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h4 id=3D"the-structure-internal-nodes-vs-leaf-nodes">The Structure: Intern=
al Nodes vs Leaf Nodes</h4>
<p>A B+ Tree has two types of nodes:</p>
<p><strong>1. Internal (non-leaf) nodes</strong> =E2=80=94 Act as signposts=
. They contain <strong>keys</strong> and <strong>pointers to child nodes</s=
trong>, but no actual row data. Their only job is to route you to the corre=
ct leaf.</p>
<p><strong>2. Leaf nodes</strong> =E2=80=94 Contain the actual <strong>inde=
xed key values</strong> plus either:</p>
<ul>
<li>The <strong>full row data</strong> (if this is the clustered/primary ke=
y index), or</li>
<li>A <strong>pointer to the row</strong> (if this is a secondary index =E2=
=80=94 the pointer is the primary key value)</li>
</ul>
<p>All leaf nodes are connected in a <strong>doubly linked list</strong> =
=E2=80=94 this is what makes range scans fast. Once you find the first matc=
hing leaf, you just walk sideways through the chain without going back up t=
he tree.</p>
<h4 id=3D"visual-example-b-tree-with-order-4">Visual Example: B+ Tree with =
Order 4</h4>
<p>&quot;Order 4&quot; means each node can hold at most 3 keys and 4 child =
pointers. (InnoDB nodes are 16 KB pages that can hold hundreds of keys; we =
use small numbers here for clarity.)</p>
<p>Suppose we insert these employee IDs in order: <code>10, 20, 30, 40, 50,=
 60, 70, 80, 90</code></p>
