<p><strong>Key observations about splits:</strong></p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Aspect</th>
<th>What happens</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>When</strong></td>
<td>A node overflows (exceeds max keys)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>What</strong></td>
<td>Node splits into two halves; middle key is promoted to parent</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Cost</strong></td>
<td>3 page writes (left child, right child, parent) =E2=80=94 this is the w=
rite overhead of B+ Trees</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Cascading</strong></td>
<td>If the parent also overflows, it splits too =E2=80=94 can cascade up to=
 the root</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Tree height increase</strong></td>
<td>Only increases when the <strong>root</strong> splits =E2=80=94 always s=
tays balanced</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Deletions</strong></td>
<td>Reverse of splits =E2=80=94 nodes merge when they become less than half=
 full</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h4 id=3D"real-innodb-numbers">Real InnoDB Numbers</h4>
<p>In production (with 16 KB pages and typical <code>BIGINT</code> keys):</=
p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>Approximate Value</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Keys per internal page</td>
<td>~1,200</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Rows per leaf page</td>
<td>~500 (depends on row size)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Height for 1 million rows</td>
<td><strong>3</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Height for 1 billion rows</td>
<td><strong>4</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Disk reads for a point lookup</td>
<td>2-4 (root is usually cached in memory)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Because the root and first-level internal pages are almost always in the=
 <strong>buffer pool</strong> (InnoDB's memory cache), a typical lookup in =
a well-configured database needs only <strong>1 disk read</strong> (the lea=
f page).</p>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"types-of-indexes" data-toc=3D"Types of Indexes">Types of Indexes<=
/h3>
<h4 id=3D"1-primary-key-index-clustered-index">1. Primary Key Index (Cluste=
red Index)</h4>
<p>In InnoDB, the <strong>primary key IS the table</strong>. The actual row=
 data is stored in the leaf nodes of the primary key's B+ Tree. This is cal=
led a <strong>clustered index</strong> =E2=80=94 the table data is physical=
ly ordered (clustered) by the primary key.</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keywor=
d">TABLE</span> employees (
    id <span class=3D"hljs-type">INT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">PR=
IMARY</span> KEY,      <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- =E2=86=90 This IS th=
e clustered index</span>
    name <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class=3D"hljs-numbe=
r">100</span>),
    department <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class=3D"hljs=
-number">50</span>),
    salary <span class=3D"hljs-type">DECIMAL</span>(<span class=3D"hljs-num=
ber">10</span>,<span class=3D"hljs-number">2</span>)
);</code></pre></div>
<p><strong>Key facts:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Every InnoDB table has <strong>exactly one</strong> clustered index</li=
>
<li>If you don't define a <code>PRIMARY KEY</code>, InnoDB uses the first <=
code>UNIQUE NOT NULL</code> index; if none exists, it creates a hidden 6-by=
te row ID as the clustered key</li>
<li>Because data is physically sorted by primary key, <strong>sequential pr=
imary key access is very fast</strong> (data is in adjacent pages)</li>
<li>Auto-increment IDs are ideal because new rows always append to the <str=
ong>end</strong> of the tree =E2=80=94 no splits in the middle, minimal pag=
e fragmentation</li>
</ul>
<p>=F0=9F=94=97 How the clustered index differs from secondary indexes (lea=
f contents, bookmark lookups, PK size) is covered in <a href=3D"#clustered-=
vs-secondary-indexes-innodb-specifics">Clustered vs Secondary Indexes (Inno=
DB Specifics)</a>.</p>
<h4 id=3D"2-unique-index">2. Unique Index</h4>
<p>Enforces that no two rows can have the same value in the indexed column(=
s). Internally, it's the same B+ Tree structure as any other index, but MyS=
QL checks for duplicates on every insert/update.</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keywor=
d">UNIQUE</span> INDEX idx_email <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ON</span> use=
rs (email);

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Or inline:</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TAB=
LE</span> users (
    id <span class=3D"hljs-type">INT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">PR=
IMARY</span> KEY,
    email <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class=3D"hljs-numb=
er">255</span>) <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">UNIQUE</span>,         <span c=
lass=3D"hljs-comment">-- =E2=86=90 creates a unique index automatically</sp=
an>
    username <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class=3D"hljs-n=
umber">50</span>) <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">UNIQUE</span>
);</code></pre></div>
<p><strong>When to use:</strong> When you have a business rule that a colum=
n must contain distinct values =E2=80=94 email, username, phone number, SSN=
. Beyond data integrity, unique indexes also help the optimizer: when MySQL=
 knows a column is unique, it can stop searching after finding one match (<=
code>const</code> or <code>eq_ref</code> access type in <code>EXPLAIN</code=
>).</p>
<p>Unlike a primary key, a unique index <strong>allows NULLs</strong> =E2=
=80=94 and multiple NULLs, since NULL =E2=89=A0 NULL. See <a href=3D"#prima=
ry-key-vs-unique-key">Primary Key vs Unique Key</a>.</p>
<h4 id=3D"3-composite-multi-column-index">3. Composite (Multi-Column) Index=
</h4>
<p>A <strong>composite index</strong> (also called a <strong>compound</stro=
ng> or <strong>multi-column</strong> index) is one B+ Tree index built from=
 <strong>two or more columns</strong> (MySQL: up to 16). The B+ Tree is sor=
ted by the first column, then by the second column within rows that share t=
he same first column, and so on =E2=80=94 like a phone book sorted by last =
name, then first name.</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> INDEX idx_dept_salary <spa=
n class=3D"hljs-keyword">ON</span> employees (department, salary);</code></=
pre></div>
<p><strong>How the B+ Tree is sorted:</strong></p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">text</span></div><pre><code class=3D=
"hljs">('Engineering', 70000)
('Engineering', 85000)
('Engineering', 95000)
('Marketing',   60000)
('Marketing',   72000)
('Sales',       55000)
('Sales',       68000)
('Sales',       90000)</code></pre></div>
<p>How to use it well =E2=80=94 the <strong>leftmost-prefix rule</strong>, =
choosing column order, and composite vs separate indexes =E2=80=94 is cover=
ed in <a href=3D"#leftmost-prefix-rule">Leftmost-Prefix Rule</a> right afte=
r the index types.</p>
<h4 id=3D"4-full-text-index">4. Full-Text Index</h4>
<p>Designed for <strong>natural language text search</strong> =E2=80=94 fin=
ding words or phrases inside <code>TEXT</code> or <code>VARCHAR</code> colu=
mns. Regular B+ Tree indexes are useless for <code>LIKE '%keyword%'</code> =
(leading wildcard prevents index usage). Full-text indexes use an <strong>i=
nverted index</strong> internally: a mapping from each word to the list of =
rows that contain it.</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> FULLTEXT INDEX idx_ft_cont=
ent <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ON</span> articles (title, body);

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Search for articles mentioning &quot;databa=
se indexing&quot;</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> articles
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">MATC=
H</span>(title, body) AGAINST(<span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;database in=
dexing&#x27;</span>);

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Boolean mode =E2=80=94 more control (+ mean=
s must include, - means must exclude)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> articles
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">MATC=
H</span>(title, body) AGAINST(<span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;+database +=
indexing -NoSQL&#x27;</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">IN</span> <span c=
lass=3D"hljs-type">BOOLEAN</span> MODE);</code></pre></div>
<p><strong>When to use:</strong> Blog search, product search, documentation=
 search =E2=80=94 anywhere you need to find rows containing specific words =
inside large text blocks. For more advanced search (fuzzy matching, synonym=
s, stemming, ranking), consider <strong>Elasticsearch</strong> or <strong>S=
olr</strong> instead of MySQL full-text.</p>
<h4 id=3D"5-spatial-index-r-tree">5. Spatial Index (R-Tree)</h4>
<p>Used for <strong>geometry</strong> and <strong>geographic</strong> data =
types (<code>POINT</code>, <code>POLYGON</code>, etc.). Spatial indexes use=
 <strong>R-Trees</strong> (not B+ Trees) which partition multi-dimensional =
space into nested bounding rectangles.</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> SPATIAL INDEX idx_location=
 <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ON</span> stores (coordinates);

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Find all stores within 5 km of a point</spa=
n>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> name <span class=3D"hljs-keyword=
">FROM</span> stores
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> ST_Distance_Sphere(coordinates, S=
T_GeomFromText(<span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;POINT(77.5946 12.9716)&#x2=
7;</span>)) <span class=3D"hljs-operator">&lt;</span> <span class=3D"hljs-n=
umber">5000</span>;</code></pre></div>
<p><strong>When to use:</strong> &quot;Find nearest&quot; queries, geofenci=
ng, delivery zone checks, map-based applications.</p>
<h4 id=3D"6-hash-index-memory-engine-only">6. Hash Index (MEMORY Engine Onl=
y)</h4>
<p>A hash index stores a <strong>hash of the key</strong> =E2=86=92 row poi=
nter mapping. Lookups are O(1) =E2=80=94 instant for exact-match queries. B=
ut it <strong>cannot</strong> do range scans, sorting, or partial matches b=
ecause hashing destroys order.</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Only available with the MEMORY storag=
e engine</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TAB=
LE</span> sessions (
    session_id <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class=3D"hljs=
-number">64</span>),
    user_id <span class=3D"hljs-type">INT</span>,
    data TEXT,
    INDEX <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">USING</span> HASH (session_id)
) ENGINE <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> MEMORY;</code></pre></div=
>
<p><strong>InnoDB does NOT support explicit hash indexes</strong>, but it h=
as an <strong>Adaptive Hash Index (AHI)</strong> =E2=80=94 InnoDB automatic=
ally detects &quot;hot&quot; B+ Tree pages that are accessed very frequentl=
y and builds an in-memory hash index on top of them. You don't create this =
manually; InnoDB manages it transparently.</p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Index Type</th>
<th>Structure</th>
<th>Equality</th>
<th>Range</th>
<th>Sorting</th>
<th>Partial Match</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>B+ Tree (default)</td>
<td>Sorted tree</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 (prefix only)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Hash</td>
<td>Hash table</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 (O(1))</td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C</td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C</td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Full-Text</td>
<td>Inverted index</td>
<td>Words only</td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C</td>
<td>By relevance</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 (words)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Spatial (R-Tree)</td>
<td>Bounding rectangles</td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C</td>
<td>Spatial only</td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C</td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<hr>
<h4 id=3D"7-prefix-index">7. Prefix Index</h4>
<p>Indexes only the <strong>first N characters</strong> of a column (bytes =
for binary types like <code>BLOB</code>). Much smaller; used for long <code=
>VARCHAR</code>/<code>TEXT</code>/<code>BLOB</code> columns.</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> INDEX idx_name_pfx <span c=
lass=3D"hljs-keyword">ON</span> users (name(<span class=3D"hljs-number">10<=
/span>));   <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- prefix: first 10 characters</sp=
an></code></pre></div>
<div class=3D"markdown-alert markdown-alert-note"><p class=3D"markdown-aler=
t-title"><svg class=3D"octicon octicon-info mr-2" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 16" ver=
sion=3D"1.1" width=3D"16" height=3D"16" aria-hidden=3D"true"><path d=3D"M0 =
8a8 8 0 1 1 16 0A8 8 0 0 1 0 8Zm8-6.5a6.5 6.5 0 1 0 0 13 6.5 6.5 0 0 0 0-13=
ZM6.5 7.75A.75.75 0 0 1 7.25 7h1a.75.75 0 0 1 .75.75v2.75h.25a.75.75 0 0 1 =
0 1.5h-2a.75.75 0 0 1 0-1.5h.25v-2h-.25a.75.75 0 0 1-.75-.75ZM8 6a1 1 0 1 1=
 0-2 1 1 0 0 1 0 2Z"></path></svg>Note</p><p>Some references call this a &q=
uot;partial&quot; index, but it is <strong>not</strong> the same as a Postg=
reSQL <strong>partial (filtered)</strong> index (<code>CREATE INDEX ... WHE=
RE condition</code>), which MySQL does not support.</p>
</div>
<p>A <strong>covering index</strong> isn't a separate index type =E2=80=94 =
it's any index (usually composite) that contains every column a query needs=
. See <a href=3D"#covering-indexes-and-index-only-scans">Covering Indexes a=
nd Index-Only Scans</a>.</p>
<h4 id=3D"creating-and-dropping-indexes">Creating and Dropping Indexes</h4>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Clustered index: in MySQL/InnoDB this=
 is always the PRIMARY KEY =E2=80=94 there is no separate syntax</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- (SQL Server syntax: CREATE CLUSTERED INDEX =
idx_emp_id ON employees(emp_id);)</span>

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Non-clustered (secondary) index</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> INDEX idx_emp_name <span class=
=3D"hljs-keyword">ON</span> employees(name);

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Non-clustered index on multiple columns (co=
mposite)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> INDEX idx_dept_salary <span clas=
s=3D"hljs-keyword">ON</span> employees(department, salary);

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Unique non-clustered index</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">UNI=
QUE</span> INDEX idx_email <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ON</span> employees=
(email);

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Drop an index</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">DROP</span> INDEX idx_emp_name <span class=3D"=
hljs-keyword">ON</span> employees;</code></pre></div>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"leftmost-prefix-rule" data-toc=3D"Leftmost-Prefix Rule">Leftmost-=
Prefix Rule</h3>
<p>This is the most important rule for <a href=3D"#3-composite-multi-column=
-index">composite indexes</a>. Take this index:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> INDEX idx_orders_customer_=
status_created
    <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ON</span> orders (customer_id, status, cre=
ated_at);</code></pre></div>
<p>The index is ordered lexicographically, like a phone book sorted by <cod=
e>(customer_id, status, created_at)</code>: first by <code>customer_id</cod=
e>; within the same customer by <code>status</code>; and within the same cu=
stomer and status by <code>created_at</code>.</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">text</span></div><pre><code class=3D=
"hljs">(customer_id, status, created_at)
(10, 'PAID',    2026-08-01)
(10, 'PAID',    2026-08-05)
(10, 'PENDING', 2026-08-02)
(11, 'PAID',    2026-08-03)</code></pre></div>
<p>The database can efficiently use only the <strong>leading, contiguous</s=
trong> columns of the index.</p>
<p>In general, a composite index on <code>(A, B, C)</code> can be used for =
queries that filter on:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>A</code> alone =E2=9C=85</li>
<li><code>A</code> and <code>B</code> =E2=9C=85</li>
<li><code>A</code> and <code>B</code> and <code>C</code> =E2=9C=85</li>
<li><code>A</code> and <code>C</code> =E2=80=94 partially =E2=9C=85 (uses A=
, skips to data for C with less efficiency)</li>
</ul>
<p>But it <strong>cannot</strong> be used for:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>B</code> alone =E2=9D=8C</li>
<li><code>C</code> alone =E2=9D=8C</li>
<li><code>B</code> and <code>C</code> =E2=9D=8C</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Why?</strong> Think of the phone book analogy. A phone book sort=
ed by <code>(last_name, first_name)</code> can answer &quot;find all Smiths=
&quot; and &quot;find Alice Smith&quot;, but it <strong>cannot</strong> ans=
wer &quot;find all Alices&quot; without scanning the entire book =E2=80=94 =
because Alices are scattered across different last names.</p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Query predicate / ordering</th>
<th style=3D"text-align:center">Uses this index efficiently?</th>
<th>Why</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>WHERE customer_id =3D 10</code></td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">Yes</td>
<td>Uses the first column</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>WHERE customer_id =3D 10 AND status =3D 'PAID'</code></td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">Yes</td>
<td>Uses the first two columns</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>WHERE customer_id =3D 10 AND status =3D 'PAID' AND created_at &gt=
;=3D '2026-08-01'</code></td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">Yes</td>
<td>Equality on leading columns, then a range on the next column</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>WHERE status =3D 'PAID'</code></td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">Usually no</td>
<td>The leading <code>customer_id</code> is missing</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>WHERE customer_id =3D 10 ORDER BY status, created_at</code></td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">Yes</td>
<td>The requested order matches the remaining index order</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>WHERE customer_id =3D 10 AND status &gt; 'PAID' AND created_at &g=
t;=3D '2026-08-01'</code></td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">Partly</td>
<td>The range on <code>status</code> limits how usefully later columns can =
narrow/search or satisfy ordering</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- =E2=9C=85 Uses the index (leftmost pr=
efix)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> employees <span class=3D"hl=
js-keyword">WHERE</span> department <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span=
> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;Engineering&#x27;</span>;
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> employees <span class=3D"hl=
js-keyword">WHERE</span> department <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span=
> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;Engineering&#x27;</span> <span class=3D=
"hljs-keyword">AND</span> salary <span class=3D"hljs-operator">&gt;</span> =
<span class=3D"hljs-number">80000</span>;

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- =E2=9D=8C Cannot use the index (skips the l=
eftmost column)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> employees <span class=3D"hl=
js-keyword">WHERE</span> salary <span class=3D"hljs-operator">&gt;</span> <=
span class=3D"hljs-number">80000</span>;</code></pre></div>
<div class=3D"markdown-alert markdown-alert-important"><p class=3D"markdown=
-alert-title"><svg class=3D"octicon octicon-report mr-2" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 =
16" version=3D"1.1" width=3D"16" height=3D"16" aria-hidden=3D"true"><path d=
=3D"M0 1.75C0 .784.784 0 1.75 0h12.5C15.216 0 16 .784 16 1.75v9.5A1.75 1.75=
 0 0 1 14.25 13H8.06l-2.573 2.573A1.458 1.458 0 0 1 3 14.543V13H1.75A1.75 1=
.75 0 0 1 0 11.25Zm1.75-.25a.25.25 0 0 0-.25.25v9.5c0 .138.112.25.25.25h2a.=
75.75 0 0 1 .75.75v2.19l2.72-2.72a.749.749 0 0 1 .53-.22h6.5a.25.25 0 0 0 .=
25-.25v-9.5a.25.25 0 0 0-.25-.25Zm7 2.25v2.5a.75.75 0 0 1-1.5 0v-2.5a.75.75=
 0 0 1 1.5 0ZM9 9a1 1 0 1 1-2 0 1 1 0 0 1 2 0Z"></path></svg>Important</p><=
p><strong>Key idea:</strong> An index on <code>(A, B, C)</code> is generall=
y useful for <code>(A)</code>, <code>(A, B)</code>, and <code>(A, B, C)</co=
de>, but not usually for <code>(B)</code> or <code>(C)</code> alone. It is =
not the same as three independent single-column indexes.</p>
</div>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"choosing-column-order" data-toc=3D"Choosing Column Order">Choosin=
g Column Order</h3>
<p>Design the index for the actual query pattern:</p>
<ol>
<li>Put columns tested with equality (<code>=3D</code> or <code>IN</code>) =
first =E2=80=94 among them, the <strong>most frequently filtered</strong> a=
nd <strong>most selective</strong> column (the one that eliminates the most=
 rows) leads.</li>
<li>Put the range column (<code>&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>BETWE=
EN</code>, prefix <code>LIKE</code>) after those equality columns.</li>
<li>Put columns used to satisfy <code>ORDER BY</code> next, when their dire=
ction/order can match the query.</li>
<li>Consider adding selected output columns last only when the database sup=
ports a covering index and the extra index size is justified (see <a href=
=3D"#covering-indexes-and-index-only-scans">Covering Indexes</a>).</li>
</ol>
<p>In short: <strong>equality columns first, range/sort columns last</stron=
g>.</p>
<p>For the earlier query, <code>(customer_id, status, created_at)</code> is=
 better than <code>(created_at, customer_id, status)</code> because the que=
ry first fixes <code>customer_id</code> and <code>status</code>, then needs=
 the matching rows ordered by <code>created_at</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Equality before range, even over selectivity:</strong> if the le=
ss selective column is used in equality and the more selective one is used =
in a range, MySQL can still benefit from the less selective column being fi=
rst.</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- If &#x27;department&#x27; selects 5% =
and &#x27;created_at&#x27; range selects 10%:</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Better: (department, created_at) =E2=80=94 =
equality first, range second</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> employees <span class=3D"hl=
js-keyword">WHERE</span> department <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span=
> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;Eng&#x27;</span> <span class=3D"hljs-ke=
yword">AND</span> created_at <span class=3D"hljs-operator">&gt;</span> <spa=
n class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;2025-01-01&#x27;</span>;</code></pre></div>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"composite-index-vs-separate-indexes" data-toc=3D"Composite Index =
vs Separate Indexes">Composite Index vs Separate Indexes</h3>
<p>If a common query filters on both columns, a composite index is often be=
tter:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Common query</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> orders
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> customer_id <span class=3D"hljs-o=
perator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">42</span> <span class=3D"hl=
js-keyword">AND</span> status <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> <spa=
n class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;PAID&#x27;</span>;

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Usually preferable for this query pattern</=
span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> INDEX idx_orders_customer_status=
 <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ON</span> orders (customer_id, status);</code=
></pre></div>
<p>Separate indexes on <code>customer_id</code> and <code>status</code> may=
 require the optimizer to use only one index or merge two index result sets=
. A composite index already stores the exact pair in useful order, so it us=
ually reads fewer entries.</p>
<div class=3D"markdown-alert markdown-alert-note"><p class=3D"markdown-aler=
t-title"><svg class=3D"octicon octicon-info mr-2" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 16" ver=
sion=3D"1.1" width=3D"16" height=3D"16" aria-hidden=3D"true"><path d=3D"M0 =
8a8 8 0 1 1 16 0A8 8 0 0 1 0 8Zm8-6.5a6.5 6.5 0 1 0 0 13 6.5 6.5 0 0 0 0-13=
ZM6.5 7.75A.75.75 0 0 1 7.25 7h1a.75.75 0 0 1 .75.75v2.75h.25a.75.75 0 0 1 =
0 1.5h-2a.75.75 0 0 1 0-1.5h.25v-2h-.25a.75.75 0 0 1-.75-.75ZM8 6a1 1 0 1 1=
 0-2 1 1 0 0 1 0 2Z"></path></svg>Note</p><p><strong>Trade-off:</strong> Do=
 not create every possible column combination. Composite indexes consume sp=
ace and increase write overhead. Keep only indexes that support real, measu=
red query patterns, and verify them with <code>EXPLAIN</code>.</p>
</div>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"clustered-vs-secondary-indexes-innodb-specifics" data-toc=3D"Clus=
tered vs Secondary Indexes (InnoDB Specifics)">Clustered vs Secondary Index=
es (InnoDB Specifics)</h3>
<p>This distinction is unique to InnoDB and is <strong>critical</strong> fo=
r understanding query performance. In general DBMS terms, the InnoDB primar=
y key index is the <strong>clustered index</strong> and every other index i=
s a <strong>secondary (non-clustered) index</strong>.</p>
<h4 id=3D"clustered-index-primary-key">Clustered Index (Primary Key)</h4>
<p>A <strong>clustered index</strong> physically sorts and stores the <stro=
ng>actual data rows</strong> in the order of the index key. The table data =
IS the index.</p>
<figure class=3D"diagram"><img class=3D"fig" src=3D"data:image/svg+xml;base=
64,PHN2ZyB4bWxucz0iaHR0cDovL3d3dy53My5vcmcvMjAwMC9zdmciIHdpZHRoPSI1NjQiIGhl=
aWdodD0iMjE0IiB2aWV3Qm94PSIwIDAgNTY0IDIxNCIgZm9udC1mYW1pbHk9IlNlZ29lIFVJLCB=
JbnRlciwgc3lzdGVtLXVpLCAtYXBwbGUtc3lzdGVtLCBzYW5zLXNlcmlmIiBzdHlsZT0iZm9udC=
12YXJpYW50LWxpZ2F0dXJlczpub25lIj4KICA8dGl0bGU+Q2x1c3RlcmVkIGluZGV4IG9uIGVtc=
