<h4 id=3D"why-primary-key-size-matters">Why Primary Key Size Matters</h4>
<p>Since <strong>every secondary index stores a copy of the primary key</st=
rong> in its leaf nodes, a large primary key bloats all secondary indexes.<=
/p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Primary Key Type</th>
<th>PK Size</th>
<th>Impact on Each Secondary Index Entry</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>INT</code></td>
<td>4 bytes</td>
<td>Small =E2=80=94 minimal overhead</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>BIGINT</code></td>
<td>8 bytes</td>
<td>Fine for most use cases</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>UUID</code> (<code>CHAR(36)</code>)</td>
<td>36 bytes</td>
<td>=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F 4.5x larger than <code>BIGINT</code> =E2=80=94 every=
 secondary index wastes space</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>VARCHAR(255)</code></td>
<td>Up to 255 bytes</td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C Terrible =E2=80=94 massively bloats all secondary indexes</td=
>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Keep primary keys <strong>short</strong=
> (prefer <code>INT</code> or <code>BIGINT</code> with auto-increment). If =
you must use UUIDs, store them as <code>BINARY(16)</code> (16 bytes) instea=
d of <code>CHAR(36)</code> (36 bytes). =F0=9F=94=97 The full trade-off is i=
n <a href=3D"#uuid-vs-auto-increment-integer-as-primary-key">UUID vs Auto-I=
ncrement Integer as Primary Key</a>.</p>
<h4 id=3D"clustered-vs-secondary--quick-reference">Clustered vs Secondary =
=E2=80=94 Quick Reference</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Aspect</th>
<th>Clustered Index</th>
<th>Secondary (Non-Clustered) Index</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Number per table</strong></td>
<td>Exactly 1</td>
<td>Many (InnoDB: up to 64; SQL Server: up to 999)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Data order</strong></td>
<td>Physically sorts data rows</td>
<td>Separate structure; doesn't change row order</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>What leaf nodes store</strong></td>
<td>Full row data (the data pages)</td>
<td>Indexed columns + primary key (the row pointer)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Additional disk space?</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C No (data IS the index)</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 Yes (separate index structure)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Columns returned directly</strong></td>
<td>All (it IS the row)</td>
<td>Only indexed columns (otherwise needs bookmark lookup)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Lookup cost</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 1 B+ Tree traversal</td>
<td>=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F 1 (index) + 1 (clustered lookup) =3D 2 traversals</t=
d>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Range scan</strong></td>
<td>Very fast (data is physically contiguous)</td>
<td>Slower (each row may need a random clustered lookup)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Default for</strong></td>
<td>Primary Key</td>
<td><code>UNIQUE</code> constraints and every other <code>CREATE INDEX</cod=
e></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Best for</strong></td>
<td>Range queries, <code>ORDER BY</code> on the key</td>
<td>Exact lookups, frequently searched columns</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Insert behavior</strong></td>
<td>Appends to end if auto-increment PK; random inserts cause page splits</=
td>
<td>Always positions by indexed value; random by nature</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Insert/Update overhead</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F Higher (must maintain physical order)</td>
<td>=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F Moderate (update index entries)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Analogy</strong></td>
<td>Phone book sorted by name</td>
<td>Index at back of textbook</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h4 id=3D"advantages--disadvantages">Advantages &amp; Disadvantages</h4>
<h5 id=3D"clustered-index">Clustered Index</h5>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>=E2=9C=85 Advantages</th>
<th>=E2=9D=8C Disadvantages</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Fast range queries (<code>BETWEEN</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;=
</code>)</td>
<td>Only one per table</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>No extra disk space</td>
<td>Slow inserts in non-sequential order (page splits)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Data is always sorted</td>
<td>Updates to indexed columns are expensive</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Cache-friendly (sequential reads)</td>
<td>Fragmentation over time</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h5 id=3D"secondary-non-clustered-index">Secondary (Non-Clustered) Index</h=
5>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>=E2=9C=85 Advantages</th>
<th>=E2=9D=8C Disadvantages</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Multiple per table</td>
<td>Extra disk space needed</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Speeds up frequent lookups</td>
<td>Bookmark lookup overhead</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Doesn't affect physical data order</td>
<td>Must update when clustering key changes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Can cover queries (covering index)</td>
<td>Maintenance cost on heavy write tables</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
