ng> &quot;What happens if you create a table without a primary key?&quot; =
=E2=80=94 In engines like SQL Server or PostgreSQL the table has no cluster=
ed index (it's a <strong>heap</strong>). InnoDB never stores a true heap: i=
t uses the first <code>UNIQUE NOT NULL</code> index as the clustered index,=
 or else creates a hidden 6-byte row ID. That hidden key can't be used in y=
our queries, so any lookup not covered by a secondary index is a full table=
 scan. Always define a primary key.</p>
</div>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"when-to-use-an-index" data-toc=3D"When to Use an Index">When to U=
se an Index</h3>
<h4 id=3D"-index-these-columns">=E2=9C=85 Index These Columns</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Column Type</th>
<th>Why</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Primary key</strong></td>
<td>Automatically indexed; defines the clustered index</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Foreign keys</strong></td>
<td>MySQL needs them for <code>JOIN</code> performance and <code>ON DELETE =
CASCADE</code> checks =E2=80=94 without an index, every FK check triggers a=
 full table scan on the child table</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Columns in <code>WHERE</code> clauses</strong></td>
<td>Equality (<code>=3D</code>), range (<code>&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;</code=
>, <code>BETWEEN</code>), and <code>IN</code> filters all benefit</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Columns in <code>JOIN ON</code> conditions</strong></td>
<td>Indexes on both sides of a join condition dramatically reduce lookup ti=
me</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Columns in <code>ORDER BY</code></strong></td>
<td>Avoids an expensive filesort =E2=80=94 data is already sorted in the in=
dex</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Columns in <code>GROUP BY</code></strong></td>
<td>Allows MySQL to group rows by reading the index in order</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>High-cardinality columns</strong></td>
<td>Columns with many distinct values (e.g., <code>email</code>, <code>orde=
r_id</code>) are ideal for indexes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Columns used in <code>UNIQUE</code> constraints</strong></td>
<td>Automatically indexed; also enables the optimizer to stop after one mat=
ch</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h4 id=3D"-do-not-index-these">=E2=9D=8C Do NOT Index These</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Column Type</th>
<th>Why</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Low-cardinality columns</strong></td>
<td>A <code>gender</code> column with only <code>M</code> and <code>F</code=
> has terrible selectivity =E2=80=94 an index on it would still match ~50% =
of rows, making a full scan cheaper</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Columns you rarely query on</strong></td>
<td>An index no one uses just wastes storage and slows down writes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Very wide columns</strong></td>
<td>A <code>VARCHAR(5000)</code> index entry is huge =E2=80=94 use a prefix=
 index or reconsider</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Frequently updated columns</strong></td>
<td>Every <code>UPDATE</code> to an indexed column means updating the index=
 B+ Tree too</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Small tables</strong></td>
<td>A table with 100 rows is faster to full-scan than to use an index (disk=
 seek for the index + disk seek for the row &gt; sequential scan of 100 row=
s)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Columns with lots of NULLs</strong> (usually)</td>
<td>Depends on the use case, but indexes on mostly-NULL columns are often w=
asteful</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Tables with heavy inserts</strong></td>
<td>Each index slows down writes =E2=80=94 keep indexes to a minimum (see <=
a href=3D"#impact-on-write-performance">Impact on Write Performance</a>)</t=
d>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"how-indexes-are-used-in-common-operations" data-toc=3D"How Indexe=
s Are Used in Common Operations">How Indexes Are Used in Common Operations<=
/h3>
<h4 id=3D"1-point-lookups-equality-queries">1. Point Lookups (Equality Quer=
ies)</h4>
<p>The most basic use case. MySQL walks down the B+ Tree to find the exact =
matching leaf node.</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Uses PRIMARY KEY index (1-2 page read=
s)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> employees <span class=3D"hl=
js-keyword">WHERE</span> id <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> <span =
class=3D"hljs-number">42</span>;

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Uses index on email (2-3 page reads for ind=
ex + 1 for row data)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> users <span class=3D"hljs-k=
eyword">WHERE</span> email <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> <span c=
lass=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;alice@example.com&#x27;</span>;</code></pre></di=
v>
<p><strong>Without index:</strong> Full table scan =E2=80=94 reads every si=
ngle row. O(N).
<strong>With index:</strong> B+ Tree seek. O(log N) =E2=80=94 in practice, =
2-4 disk reads.</p>
<h4 id=3D"2-range-queries">2. Range Queries</h4>
<p>This is where the B+ Tree's <strong>linked list at the leaf level</stron=
g> shines. MySQL seeks to the starting point, then walks the linked list fo=
rward (or backward) until the range boundary.</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Find all orders placed in January 202=
5</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> orders
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> order_date <span class=3D"hljs-ke=
yword">BETWEEN</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;2025-01-01&#x27;</s=
pan> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">AND</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#=
x27;2025-01-31&#x27;</span>;

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Find all products priced between $50 and $1=
00</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> products <span class=3D"hlj=
s-keyword">WHERE</span> price <span class=3D"hljs-operator">&gt;=3D</span> =
<span class=3D"hljs-number">50</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">AND</spa=
n> price <span class=3D"hljs-operator">&lt;=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-n=
umber">100</span>;

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Find all employees with salary &gt; 100000<=
/span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> employees <span class=3D"hl=
js-keyword">WHERE</span> salary <span class=3D"hljs-operator">&gt;</span> <=
span class=3D"hljs-number">100000</span>;</code></pre></div>
