<h3 id=3D"cardinality--selectivity--how-duplicate-values-affect-an-index" d=
ata-toc=3D"Cardinality &amp; Selectivity =E2=80=94 How Duplicate Values =E2=
=80=A6">Cardinality &amp; Selectivity =E2=80=94 How Duplicate Values Affect=
 an Index</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Many people share the same <code>name</code>.=
 If I create <code>INDEX(name)</code>, do the duplicates break it?</p>
<p><strong>Short answer:</strong> No. Duplicates don't break an index =E2=
=80=94 they make it <strong>less useful</strong>, and past a threshold the =
optimizer stops using it entirely.</p>
<h4 id=3D"structurally-duplicates-are-a-non-issue">Structurally, Duplicates=
 Are a Non-Issue</h4>
<p>A non-unique secondary index in InnoDB stores <code>[indexed column | pr=
imary key]</code>. So <code>INDEX(name)</code> is physically sorted by <cod=
e>(name, id)</code> =E2=80=94 every entry is still unique at the storage le=
vel. 500 people named &quot;John Smith&quot; =3D 500 index entries sitting =
contiguously, sorted by <code>id</code>. Nothing collides, nothing overflow=
s.</p>
