YW4+IGZ1bGwgdGFibGUgc2NhbjwvdGV4dD4KPC9zdmc+Cg=3D=3D" alt=3D"WHERE gender=
=3D'M': 500,000 cheap index entries but 500,000 random clustered lookups co=
st more than a sequential scan, so the optimizer ignores the index" title=
=3D"WHERE gender=3D'M': 500,000 cheap index entries but 500,000 random clus=
tered lookups cost more than a sequential scan, so the optimizer ignores th=
e index" loading=3D"lazy"></figure>
<p>An index is useful only when it can eliminate the <strong>vast majority<=
/strong> of rows. The rough tipping point is <strong>~20=E2=80=9330% of the=
 table</strong>. Past that, a sequential scan wins =E2=80=94 the random I/O=
 of index lookups costs more than a sequential read. <code>EXPLAIN</code> g=
ives it away:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs">EXPLAIN <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hlj=
s-operator">*</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> students <spa=
n class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> gender <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=
=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;M&#x27;</span>;
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">--   type: ALL   key: NULL     =E2=86=90 index=
 exists but was NOT used</span></code></pre></div>
<p>And you still pay for that index on <strong>every</strong> <code>INSERT<=
/code>, <code>UPDATE</code>, and <code>DELETE</code>. Worst of both worlds =
=E2=80=94 write cost with no read benefit.</p>
<p><strong>So for <code>name</code>: totally fine.</strong> Even a very com=
mon name is a tiny fraction of the table. Duplicates only hurt when a <stro=
ng>single value</strong> covers a large share of rows.</p>
<h4 id=3D"=EF=B8=8F-the-nuance-that-bites--data-skew">=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F Th=
e Nuance That Bites =E2=80=94 Data Skew</h4>
<p>The decision is made <strong>per value</strong>, not per column. If <cod=
e>status</code> is 95% <code>'active'</code>:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> status <span class=3D"hljs-=
operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;active&#x27;</span> =
    <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- 950,000 rows =E2=86=92 full table scan,=
 index ignored</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> status <span class=3D"hljs-operat=
or">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;cancelled&#x27;</span>  <s=
pan class=3D"hljs-comment">--     800 rows =E2=86=92 index used</span></cod=
e></pre></div>
<p>Same index, opposite decisions. By default MySQL assumes values are <str=
ong>uniformly distributed</strong>, so it guesses wrong on skewed columns. =
Fix it by giving the optimizer real distribution data:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs">ANALYZE <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TABLE</span> orders <span class=
=3D"hljs-keyword">UPDATE</span> HISTOGRAM <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ON</=
span> status;   <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- MySQL 8.0+</span></code></p=
re></div>
<h4 id=3D"how-to-check-your-own-columns">How to Check Your Own Columns</h4>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Exact selectivity</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">CO=
UNT</span>(<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">DISTINCT</span> name) <span class=
=3D"hljs-operator">/</span> <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">COUNT</span>(<spa=
n class=3D"hljs-operator">*</span>) <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">AS</span> =
selectivity <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> students;

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Rows per value =E2=80=94 the number that ac=
tually matters</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> name, <span class=3D"hljs-built_=
in">COUNT</span>(<span class=3D"hljs-operator">*</span>) <span class=3D"hlj=
s-keyword">AS</span> cnt
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> students <span class=3D"hljs-keywo=
rd">GROUP</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">BY</span> name <span class=3D=
"hljs-keyword">ORDER</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">BY</span> cnt <spa=
n class=3D"hljs-keyword">DESC</span> LIMIT <span class=3D"hljs-number">10</=
span>;

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- What the optimizer currently believes (an e=
stimate from sampled dives)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SHOW</span> INDEX <span class=3D"hljs-keyword"=
>FROM</span> students;   <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- read the Cardinali=
ty column</span>
ANALYZE <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TABLE</span> students;     <span class=
=3D"hljs-comment">-- refresh those estimates</span>

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Optimizer statistics for every index, with =
selectivity as a percentage</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span>
    INDEX_NAME,
    COLUMN_NAME,
    <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CARDINALITY</span>,
    ROUND(<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CARDINALITY</span> <span class=3D"hl=
js-operator">/</span> (<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span cla=
ss=3D"hljs-built_in">COUNT</span>(<span class=3D"hljs-operator">*</span>) <=
span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> students) <span class=3D"hljs-opera=
tor">*</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">100</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-n=
umber">2</span>) <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">AS</span> selectivity_pct
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> information_schema.STATISTICS
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> TABLE_NAME <span class=3D"hljs-op=
erator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;students&#x27;</span>;=
</code></pre></div>
<h4 id=3D"the-fix-for-a-low-selectivity-column">The Fix for a Low-Selectivi=
ty Column</h4>
<p>Don't index it alone =E2=80=94 make it part of a <strong>composite index=
</strong>, with a selective column leading:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- =E2=9D=8C Useless on its own</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> INDEX idx_gender <span class=3D"=
hljs-keyword">ON</span> students(gender);

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- =E2=9C=85 Selective column first, then the =
low-cardinality one</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> INDEX idx_dept_gender <span clas=
s=3D"hljs-keyword">ON</span> students(dept_id, gender);

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- =E2=9C=85 Or pair it with something that ma=
kes the result set small</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> INDEX idx_status_created <span c=
lass=3D"hljs-keyword">ON</span> orders(status, created_at);</code></pre></d=
iv>
<p>The <strong>leading column</strong> decides whether the index is seekabl=
e at all =E2=80=94 see <a href=3D"#leftmost-prefix-rule">Leftmost-Prefix Ru=
le</a> and <a href=3D"#choosing-column-order">Choosing Column Order</a>.</p=
>
