 B rows (~20 per 16 KB page) vs secondary email leaf holding ~40-byte (emai=
l, PK) entries (~400 per page)" title=3D"Clustered PK leaf holds whole ~800=
 B rows (~20 per 16 KB page) vs secondary email leaf holding ~40-byte (emai=
l, PK) entries (~400 per page)" loading=3D"lazy"></figure>
<p>A split in the clustered index relocates half a page of <strong>full row=
s</strong>. A split in a secondary index relocates half a page of <strong>t=
iny pointers</strong>.</p>
<h5 id=3D"blast-radius--the-real-reason">Blast Radius =E2=80=94 The Real Re=
ason</h5>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th>Random <strong>PK</strong> (UUIDv4)</th>
<th>Random <strong>secondary index</strong> (email)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>What gets fragmented</strong></td>
<td>=E2=98=A0=EF=B8=8F <strong>The entire table</strong></td>
<td>Only that one index</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Effect on other indexes</strong></td>
<td>=E2=98=A0=EF=B8=8F <strong>Every</strong> secondary index gets fatter =
=E2=80=94 they all store the PK as their row pointer, so a 16-byte UUID blo=
ats all of them</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 None</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Effect on range scans over the table</strong></td>
<td>=E2=98=A0=EF=B8=8F Destroyed =E2=80=94 the table's physical order <em>i=
s</em> the PK order</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 None =E2=80=94 the table stays perfectly packed</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Can you undo it later?</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C Only by rebuilding the whole table</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 <code>ALTER TABLE t DROP INDEX idx_email</code> =E2=80=94 ins=
tant</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>The root cause of the asymmetry:</strong> InnoDB's clustered ind=
ex <em>forces the physical order of your rows</em> to follow the primary ke=
y. A secondary index has no such power =E2=80=94 it's just a side tree. So =
a bad PK poisons <strong>everything</strong>; a bad secondary index poisons=
 <strong>only itself</strong>.</p>
<h5 id=3D"the-same-percentage-a-wildly-different-bill">The Same Percentage,=
 a Wildly Different Bill</h5>
<p>If your table is 100 GB and the email index is 3 GB, 50% fragmentation m=
eans:</p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th>Before</th>
<th>After</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Random PK</td>
<td>100 GB</td>
<td><strong>~200 GB</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Random email index</td>
<td>3 GB</td>
<td>~6 GB</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h5 id=3D"where-the-worry-does-have-teeth">Where the Worry <em>Does</em> Ha=
ve Teeth</h5>
<p><code>email</code> is almost always <code>UNIQUE</code> =E2=80=94 and un=
ique secondary indexes <strong>cannot use the change buffer</strong> (see t=
he table above). Every insert must read the target page from disk <em>right=
 now</em> to check for duplicates.</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">UNIQUE</span> KEY uk_email (email)   <sp=
an class=3D"hljs-comment">-- =E2=9D=8C no change buffer, forced random read=
 per insert</span>
KEY idx_email (email)         <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- =E2=9C=85 cha=
nge buffer defers the write</span></code></pre></div>
<p>So <code>UNIQUE(email)</code> genuinely is one of the more expensive sec=
ondary indexes you can have. It's still worth it =E2=80=94 you want the dat=
abase enforcing that constraint, not application code =E2=80=94 but that's =
the honest cost.</p>
