thread removes records and frees undo pages, page merge below MERGE_THRESHO=
LD" title=3D"DELETE lifecycle: delete-mark in clustered and secondary index=
es, commit, purge thread removes records and frees undo pages, page merge b=
elow MERGE_THRESHOLD" loading=3D"lazy"></figure>
<p><strong>Why delete-mark instead of deleting?</strong> Two reasons:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>MVCC</strong> =E2=80=94 an older transaction's read view may st=
ill legitimately need to see that row</li>
<li><strong>Rollback</strong> =E2=80=94 the transaction isn't committed yet=
 and may be undone</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>And the space still doesn't return to the OS.</strong> Freed pag=
es go onto the tablespace's free list for reuse by that table; the <code>.i=
bd</code> file does not shrink. Only a full table rebuild reclaims it:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs">OPTIMIZE <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TABLE</span> orders;        <sp=
an class=3D"hljs-comment">-- InnoDB maps this to a rebuild + ANALYZE</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ALTER</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TABL=
E</span> orders FORCE;     <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- identical operat=
ion</span></code></pre></div>
<p>=F0=9F=94=97 See <a href=3D"#truncate-vs-drop-vs-delete--a-common-confus=
ion">TRUNCATE vs DROP vs DELETE</a> =E2=80=94 <code>TRUNCATE</code> drops a=
nd recreates the tablespace, returning all space instantly.</p>
<h4 id=3D"=EF=B8=8F-purge-lag--when-delete-backfires">=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F Pu=
rge Lag =E2=80=94 When DELETE Backfires</h4>
<p>If a <strong>long-running transaction</strong> (even an idle-in-transact=
ion <code>SELECT</code>) holds an old read view, the purge thread cannot ad=
vance. Delete-marked rows pile up, undo history grows, and the undo tablesp=
ace can bloat to hundreds of GB.</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SHOW</span> ENGINE INNODB STATUS\G     <=
span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- look for: History list length</span></code><=
/pre></div>
<p>A history list length in the millions means purge is starving =E2=80=94 =
find and kill the long transaction:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operat=
or">*</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> information_schema.IN=
NODB_TRX <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ORDER</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyw=
ord">BY</span> trx_started LIMIT <span class=3D"hljs-number">5</span>;</cod=
e></pre></div>
<h4 id=3D"real-world-write-overhead">Real-World Write Overhead</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Indexes on Table</th>
<th>INSERT Relative Speed</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>0 indexes (heap)</td>
<td>1=C3=97 (baseline)</td>
<td>Not possible in InnoDB (always has PK)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>1 (primary key only)</td>
<td>~1=C3=97</td>
<td>Just the clustered index</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3 indexes</td>
<td>~1.5-2=C3=97 slower</td>
<td>Each index adds ~15-30% overhead</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>5 indexes</td>
<td>~2-3=C3=97 slower</td>
<td>Noticeable on high-throughput writes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>10+ indexes</td>
<td>~3-5=C3=97 slower</td>
<td>Seriously impacts write performance</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h4 id=3D"best-practices-for-write-heavy-tables">Best Practices for Write-H=
eavy Tables</h4>
<ol>
<li><strong>Use a sequential primary key</strong> (<code>AUTO_INCREMENT</co=
de>, UUIDv7/ULID) =E2=80=94 this single choice eliminates most page splits =
and fragmentation</li>
<li><strong>Only create indexes you actually use</strong> =E2=80=94 run <co=
de>SHOW INDEX FROM table</code> and check if any index is never hit</li>
<li><strong>Monitor unused indexes:</strong><div class=3D"code"><div class=
=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span class=
=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-com=
ment">-- MySQL 8.0+ performance_schema</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> OBJECT_SCHEMA, OBJECT_NAME, INDE=
X_NAME
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> performance_schema.table_io_waits_=
summary_by_index_usage
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> INDEX_NAME <span class=3D"hljs-ke=
yword">IS</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">NOT</span> <span class=3D"hlj=
s-keyword">NULL</span>
  <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">AND</span> COUNT_STAR <span class=3D"hljs-op=
erator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">0</span>
  <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">AND</span> OBJECT_SCHEMA <span class=3D"hljs=
-operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;your_database&#x27;=
</span>;</code></pre></div>
</li>
<li><strong>Bulk insert tip:</strong> insert in <strong>primary key order</=
strong>, and drop non-unique secondary indexes before bulk loading, then re=
create them =E2=80=94 building an index once from sorted data is much faste=
r than inserting into an existing index row by row</li>
<li><strong>Prefer composite indexes over multiple single-column indexes</s=
trong> =E2=80=94 one composite index replaces two or three single-column in=
dexes, reducing write overhead</li>
<li><strong>Avoid <code>UNIQUE</code> on secondary indexes unless required<=
/strong> =E2=80=94 it disables the change buffer for that index</li>
<li><strong>Never update the primary key</strong> =E2=80=94 treat it as imm=
utable; it's the most expensive write in InnoDB</li>
<li><strong>Run <code>ANALYZE TABLE</code> after bulk inserts or deletes</s=
trong> =E2=80=94 statistics go stale and the optimizer starts choosing bad =
plans</li>
<li><strong>Clearing a whole table?</strong> Use <code>TRUNCATE</code>, not=
 <code>DELETE</code> =E2=80=94 no per-row undo, no purge backlog, space ret=
urned immediately</li>
</ol>
<h4 id=3D"quick-fire-qa">Quick-Fire Q&amp;A</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Question</th>
<th>Answer</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>How many B+ Trees does one INSERT touch?</td>
<td>1 clustered + 1 per secondary index</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Does DELETE remove the index entry?</td>
<td><strong>No</strong> =E2=80=94 it delete-marks it; the purge thread remo=
ves it later</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Why doesn't the <code>.ibd</code> file shrink after a big DELETE?</td>
<td>Freed pages return to the table's free list, not to the OS. A file can =
only be truncated from the <em>end</em>, and free pages are scattered throu=
ghout. Only a rebuild reclaims it.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Which is cheapest: updating an indexed column, a non-indexed column, or=
 the PK?</td>
<td>Non-indexed column (in place) &lt; indexed column (delete+insert on tha=
t index) &lt; PK (delete+insert on <strong>every</strong> index)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Why is a UNIQUE secondary index slower to write than a normal one?</td>
<td>It can't use the change buffer =E2=80=94 uniqueness must be checked imm=
ediately, forcing a disk read</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>What causes a page split?</td>
<td>Inserting into a leaf page that is already full =E2=80=94 typical with =
a random PK</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>If a random UUID is bad as a PK, is a random <code>email</code> index b=
ad too?</td>
<td>Same mechanism, far smaller blast radius. Only the <strong>clustered in=
dex</strong> must be sequential; random secondary indexes are normal and co=
rrect</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>What is &quot;History list length&quot; telling you?</td>
<td>How far behind the purge thread is; a large value means a long transact=
ion is blocking purge</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"explain--reading-query-execution-plans" data-toc=3D"EXPLAIN =E2=
=80=94 Reading Query Execution Plans">EXPLAIN =E2=80=94 Reading Query Execu=
tion Plans</h3>
<p><code>EXPLAIN</code> is the single most important tool for understanding=
 how MySQL executes your queries and whether your indexes are being used. A=
lways use <code>EXPLAIN</code> before and after adding indexes. (Where the =
plan comes from is covered in <a href=3D"#mysql-query-pipeline--parser-prec=
ompiler--the-cost-based-optimizer">The Cost-Based Optimizer</a>.)</p>
<h4 id=3D"explain-vs-explain-analyze">EXPLAIN vs EXPLAIN ANALYZE</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th><code>EXPLAIN</code></th>
<th><code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> (MySQL 8.0.18+)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Runs the query?</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C No =E2=80=94 only asks the optimizer for its plan</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 <strong>Yes</strong> =E2=80=94 executes it and measures every=
 step</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Row counts</strong></td>
<td><strong>Estimates</strong> (from index statistics)</td>
<td>Estimates <strong>and actual</strong> rows</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Timing</strong></td>
<td>None</td>
<td>Actual time per step (ms)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Speed / safety</strong></td>
<td>Instant, always safe</td>
<td>Takes as long as the query itself</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Use it to=E2=80=A6</strong></td>
<td>See <em>which</em> index and access path the optimizer picked</td>
<td>Find <em>where</em> the time really goes, and catch wrong estimates</td=
>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
