p><strong>Real time of a node =E2=89=88 <code>LAST =C3=97 loops</code>.</st=
rong> A tiny 0.001 ms lookup that runs 100,000 times is 100 ms. And when th=
e <strong>estimated rows</strong> and <strong>actual rows</strong> differ b=
y 10=C3=97 or more, the optimizer planned with wrong numbers. That's usuall=
y the root cause of a bad plan.</p>
</div>
<p><strong>Example A =E2=80=94 Scenario 1 (no index):</strong></p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs">EXPLAIN ANALYZE <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span clas=
s=3D"hljs-operator">*</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> order=
s <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> customer_id <span class=3D"hljs=
-operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">42</span>;</code></pre></=
div>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">text</span></div><pre><code class=3D=
"hljs">-&gt; Filter: (orders.customer_id =3D 42)  (cost=3D50540.25 rows=3D4=
9873) (actual time=3D0.422..196.310 rows=3D10 loops=3D1)
    -&gt; Table scan on orders  (cost=3D50540.25 rows=3D498734) (actual tim=
e=3D0.065..171.470 rows=3D500000 loops=3D1)</code></pre></div>
<p>Reading inside-out:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Table scan on orders</strong> read <strong>500,000</strong> row=
s in ~171 ms.</li>
<li><strong>Filter</strong> kept <strong>10</strong> of them; whole query t=
ook ~196 ms.</li>
<li>Estimate vs actual: the filter expected <strong>49,873</strong> rows (t=
he 10% guess) but got <strong>10</strong>. A 5,000=C3=97 miss: MySQL has no=
 statistics on an un-indexed column.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Same query after Scenario 2's index:</strong></p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">text</span></div><pre><code class=3D=
"hljs">-&gt; Index lookup on orders using idx_customer (customer_id=3D42)  =
(cost=3D3.50 rows=3D10) (actual time=3D0.038..0.052 rows=3D10 loops=3D1)</c=
ode></pre></div>
<p>One node, 10 estimated =3D 10 actual, <strong>~0.05 ms instead of ~196 m=
s</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>Example B =E2=80=94 Scenario 4 (<code>ORDER BY =E2=80=A6 LIMIT</=
code>), before and after the fix:</strong></p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">text</span></div><pre><code class=3D=
"hljs">-- Before (idx_cust_status_date): sort step present
-&gt; Limit: 20 row(s)  (cost=3D4.54 rows=3D10) (actual time=3D0.089..0.091=
 rows=3D10 loops=3D1)
    -&gt; Sort: orders.created_at DESC, limit input to 20 row(s) per chunk =
 (cost=3D4.54 rows=3D10) (actual time=3D0.088..0.089 rows=3D10 loops=3D1)
        -&gt; Index lookup on orders using idx_cust_status_date (customer_i=
d=3D42)  (cost=3D4.54 rows=3D10) (actual time=3D0.040..0.075 rows=3D10 loop=
s=3D1)

-- After (idx_cust_date): no Sort node, index read in reverse
-&gt; Limit: 20 row(s)  (cost=3D3.50 rows=3D10) (actual time=3D0.035..0.051=
 rows=3D10 loops=3D1)
    -&gt; Index lookup on orders using idx_cust_date (customer_id=3D42) (re=
verse)  (cost=3D3.50 rows=3D10) (actual time=3D0.034..0.049 rows=3D10 loops=
=3D1)</code></pre></div>
<p>The <code>Sort</code> node disappears. For customer 42 the saving is tin=
y; for a customer with 50,000 orders the old plan sorts 50,000 rows to retu=
rn 20, while the new one reads just 20.</p>
<p><strong>Example C =E2=80=94 Scenario 6 (the JOIN):</strong></p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">text</span></div><pre><code class=3D=
"hljs">-&gt; Nested loop inner join  (cost=3D1201.25 rows=3D1087) (actual t=
ime=3D0.152..6.881 rows=3D1203 loops=3D1)
    -&gt; Filter: (o.`status` =3D 'SHIPPED')  (cost=3D820.60 rows=3D1087) (=
actual time=3D0.120..4.502 rows=3D1203 loops=3D1)
        -&gt; Index range scan on o using idx_created over ('2026-09-01 00:=
00:00' &lt;=3D created_at)  (cost=3D820.60 rows=3D5434) (actual time=3D0.11=
2..3.910 rows=3D6011 loops=3D1)
    -&gt; Single-row index lookup on c using PRIMARY (id=3Do.customer_id)  =
(cost=3D0.25 rows=3D1) (actual time=3D0.001..0.001 rows=3D1 loops=3D1203)</=
code></pre></div>
<ol>
<li><strong>Index range scan</strong> (deepest) read <strong>6,011</strong>=
 orders (estimate 5,434: close enough =E2=9C=85) in ~3.9 ms.</li>
<li><strong>Filter</strong> kept <strong>1,203</strong> <code>SHIPPED</code=
> ones (estimate 1,087 =E2=9C=85).</li>
<li><strong>Single-row index lookup on c</strong> ran <strong>1,203 times</=
strong> (<code>loops=3D1203</code>), ~0.001 ms each =E2=86=92 ~1.2 ms total=
.</li>
<li><strong>Nested loop join</strong> returned 1,203 rows; total ~6.9 ms. E=
stimates match actuals, so the plan is sound. Most of the time is the range=
 scan, which is where an index on <code>(status, created_at)</code> would h=
elp.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>When estimates lie:</strong> if a node shows <code>rows=3D27</co=
de> estimated but <code>rows=3D48210</code> actual, the optimizer chose its=
 plan for 27 rows. Typical causes and fixes:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs">ANALYZE <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TABLE</span> orders;            =
                       <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- refresh index statis=
tics</span>
ANALYZE <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TABLE</span> orders <span class=3D"hlj=
s-keyword">UPDATE</span> HISTOGRAM <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ON</span> s=
tatus;        <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- 8.0+: stats for skewed, un-in=
dexed columns</span></code></pre></div>
<h4 id=3D"spotting-problems--a-60-second-checklist">Spotting Problems =E2=
=80=94 A 60-Second Checklist</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>You see</th>
<th>It means</th>
<th>Try</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>type =3D ALL</code> on a big table</td>
<td>Full table scan</td>
<td>Index the <code>WHERE</code> / <code>JOIN</code> columns</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>possible_keys</code> set but <code>key =3D NULL</code></td>
<td>Optimizer rejected the index (low selectivity, or a function/implicit c=
ast on the column)</td>
<td>Check <a href=3D"#cardinality--selectivity--how-duplicate-values-affect=
-an-index">selectivity</a>; remove <code>func(col)</code> and type mismatch=
es</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>key_len</code> smaller than expected</td>
<td>Only the leading part of a composite index is used</td>
<td>Reorder columns: equality first, range/sort last</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>Using filesort</code> on a large result</td>
<td>Sort not served by an index</td>
<td>Index matching <code>WHERE</code> columns + <code>ORDER BY</code> colum=
ns</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>Using temporary</code></td>
<td>Temp table for <code>GROUP BY</code> / <code>DISTINCT</code></td>
<td>Index on the grouping columns</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>Using join buffer (hash join)</code></td>
<td>Join column not indexed</td>
<td>Index the inner table's join column</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>rows =C3=97 filtered</code> huge across a join</td>
<td>Lots of rows flowing through the join</td>
<td>Filter earlier, better join order, covering index</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Estimated vs actual rows off by 10=C3=97+ (<code>ANALYZE</code>)</td>
<td>Stale or missing statistics</td>
<td><code>ANALYZE TABLE</code>, histograms</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Small <code>actual time</code> but huge <code>loops</code></td>
<td>Inner side of a join runs too often</td>
<td>Reduce outer rows, or index the inner lookup</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
