<h3 id=3D"common-index-mistakes" data-toc=3D"Common Index Mistakes">Common =
Index Mistakes</h3>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Mistake</th>
<th>Why It's Bad</th>
<th>Fix</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Indexing every column</td>
<td>Wastes storage, kills write performance</td>
<td>Index only queried columns</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>WHERE YEAR(created_at) =3D 2025</code></td>
<td>Function on column =E2=86=92 index unusable</td>
<td><code>WHERE created_at &gt;=3D '2025-01-01' AND created_at &lt; '2026-0=
1-01'</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>WHERE age + 10 &gt; 30</code></td>
<td>Arithmetic on column =E2=86=92 index unusable</td>
<td><code>WHERE age &gt; 20</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>WHERE LOWER(email) =3D 'alice@...'</code></td>
<td>Function on column =E2=86=92 index unusable</td>
<td>Use a generated column or collation: <code>WHERE email =3D 'alice@...'<=
/code> (if collation is ci)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>VARCHAR(255)</code> primary key</td>
<td>Bloats every secondary index by 255 bytes per entry</td>
<td>Use <code>INT</code> or <code>BIGINT</code> auto-increment</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Too many single-column indexes</strong></td>
<td>MySQL uses at most 1 index per table per query (in most cases)</td>
<td>Use composite indexes that match your query patterns</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Wrong column order in composite index</strong></td>
<td><code>INDEX(salary, dept)</code> can't help <code>WHERE dept =3D 'Eng'<=
/code></td>
<td>Put equality columns first, range columns last</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Not checking EXPLAIN</strong></td>
<td>You never know if your index is actually being used</td>
<td>Always <code>EXPLAIN</code> your important queries</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Redundant indexes</strong></td>
<td><code>INDEX(a)</code> is redundant if <code>INDEX(a, b)</code> already =
exists</td>
<td>Drop the single-column index</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"quick-decision-guide-for-indexes" data-toc=3D"Quick Decision Guid=
e for Indexes">Quick Decision Guide for Indexes</h3>
<h4 id=3D"index-types-at-a-glance">Index Types at a Glance</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Type</th>
<th>What it is</th>
<th>Note</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Primary / Clustered</strong></td>
<td>The index <strong>is</strong> the table =E2=80=94 leaf nodes hold the a=
ctual rows, physically ordered by the key</td>
<td>Only <strong>one</strong> per table. See <a href=3D"#clustered-vs-secon=
dary-indexes-innodb-specifics">Clustered vs Secondary Indexes</a></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Secondary / Non-Clustered</strong></td>
<td>A separate structure whose leaves hold the key + a pointer to the row (=
the PK in InnoDB)</td>
<td>Many per table. A lookup traverses <strong>two</strong> B+ trees (index=
, then clustered)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Unique</strong></td>
<td>Like a primary key but <strong>allows NULLs</strong> =E2=80=94 and mult=
iple NULLs, since NULL =E2=89=A0 NULL</td>
<td>See <a href=3D"#primary-key-vs-unique-key">Primary Key vs Unique Key</a=
></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Composite</strong></td>
<td>One index over multiple columns (MySQL: up to 16)</td>
<td>Usable only by a <strong>leftmost prefix</strong>: <code>(a)</code>, <c=
ode>(a,b)</code>, <code>(a,b,c)</code> =E2=80=94 see <a href=3D"#leftmost-p=
refix-rule">Leftmost-Prefix Rule</a></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Covering</strong></td>
<td>An index (usually composite) that contains <strong>every column the que=
ry needs</strong></td>
<td>The query is answered from the index alone =E2=80=94 no table access (&=
quot;index-only scan&quot;)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Prefix</strong></td>
<td>Indexes only the <strong>first N characters</strong> of a column</td>
<td>Much smaller; used for long <code>VARCHAR</code>/<code>BLOB</code> colu=
mns</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Full-Text / Spatial / Hash</strong></td>
<td>Inverted index / R-Tree / hash table</td>
<td>Word search / geometry / exact match only (MEMORY engine)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h4 id=3D"which-index-for-which-scenario">Which Index for Which Scenario</h=
4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Scenario</th>
<th>Recommended Index</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Lookup by primary key</td>
<td>Already indexed (clustered index)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Lookup by unique column (email, username)</td>
<td><code>UNIQUE INDEX</code> on that column</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Filtering + sorting on same query</td>
<td>Composite index <code>(filter_col, sort_col)</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>JOIN between two tables</td>
<td>Index on the foreign key column</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Full-text search in articles</td>
<td><code>FULLTEXT INDEX</code> on text columns</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>&quot;Find nearest&quot; geo queries</td>
<td><code>SPATIAL INDEX</code> on geometry column</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Frequent <code>GROUP BY department</code></td>
<td>Index on <code>department</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>COUNT(*)</code> / <code>SUM()</code> with filter</td>
<td>Covering index including filter + aggregated column</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>High-write, low-read table (logs)</td>
<td>Minimal indexes =E2=80=94 primary key only if possible</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
