=3D"lazy"></figure>
<h4 id=3D"step-3-store-the-index-as-a-sorted-structure">Step 3: Store the I=
ndex as a Sorted Structure</h4>
<p>The index itself is stored as a small sorted file (or B+ Tree). Since it=
's tiny, it usually fits in one or two disk pages.</p>
<h4 id=3D"in-practice-innodb">In Practice (InnoDB)</h4>
<p>You don't explicitly say <code>CREATE SPARSE INDEX</code>. The database =
engine decides internally:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>InnoDB's clustered index (B+ Tree)</strong> is conceptually sim=
ilar to a sparse index at the internal node level =E2=80=94 internal nodes =
point to <strong>pages</strong> (blocks), not individual rows. Only the lea=
f level has per-row entries.</li>
<li><strong>InnoDB's secondary indexes</strong> are dense =E2=80=94 they ha=
ve one entry per row.</li>
</ul>
<p>The sparse index concept is most visible in:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Database internals</strong> (how B+ Tree internal nodes work)</=
li>
<li><strong>SSTable files</strong> in LSM-Tree databases (Cassandra, RocksD=
B, LevelDB) =E2=80=94 they use sparse indexes on sorted data blocks</li>
<li><strong>Academic/exam contexts</strong> =E2=80=94 the concept is taught=
 as a fundamental indexing strategy</li>
</ul>
<h4 id=3D"critical-constraint">Critical Constraint</h4>
<div class=3D"markdown-alert markdown-alert-note"><p class=3D"markdown-aler=
t-title"><svg class=3D"octicon octicon-info mr-2" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 16" ver=
sion=3D"1.1" width=3D"16" height=3D"16" aria-hidden=3D"true"><path d=3D"M0 =
8a8 8 0 1 1 16 0A8 8 0 0 1 0 8Zm8-6.5a6.5 6.5 0 1 0 0 13 6.5 6.5 0 0 0 0-13=
ZM6.5 7.75A.75.75 0 0 1 7.25 7h1a.75.75 0 0 1 .75.75v2.75h.25a.75.75 0 0 1 =
0 1.5h-2a.75.75 0 0 1 0-1.5h.25v-2h-.25a.75.75 0 0 1-.75-.75ZM8 6a1 1 0 1 1=
 0-2 1 1 0 0 1 0 2Z"></path></svg>Note</p><p><strong>A sparse index can ONL=
Y be built on the column the data is physically sorted by.</strong> You can=
 have at most <strong>one</strong> sparse index per table (because a table =
can only be physically sorted one way). Any other column needs a dense inde=
x. This is exactly why InnoDB has one clustered index (sparse-like) and all=
 secondary indexes are dense.</p>
</div>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"dense-vs-sparse-index--quick-comparison" data-toc=3D"Dense vs Spa=
rse Index =E2=80=94 Quick Comparison">Dense vs Sparse Index =E2=80=94 Quick=
 Comparison</h3>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Aspect</th>
<th>Dense Index</th>
<th>Sparse Index</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Entries</strong></td>
<td>One per row</td>
<td>One per block/page</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Size</strong></td>
<td>Large (proportional to row count)</td>
<td>Small (proportional to block count)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Lookup speed</strong></td>
<td>Direct =E2=80=94 find exact row</td>
<td>Jump to block + short scan</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Insert/delete cost</strong></td>
<td>Must update index every time</td>
<td>Update only when block boundaries change</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Requires sorted data?</strong></td>
<td>No</td>
<td><strong>Yes</strong> =E2=80=94 mandatory</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Max per table</strong></td>
<td>Many</td>
<td><strong>One</strong> (only on the sort column)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Can cover queries?</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 Yes (covering index)</td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C No =E2=80=94 must read data block</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Best for</strong></td>
<td>Any column, random access, covering queries</td>
<td>Sorted/clustered data, range scans, write-heavy workloads</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<div class=3D"markdown-alert markdown-alert-important"><p class=3D"markdown=
-alert-title"><svg class=3D"octicon octicon-report mr-2" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 =
16" version=3D"1.1" width=3D"16" height=3D"16" aria-hidden=3D"true"><path d=
=3D"M0 1.75C0 .784.784 0 1.75 0h12.5C15.216 0 16 .784 16 1.75v9.5A1.75 1.75=
 0 0 1 14.25 13H8.06l-2.573 2.573A1.458 1.458 0 0 1 3 14.543V13H1.75A1.75 1=
.75 0 0 1 0 11.25Zm1.75-.25a.25.25 0 0 0-.25.25v9.5c0 .138.112.25.25.25h2a.=
75.75 0 0 1 .75.75v2.19l2.72-2.72a.749.749 0 0 1 .53-.22h6.5a.25.25 0 0 0 .=
25-.25v-9.5a.25.25 0 0 0-.25-.25Zm7 2.25v2.5a.75.75 0 0 1-1.5 0v-2.5a.75.75=
 0 0 1 1.5 0ZM9 9a1 1 0 1 1-2 0 1 1 0 0 1 2 0Z"></path></svg>Important</p><=
p><strong>The key trade-off in one line:</strong> Sparse index trades <stro=
ng>lookup precision</strong> (must scan within a block) for <strong>smaller=
 size</strong> (fits in memory, cheaper writes). Dense index trades <strong=
>size and write cost</strong> for <strong>exact, direct access</strong> to =
every row.</p>
</div>
<hr>
<hr>
</section><section class=3D"sec"><h2 id=3D"cursor-in-sql" data-toc=3D"22. C=
ursor in SQL"><span class=3D"sec-n">22.</span> Cursor in SQL</h2>
<p>A <strong>cursor</strong> is a database object that allows you to proces=
s query results <strong>one row at a time</strong>, instead of all at once.=
 Think of it as a pointer that moves through the result set row by row.</p>
<div class=3D"markdown-alert markdown-alert-note"><p class=3D"markdown-aler=
t-title"><svg class=3D"octicon octicon-info mr-2" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 16" ver=
sion=3D"1.1" width=3D"16" height=3D"16" aria-hidden=3D"true"><path d=3D"M0 =
8a8 8 0 1 1 16 0A8 8 0 0 1 0 8Zm8-6.5a6.5 6.5 0 1 0 0 13 6.5 6.5 0 0 0 0-13=
ZM6.5 7.75A.75.75 0 0 1 7.25 7h1a.75.75 0 0 1 .75.75v2.75h.25a.75.75 0 0 1 =
0 1.5h-2a.75.75 0 0 1 0-1.5h.25v-2h-.25a.75.75 0 0 1-.75-.75ZM8 6a1 1 0 1 1=
 0-2 1 1 0 0 1 0 2Z"></path></svg>Note</p><p><strong>Think of it this way:<=
/strong> A normal <code>SELECT</code> gives you the entire result at once (=
like getting a full report). A cursor is like reading that report <strong>l=
ine by line</strong> =E2=80=94 processing each row individually before movi=
ng to the next.</p>
</div>
