<h3 id=3D"sql-cursor-vs-cursor-based-pagination--quick-comparison" data-toc=
=3D"SQL Cursor vs Cursor-Based Pagination =E2=80=94 Quick =E2=80=A6">SQL Cu=
rsor vs Cursor-Based Pagination =E2=80=94 Quick Comparison</h3>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th>SQL Cursor (<code>DECLARE CURSOR</code>)</th>
<th>Cursor-Based Pagination (<code>WHERE id &lt; ?</code>)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>What it is</strong></td>
<td>Database feature for row-by-row iteration</td>
<td>API design pattern for efficient paging</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Where it runs</strong></td>
<td>Inside stored procedures on the DB server</td>
<td>Application code / API layer</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Scope</strong></td>
<td>Server-side only =E2=80=94 never exposed to clients</td>
<td>Client-facing =E2=80=94 cursor token travels in API responses</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Performance</strong></td>
<td>Fine for batch jobs; not for real-time APIs</td>
<td>Designed for low-latency, high-scale APIs</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Use case</strong></td>
<td>Data migration, batch processing</td>
<td>Chat history, feeds, search results, logs</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<blockquote>
<p>=F0=9F=94=97 For the API-side pattern in full =E2=80=94 deferred joins, =
keyset pagination and the
cursor-token contract =E2=80=94 see <a href=3D"#-mysql-pagination--offsetli=
mit-vs-cursor-based-keyset-pagination">=E2=9D=93 MySQL Pagination =E2=80=94=
 OFFSET/LIMIT vs Cursor-Based (Keyset) Pagination</a>.</p>
</blockquote>
