<hr>
<h3 id=3D"2nf--second-normal-form" data-toc=3D"2NF =E2=80=94 Second Normal =
Form">2NF =E2=80=94 Second Normal Form</h3>
<p><strong>Rule:</strong> the table is in <strong>1NF</strong>, and <strong=
>no non-prime attribute is partially dependent on any candidate key</strong=
> (see <a href=3D"#4-partial-functional-dependency">Partial Functional Depe=
ndency</a>). In practice: every non-key column must depend on the <strong>e=
ntire</strong> primary key, not just part of it.</p>
<div class=3D"markdown-alert markdown-alert-note"><p class=3D"markdown-aler=
t-title"><svg class=3D"octicon octicon-info mr-2" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 16" ver=
sion=3D"1.1" width=3D"16" height=3D"16" aria-hidden=3D"true"><path d=3D"M0 =
8a8 8 0 1 1 16 0A8 8 0 0 1 0 8Zm8-6.5a6.5 6.5 0 1 0 0 13 6.5 6.5 0 0 0 0-13=
ZM6.5 7.75A.75.75 0 0 1 7.25 7h1a.75.75 0 0 1 .75.75v2.75h.25a.75.75 0 0 1 =
0 1.5h-2a.75.75 0 0 1 0-1.5h.25v-2h-.25a.75.75 0 0 1-.75-.75ZM8 6a1 1 0 1 1=
 0-2 1 1 0 0 1 0 2Z"></path></svg>Note</p><p>2NF only becomes interesting w=
hen the primary key is <strong>composite</strong>. If the PK is a single co=
lumn, no partial dependency is possible, so a 1NF table with a single-colum=
n PK is automatically in 2NF.</p>
</div>
