<h3 id=3D"3nf--third-normal-form" data-toc=3D"3NF =E2=80=94 Third Normal Fo=
rm">3NF =E2=80=94 Third Normal Form</h3>
<p><strong>Rule:</strong> the table is in <strong>2NF</strong>, and <strong=
>no non-prime attribute is transitively dependent</strong> on the primary k=
ey (see <a href=3D"#5-transitive-functional-dependency">Transitive Function=
al Dependency</a>). Equivalently, for every non-trivial FD <code>X =E2=86=
=92 Y</code>, either <strong>X is a super key</strong> <em>or</em> <strong>=
Y is a prime attribute</strong> (part of some candidate key).</p>
<p>Plain English: <strong>every non-key column must depend on the key, the =
whole key, and nothing but the key.</strong></p>
<h4 id=3D"-violates-3nf">=E2=9D=8C Violates 3NF</h4>
<p><strong><code>students</code></strong></p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th style=3D"text-align:center">roll_no (PK)</th>
<th style=3D"text-align:center">student_name</th>
<th style=3D"text-align:center">dept</th>
<th style=3D"text-align:center">dept_head</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">1</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">Alice</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">CSE</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">Dr. Smith</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">2</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">Bob</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">ECE</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">Dr. Jones</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">3</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">Carol</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">CSE</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">Dr. Smith</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>This is the <a href=3D"#5-transitive-functional-dependency">transitive d=
ependency</a> <code>roll_no =E2=86=92 dept =E2=86=92 dept_head</code> from =
the FD section (same data): <code>dept</code> is not a candidate key, so <c=
ode>dept_head</code> depends on the key only <em>through</em> <code>dept</c=
ode>.</p>
<p><code>dept_head</code> doesn't really describe a <em>student</em> =E2=80=
=94 it describes a <em>department</em>. So <code>Dr. Smith</code> is duplic=
ated for every CSE student.</p>
<p><strong>Anomalies it causes:</strong> the department-level ones =E2=80=
=94 a new CSE head means updating every CSE student row, removing the last =
CSE student loses the fact that CSE's head is Dr. Smith, and a brand-new de=
partment can't be registered until a student joins it.</p>
