<hr>
<h3 id=3D"how-to-normalize--a-practical-checklist" data-toc=3D"How to Norma=
lize =E2=80=94 A Practical Checklist">How to Normalize =E2=80=94 A Practica=
l Checklist</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>List the attributes</strong> and the real-world facts they repr=
esent.</li>
<li><strong>Write down the functional dependencies</strong> (see <a href=3D=
"#functional-dependencies-in-dbms">Functional Dependencies</a>).</li>
<li><strong>Find all candidate keys</strong> using the attribute-closure me=
thod.</li>
<li><strong>1NF</strong> =E2=80=94 is every cell atomic? Any list, array, o=
r <code>col1..colN</code> group?</li>
<li><strong>2NF</strong> =E2=80=94 is the PK composite? Does any non-key co=
lumn depend on only <em>part</em> of it?</li>
<li><strong>3NF</strong> =E2=80=94 does any non-key column depend on anothe=
r <em>non-key</em> column?</li>
<li><strong>BCNF</strong> =E2=80=94 is the LHS of every FD a super key? If =
not, decide whether losing dependency preservation is acceptable.</li>
<li><strong>Verify</strong> each decomposition is <strong>lossless</strong>=
, and note any FD you can no longer enforce with a key.</li>
<li><strong>Measure</strong>, then denormalize only where reads demand it.<=
/li>
</ol>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"quick-fire-qa-1" data-toc=3D"Quick-Fire Q&amp;A">Quick-Fire Q&amp=
;A</h3>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Question</th>
<th>Answer</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>What is normalization?</strong></td>
<td>Decomposing tables to remove redundancy and insert/update/delete anomal=
ies, guided by functional dependencies</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Which normal form do real systems use?</strong></td>
<td><strong>3NF</strong> (sometimes BCNF) for OLTP; deliberately denormaliz=
ed star/snowflake schemas for analytics</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Can a 1NF table with a single-column PK violate 2NF?</strong></=
td>
<td>No =E2=80=94 partial dependencies require a <strong>composite</strong> =
key</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Difference between 2NF and 3NF?</strong></td>
<td>2NF removes <strong>partial</strong> dependencies; 3NF removes <strong>=
transitive</strong> ones</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Is every 3NF table in BCNF?</strong></td>
<td>No. 3NF allows <code>X =E2=86=92 Y</code> where X isn't a super key if =
Y is a prime attribute</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Why not always go to BCNF?</strong></td>
<td>BCNF decomposition may not be <strong>dependency-preserving</strong>, f=
orcing constraints into application code</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>What must every decomposition guarantee?</strong></td>
<td><strong>Lossless join</strong> =E2=80=94 rejoining the parts reproduces=
 the original relation exactly</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Downside of normalization?</strong></td>
<td>More tables =E2=86=92 more joins =E2=86=92 potentially slower reads and=
 more complex queries</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>What is denormalization?</strong></td>
<td>Deliberately adding redundancy (duplicated columns, precomputed aggrega=
tes) to speed up reads</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Higher normal form =E2=87=92 better performance?</strong></td>
<td>No. It improves <strong>integrity</strong>; read performance often <em>=
drops</em> because of extra joins</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
