<hr>
<h3 id=3D"2-consistency--how-its-achieved" data-toc=3D"2. Consistency =E2=
=80=94 How It's Achieved">2. Consistency =E2=80=94 How It's Achieved</h3>
<p>Consistency is a <strong>shared responsibility</strong>: the developer w=
rites correct transaction logic and the DBMS enforces declared constraints =
(PK, FK, UNIQUE, NOT NULL, CHECK, triggers). The enforcement table, an exam=
ple and the check flow are in <a href=3D"#consistency--the-guarantee">Consi=
stency =E2=80=94 The Guarantee</a>, inside <a href=3D"#-what-is-consistency=
-and-integrity-in-dbms">=E2=9D=93 What Is Consistency and Integrity in DBMS=
?</a>.</p>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"3-isolation--how-its-achieved" data-toc=3D"3. Isolation =E2=80=94=
 How It's Achieved">3. Isolation =E2=80=94 How It's Achieved</h3>
<p>Isolation is the most complex ACID property. It is enforced by <strong>c=
oncurrency control</strong>, using three families of mechanisms:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Locking</strong> =E2=80=94 a transaction takes a <strong>shared=
 (S)</strong> lock to read and an <strong>exclusive (X)</strong> lock to wr=
ite. Many readers can share an S-lock; an X-lock blocks everyone else.</li>
<li><strong>MVCC (Multi-Version Concurrency Control)</strong> =E2=80=94 ins=
tead of locking, the database keeps <strong>multiple versions</strong> of e=
ach row. Readers see a <strong>snapshot</strong> =E2=80=94 they never block=
 writers, and writers never block readers. Used by PostgreSQL, MySQL (InnoD=
B), Oracle.</li>
<li><strong>Timestamp ordering</strong> =E2=80=94 each transaction gets a <=
strong>timestamp</strong> when it starts, and operations are allowed/reject=
ed based on timestamp order.</li>
</ul>
