</section><section class=3D"sec"><h2 id=3D"durability-in-databases" data-to=
c=3D"29. Durability in Databases"><span class=3D"sec-n">29.</span> Durabili=
ty in Databases</h2>
<p>Durability is the <strong>D</strong> in ACID. It guarantees that once a =
transaction is <strong>committed</strong>, its changes are <strong>permanen=
t</strong> =E2=80=94 they survive crashes, power failures, and restarts. Th=
e moment the database says &quot;COMMIT successful&quot;, that data will st=
ill be there after a reboot, a crash, or a power cut.</p>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"why-durability-is-hard" data-toc=3D"Why Durability Is Hard">Why D=
urability Is Hard</h3>
<p>The problem is simple: the fastest storage is RAM, but RAM is <strong>vo=
latile</strong> =E2=80=94 pull the power and everything in it vanishes inst=
antly. Disk is <strong>persistent</strong> but slow. A naive database that =
only wrote to RAM would lose all committed data on every crash. A naive dat=
abase that wrote to disk on every single operation would be unacceptably sl=
ow.</p>
<p>Durability is the engineering challenge of making committed data survive=
 on disk <strong>without</strong> making the database unbearably slow.</p>
<h4 id=3D"key-term--dirty-page">Key Term =E2=80=94 Dirty Page</h4>
<p>The database never edits rows directly on disk. It reads data in fixed-s=
ize <strong>pages</strong> (16 KB in InnoDB) into RAM (the <a href=3D"#the-=
buffer-pool--how-data-actually-flows-between-disk-and-memory">buffer pool</=
a>) and changes them there.</p>
<ul>
<li>A <strong>clean page</strong> is a page whose RAM copy is still <strong=
>identical</strong> to the copy on disk.</li>
<li>A <strong>dirty page</strong> is a page that has been <strong>modified =
in RAM but not yet written back to disk</strong>. Disk still holds the old =
version.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> <code>UPDATE accounts SET balance =3D 400 WHER=
E id =3D 7</code> changes the balance from 500 to 400 inside page 42 in RAM=
. Page 42 is now <strong>dirty</strong>: RAM says 400, the data file on dis=
k still says 500. Some time later a background thread writes page 42 to dis=
k (a <strong>flush</strong>), and it becomes <strong>clean</strong> again.<=
/p>
<p><strong>Why durability cares:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Dirty pages live only in RAM, so <strong>a crash loses them</strong>. I=
f a committed change is still sitting in a dirty page, the only thing that =
can bring it back is the log, which is exactly the job of WAL below.</li>
<li>Databases leave pages dirty <strong>on purpose</strong>: writing a 16 K=
B page on every commit would be very slow (see <a href=3D"#-why-send-commit=
-ok-first-and-write-the-data-page-later">why the page is written later</a>)=
.</li>
<li>The more dirty pages at crash time, the more log there is to replay, so=
 restart takes longer. <a href=3D"#mechanism-3-checkpointing">Checkpointing=
</a> keeps that amount bounded by flushing dirty pages steadily in the back=
ground.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>Full clean vs dirty comparison (eviction cost, page lifecycle diagram): =
<a href=3D"#clean-vs-dirty--the-important-pair">Buffer Pool =E2=86=92 Clean=
 vs Dirty</a>.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"mechanism-1-write-ahead-logging-wal--the-core" data-toc=3D"Mechan=
ism 1: Write-Ahead Logging (WAL) =E2=80=94 The Core">Mechanism 1: Write-Ahe=
ad Logging (WAL) =E2=80=94 The Core</h3>
<p>The most important durability mechanism in every serious database (MySQL=
 InnoDB, PostgreSQL, Oracle, SQL Server) is <strong>Write-Ahead Logging (WA=
L)</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>The idea:</strong> Before you modify the actual data page on dis=
k, you first write a description of the change to a <strong>sequential log =
file</strong> (the redo log). Only after that log entry is safely on disk d=
o you acknowledge the commit to the client.</p>
<p>In one line: <strong>the log is the truth; the data files are just a cac=
he of the log that's allowed to lag behind.</strong></p>
