y point), COMMIT OK, page flushed later" title=3D"WAL commit happy path: ch=
ange in RAM, write() to OS cache, fsync() to disk (durability point), COMMI=
T OK, page flushed later" loading=3D"lazy"></figure>
<p>What each step really means:</p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Step</th>
<th>What happens</th>
<th>Why it's there</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>[0]</strong></td>
<td>The row is changed in the <strong>buffer pool</strong> (in-memory copy =
of the page). A redo record (&quot;page 42, offset 100: 500 =E2=86=92 400&q=
uot;) goes into the in-memory <strong>log buffer</strong>.</td>
<td>Reads and writes hit RAM =E2=80=94 fast. Nothing is durable yet.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>[1]</strong></td>
<td><code>write()</code> copies the log buffer into the redo log file. =E2=
=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F This only reaches the <strong>OS page cache</strong>, which=
 is still RAM.</td>
<td>Hands the bytes to the OS.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>[2]</strong></td>
<td><code>fsync()</code> forces the OS to push those bytes onto the physica=
l disk and waits until the disk confirms.</td>
<td><strong>This is the moment the transaction becomes durable.</strong></t=
d>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>[3]</strong></td>
<td>Server replies <code>COMMIT OK</code>.</td>
<td>The promise to the client =E2=80=94 made <em>only</em> after [2].</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>[4]</strong></td>
<td>Much later, the modified 16 KB data page is written to its real place i=
n the data file.</td>
<td>Expensive random write =E2=80=94 done lazily, in batches, off the commi=
t path.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h4 id=3D"-why-send-commit-ok-first-and-write-the-data-page-later">=E2=9D=
=93 Why Send &quot;COMMIT OK&quot; First and Write the Data Page Later?</h4=
>
<p>It looks backwards =E2=80=94 we tell the client &quot;done&quot; <em>bef=
ore</em> the real table file on disk is updated. It's safe, and it's much f=
aster.</p>
<p><strong>1. It's already safe =E2=80=94 the log alone is enough to rebuil=
d the page.</strong>
After [2], the redo log on disk says exactly <em>&quot;page 42, offset 100:=
 change 500 =E2=86=92 400&quot;</em>. If the server crashes before [4], rec=
overy reads that line and redoes the change (crash <strong>D</strong> below=
). Writing the data page at commit would save nothing that isn't already sa=
ved. It would just be a second copy.</p>
<p><strong>2. Writing the log is cheap; writing data pages is expensive.</s=
trong></p>
