 + 1 page write with WAL" loading=3D"lazy"></figure>
<p>Many commits can also share a single <code>fsync()</code> of the log (<s=
trong>group commit</strong>, see <a href=3D"#group-commit--amortizing-the-c=
ost">Mechanism 2</a>), so 50 concurrent commits may cost one disk flush ins=
tead of 50.</p>
<p><strong>4. It's the only way to make multi-page changes all-or-nothing.<=
/strong>
One transaction can change many pages (the row + every index, or two accoun=
ts in a transfer). If you wrote them directly and crashed halfway, some pag=
es would be new and some old: a <strong>corrupted, inconsistent</strong> da=
tabase. With WAL, the log either contains the COMMIT record or it doesn't, =
so the transaction either happened completely or not at all.</p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th>Write data pages at commit</th>
<th>WAL: log at commit, pages later</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Disk I/O per commit</td>
<td>Many <strong>random</strong> 16 KB writes</td>
<td>One small <strong>sequential</strong> append</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Hot page changed 1,000=C3=97</td>
<td>1,000 page writes</td>
<td>1 page write (+ 1,000 tiny log appends)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Crash halfway through commit</td>
<td>Some pages new, some old =E2=86=92 <strong>corrupt</strong></td>
<td>COMMIT record present or not =E2=86=92 <strong>all or nothing</strong><=
/td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Commit latency</td>
<td>High</td>
<td>Low</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Durable after OK?</td>
<td>Yes</td>
<td>Yes, the log is replayed on restart</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>Trade-off:</strong> recovery after a crash takes longer, because=
 the log since the last checkpoint must be replayed. That's why checkpointi=
ng exists (<a href=3D"#mechanism-3-checkpointing">Mechanism 3</a>): it keep=
s the amount of log to replay bounded.</p>
