<th style=3D"text-align:center">Crash</th>
<th>What's on disk at the moment of crash</th>
<th>What recovery does on restart</th>
<th>What the client saw</th>
<th>Data lost?</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style=3D"text-align:center"><strong>A</strong> =E2=80=94 after [0], bef=
ore [1]</td>
<td>Nothing about this transaction (it only existed in RAM)</td>
<td>Nothing to do =E2=80=94 no COMMIT record in the log =E2=87=92 transacti=
on never happened</td>
<td>Error / connection dropped. <strong>No OK.</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 No =E2=80=94 DB never promised anything</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style=3D"text-align:center"><strong>B</strong> =E2=80=94 after <code>wr=
ite()</code>, before <code>fsync()</code> finishes</td>
<td>Maybe nothing, maybe part of the log, maybe all of it</td>
<td>Scans the log; if the COMMIT record is missing or its block fails the <=
strong>checksum</strong> (half-written), it's ignored =E2=87=92 transaction=
 <strong>rolled back</strong></td>
<td>Error / connection dropped. <strong>No OK.</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 No =E2=80=94 never promised. =E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F Outcome can g=
o either way (see note below)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style=3D"text-align:center"><strong>C</strong> =E2=80=94 after <code>fs=
ync()</code>, before client receives OK</td>
<td>Full redo log incl. COMMIT record</td>
<td><strong>REDO</strong> replays it =E2=87=92 transaction <strong>committe=
d</strong></td>
<td>Connection dropped. <strong>No OK!</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 No =E2=80=94 but client <em>doesn't know</em> it succeeded =
=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style=3D"text-align:center"><strong>D</strong> =E2=80=94 after OK, befo=
re data page flush</td>
<td>Redo log =E2=9C=94, data file still has the <strong>old</strong> page</=
td>
<td><strong>REDO</strong> replays the log onto the old page =E2=87=92 page =
becomes new</td>
<td><code>COMMIT OK</code></td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 No =E2=80=94 this is exactly what WAL is for</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style=3D"text-align:center"><strong>E</strong> =E2=80=94 midway through=
 writing the 16 KB data page</td>
<td>Redo log =E2=9C=94, data page is <strong>torn</strong> (half old, half =
new)</td>
<td>Restore a clean copy of the page from the <strong>doublewrite buffer</s=
trong>, then REDO on top</td>
<td><code>COMMIT OK</code></td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 No =E2=80=94 see <a href=3D"#mechanism-4-double-write-buffer-=
-no-torn-pages">Mechanism 4</a></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>The guarantee in one sentence:</strong> <em>if the client got <c=
ode>OK</code>, the change survives (D, E). If the client didn't get <code>O=
K</code>, the DB is allowed to keep it or drop it (A, B, C) =E2=80=94 and t=
he client must cope with not knowing.</em></p>
<p><strong>Note on B =E2=80=94 why &quot;either way&quot;:</strong> if only=
 the <code>mysqld</code> <em>process</em> crashed, the OS is still alive an=
d its page cache will still flush the log to disk =E2=87=92 the commit may =
survive. If the whole machine lost power, the OS cache is gone =E2=87=92 th=
e commit is lost. Both are fine, because no <code>OK</code> was sent.</p>
<h4 id=3D"=EF=B8=8F-the-tricky-one-crash-c--did-my-commit-succeed">=E2=9A=
=A0=EF=B8=8F The Tricky One: Crash C =E2=80=94 &quot;Did My Commit Succeed?=
&quot;</h4>
