<p><strong>Fix (application side):</strong> make writes <strong>idempotent<=
/strong> =E2=80=94 send a unique <code>request_id</code> / idempotency key =
with a <code>UNIQUE</code> constraint, or check whether the change already =
exists before retrying. WAL protects durability; it cannot protect you from=
 a blind retry.</p>
<h4 id=3D"what-if-fsync-itself-returns-an-error-no-crash">What If <code>fsy=
nc()</code> Itself Returns an Error? (No Crash)</h4>
<p>Sometimes the disk reports an I/O error (<code>EIO</code>) instead of th=
e machine crashing. The tempting fix =E2=80=94 &quot;just call <code>fsync(=
)</code> again&quot; =E2=80=94 is <strong>wrong</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>On Linux, after a failed writeback the kernel may <strong>drop the dirt=
y pages and mark them clean</strong>. A second <code>fsync()</code> then re=
turns <em>success</em> even though the data never reached disk.</li>
<li>This was discovered in PostgreSQL in 2018 (nicknamed <strong>&quot;fsyn=
cgate&quot;</strong>).</li>
</ul>
<p>So modern databases treat an <code>fsync()</code> failure as <strong>fat=
al</strong>: PostgreSQL <code>PANIC</code>s and InnoDB aborts the server on=
 purpose. On restart, recovery rebuilds state from the last good WAL. The c=
lient never received <code>OK</code>, so no promise was broken =E2=80=94 th=
is behaves exactly like crash <strong>B</strong>.</p>
<h4 id=3D"the-one-rule-that-makes-all-this-work--write-ahead">The One Rule =
That Makes All This Work =E2=80=94 &quot;Write-AHEAD&quot;</h4>
<div class=3D"markdown-alert markdown-alert-important"><p class=3D"markdown=
-alert-title"><svg class=3D"octicon octicon-report mr-2" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 =
