<h4 id=3D"why-fsync-is-expensive">Why fsync Is Expensive</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Storage</th>
<th>Typical <code>fsync</code> cost</th>
<th>Max durable commits/sec (no batching)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Spinning HDD</td>
<td>~5=E2=80=9310 ms (waits for a platter rotation)</td>
<td>~100=E2=80=93200</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Consumer SSD</td>
<td>~0.5=E2=80=932 ms</td>
<td>~500=E2=80=932,000</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Enterprise NVMe with power-loss protection</td>
<td>~50=E2=80=93200 =C2=B5s</td>
<td>~5,000=E2=80=9320,000</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>It's a <strong>synchronous stall</strong> =E2=80=94 the committing threa=
d can do nothing while it waits. This one call is usually the hard ceiling =
on a database's write throughput.</p>
<h4 id=3D"group-commit--amortizing-the-cost">Group Commit =E2=80=94 Amortiz=
ing the Cost</h4>
