ee separate fsyncs cost 3 ms vs one shared fsync for T1=E2=80=93T3 costing =
1 ms, all durable together" loading=3D"lazy"></figure>
<p>Each transaction still waits for a real <code>fsync</code>, so durabilit=
y is intact =E2=80=94 the <em>cost</em> is just shared. This is why databas=
e write throughput can far exceed &quot;1 =C3=B7 fsync latency&quot;.</p>
<h4 id=3D"the-tuning-knobs-and-what-you-trade-away">The Tuning Knobs (and W=
hat You Trade Away)</h4>
<p><strong>MySQL / InnoDB =E2=80=94 <code>innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit</c=
ode>.</strong> This is the single most important durability knob in MySQL:<=
/p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th style=3D"text-align:center">Value</th>
<th>Behaviour at COMMIT</th>
<th style=3D"text-align:center">Survives process crash?</th>
<th style=3D"text-align:center">Survives OS crash / power loss?</th>
<th>Use Case</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style=3D"text-align:center"><strong>1</strong> (default, ACID)</td>
<td><code>write()</code> + <code>fsync()</code> the log on every commit</td=
>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9C=85</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9C=85 =E2=80=94 <strong>zero</strong> d=
ata loss</td>
<td>Production =E2=80=94 full ACID</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style=3D"text-align:center"><strong>2</strong></td>
<td><code>write()</code> to OS cache only; <code>fsync</code> once per seco=
nd</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9C=85</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9D=8C lose up to ~1 s</td>
<td>Acceptable if OS crash is rare</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style=3D"text-align:center"><strong>0</strong></td>
<td><code>write()</code> + <code>fsync</code> once per second</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9D=8C lose up to ~1 s</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">=E2=9D=8C lose up to ~1 s</td>
<td>Dev/staging only</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>Rule:</strong> Always use <code>1</code> in production for any t=
ransactional or financial system.</p>
<p>Similarly, <code>sync_binlog =3D 1</code> ensures the binary log (used f=
or replication and point-in-time recovery) is also fsynced on every commit.=
 The combination of <code>innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit =3D 1</code> and <=
code>sync_binlog =3D 1</code> is called the <strong>&quot;double-1&quot; co=
nfiguration</strong> =E2=80=94 this is the gold standard for full MySQL dur=
ability.</p>
<p><strong>PostgreSQL:</strong> <code>synchronous_commit =3D on</code> (def=
ault) does the equivalent of value 1; <code>off</code> returns before the f=
lush and can lose recent commits. <code>fsync =3D off</code> disables it en=
tirely and risks <strong>unrecoverable corruption</strong>, not just lost t=
ransactions =E2=80=94 never use it on data you care about.</p>
