<p>On crash recovery, if InnoDB finds a torn page in a data file, it copies=
 the intact version from the doublewrite area and then applies the redo log=
 on top of it. The page is repaired.</p>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"mechanism-5-replication--cross-machine-durability" data-toc=3D"Me=
chanism 5: Replication =E2=80=94 Cross-Machine Durability">Mechanism 5: Rep=
lication =E2=80=94 Cross-Machine Durability</h3>
<p>All the above mechanisms protect a single machine. But what if the disk =
physically fails, or the whole server rack burns?</p>
<p><strong>Replication</strong> keeps copies of the data on other machines.=
 For durability, the question is <strong>when the primary says &quot;COMMIT=
 OK&quot; relative to the replicas</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Asynchronous:</strong> OK immediately, so a primary crash can l=
ose the last few transactions.</li>
<li><strong>Semi-synchronous:</strong> OK after a replica confirms it <em>r=
eceived</em> the change, so no loss if that replica takes over.</li>
<li><strong>Group Replication:</strong> OK after a <em>majority</em> agrees=
, so no loss while a majority survives.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each extra guarantee costs at least one network round trip per commit.</=
p>
