<h3 id=3D"integrity--the-rules" data-toc=3D"Integrity =E2=80=94 The Rules">=
Integrity =E2=80=94 The Rules</h3>
<p><strong>Integrity</strong> means the data in the database is <strong>acc=
urate, valid, and trustworthy</strong>. It's enforced through <strong>integ=
rity constraints</strong> =E2=80=94 rules you declare at schema-design time=
.</p>
<h4 id=3D"the-four-types-of-integrity-constraints">The Four Types of Integr=
ity Constraints</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Type</th>
<th>Rule</th>
<th>Enforced By</th>
<th>Example</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Entity Integrity</strong></td>
<td>Every row must be uniquely identifiable =E2=80=94 the primary key can n=
ever be <code>NULL</code> or duplicated</td>
<td><code>PRIMARY KEY</code></td>
<td><code>emp_id</code> cannot be NULL</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Referential Integrity</strong></td>
<td>A foreign key must match an existing primary key in the parent table, o=
r be <code>NULL</code></td>
<td><code>FOREIGN KEY</code></td>
<td><code>employees.dept_id</code> must exist in <code>departments</code></=
td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Domain Integrity</strong></td>
<td>Every value must belong to the column's allowed set of values</td>
<td>Data type, <code>NOT NULL</code>, <code>CHECK</code>, <code>DEFAULT</co=
de></td>
<td><code>age INT CHECK (age BETWEEN 18 AND 65)</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>User-Defined (Business) Integrity</strong></td>
<td>Custom business rules that don't fit the above</td>
<td><code>CHECK</code>, triggers, application code</td>
<td>&quot;A savings account balance can never go below =E2=82=B9500&quot;</=
td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
