p>The tree hangs &quot;Recoverable / Non-Recoverable&quot; under Non-Serial=
izable to keep the picture simple, but recoverability applies to <strong>ev=
ery</strong> schedule, serializable or not. A schedule your database should=
 actually allow needs <strong>both</strong>: serializable (correct result) =
<strong>and</strong> at least recoverable (safe abort). Real engines aim fo=
r conflict-serializable + strict.</p>
</div>
<h4 id=3D"each-type-in-plain-words">Each Type in Plain Words</h4>
<p><strong>Schedule.</strong> The order in which the reads/writes of severa=
l concurrent transactions actually run. Inside one transaction the order ne=
ver changes; only <em>how the transactions are interleaved</em> differs.</p=
>
<p><strong><a href=3D"#1-serial-schedule">Serial</a></strong> =E2=80=94 one=
 transaction runs completely, then the next (<code>T1: R(A) W(A)</code> =E2=
=86=92 then <code>T2: R(A) W(A)</code>).</p>
<ul>
<li>=E2=9C=85 Always correct: if each transaction is correct alone, running=
 them back-to-back is correct.</li>
<li>=E2=9D=8C No concurrency: everyone waits. For <em>n</em> transactions t=
here are <em>n!</em> possible serial orders, and any of them counts as &quo=
t;correct&quot;.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong><a href=3D"#2-non-serial-schedule">Non-Serial</a></strong> (conc=
urrent / interleaved) =E2=80=94 operations of different transactions are mi=
xed (<code>R1(A) R2(A) W1(A) W2(A)</code>).</p>
<ul>
<li>=E2=9C=85 Fast: one transaction uses the CPU while another waits on dis=
k.</li>
<li>=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F Might be correct, might not. That's what the next le=
vel decides.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong><a href=3D"#3-serializable-schedule">Serializable</a></strong> =
=E2=80=94 interleaved, but its result is <strong>equivalent to some serial =
schedule</strong>. You get the speed of concurrency with the correctness of=
 serial execution. There are two definitions of &quot;equivalent&quot;:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong><a href=3D"#3a-conflict-serializability">Conflict Serializable<=
/a></strong> =E2=80=94 you can turn it into a serial schedule by <strong>sw=
apping adjacent non-conflicting operations</strong> (two operations <em>con=
flict</em> if they're from different transactions, touch the same item, and=
 at least one is a write).
<ul>
<li><strong>How to check:</strong> draw the precedence graph; <strong>no cy=
cle =E2=87=92 conflict serializable</strong>. Fast (polynomial), so this is=
 what databases actually enforce (e.g. via two-phase locking).</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong><a href=3D"#3b-view-serializability">View Serializable</a></str=
ong> =E2=80=94 every transaction <strong>reads the same values</strong> (sa=
me initial reads, same &quot;who read from whom&quot;) and the <strong>same=
 final writes</strong> survive, as in some serial schedule.
<ul>
<li>It's a looser rule: every conflict-serializable schedule is view-serial=
izable, but not the other way round. The extra schedules it allows always c=
ontain a <strong>blind write</strong> (a write with no read of that item be=
fore it).</li>
<li><strong>How to check:</strong> NP-complete in general, so it's a theory=
 concept =E2=80=94 no real DBMS tests for it.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p><strong><a href=3D"#4-non-serializable-schedules">Non-Serializable</a></=
strong> =E2=80=94 not equivalent to <em>any</em> serial order, so it can pr=
oduce results no one-at-a-time run could (lost updates, incorrect sums, etc=
.). Databases prevent these via concurrency control, or allow some of them =
deliberately through weaker <a href=3D"#sql-isolation-levels">isolation lev=
els</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Recoverability classes</strong> (each one is stricter than the o=
ne before; strict =E2=8A=82 cascadeless =E2=8A=82 recoverable):</p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Class</th>
<th>Rule</th>
<th>What goes wrong / right</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong><a href=3D"#4a-non-recoverable-schedule--must-avoid">Non-Recove=
rable</a></strong> =E2=9D=8C</td>
<td>T2 reads T1's <strong>uncommitted</strong> write and <strong>commits be=
fore T1</strong></td>
<td>If T1 then aborts, T2 has already committed a result built on a value t=
hat never existed =E2=80=94 and a commit can't be undone. <strong>Never all=
owed.</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong><a href=3D"#4b-recoverable-schedule-">Recoverable</a></strong> =
=E2=9C=85</td>
<td>If T2 reads from T1, <strong>T1 commits before T2 commits</strong></td>
<td>Aborts can always be handled, but one abort may force others to abort t=
oo (<strong>cascading rollback</strong>).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong><a href=3D"#4d-cascadeless-schedule--no-cascading-aborts">Casca=
deless</a></strong> =E2=9C=85</td>
<td>Transactions read <strong>only committed</strong> data</td>
<td>No cascading rollbacks: an abort only undoes itself.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong><a href=3D"#4e-strict-schedule--strongest">Strict</a></strong> =
=E2=9C=85</td>
<td>No one reads <strong>or overwrites</strong> an item until the transacti=
on that last wrote it has committed/aborted</td>
<td>Undo is trivial (just restore the old value). This is what strict 2PL p=
roduces and what real databases use.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"1-serial-schedule" data-toc=3D"1. Serial Schedule">1. Serial Sche=
dule</h3>
