a.75.75 0 0 1-1.5 0v-2.5a.75.75 0 0 1 1.5 0ZM9 11a1 1 0 1 1-2 0 1 1 0 0 1 2=
 0Z"></path></svg>Warning</p><p><strong>* Lost Update under <code>REPEATABL=
E READ</code> depends on the engine.</strong> The table is the textbook (lo=
ck-based) view. MySQL InnoDB's <code>REPEATABLE READ</code> reads from a sn=
apshot, so a plain <code>SELECT</code> followed by an <code>UPDATE</code> c=
omputed from it can still overwrite another transaction's change, and InnoD=
B does not detect it (PostgreSQL's <code>REPEATABLE READ</code> aborts the =
second writer instead). In MySQL, prevent it with <code>SELECT =E2=80=A6 FO=
R UPDATE</code>, an atomic <code>UPDATE =E2=80=A6 SET x =3D x - 1</code>, o=
r a version column. See <a href=3D"#locking--concurrency-control-in-mysql">=
Locking &amp; Concurrency Control in MySQL</a>.</p>
