<h4 id=3D"setting-isolation-level">Setting Isolation Level</h4>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Set for current session (MySQL syntax=
)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SET</span> SESSION TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL=
 READ COMMITTED;

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Set for the next transaction only</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SET</span> TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALI=
ZABLE;
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">START</span> TRANSACTION;
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- ... operations ...</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">COMMIT</span>;</code></pre></div>
<h4 id=3D"which-level-to-use">Which Level to Use?</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Use Case</th>
<th style=3D"text-align:center">Recommended Level</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Analytics / reporting (read-only, stale data OK)</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">READ COMMITTED</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Most web applications</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">READ COMMITTED or REPEATABLE READ</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Banking / financial transactions</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">SERIALIZABLE</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>High-traffic reads, eventual consistency OK</td>
<td style=3D"text-align:center">READ UNCOMMITTED (rarely)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>Quick guidance:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong><code>READ COMMITTED</code></strong> =E2=80=94 Use when you wan=
t to avoid dirty reads but can tolerate seeing new committed data mid-trans=
action. Common in high-throughput systems where some inconsistency is accep=
table.</li>
<li><strong><code>REPEATABLE READ</code></strong> =E2=80=94 Good for most a=
pplications. Once you read a row, you see the same value throughout your tr=
ansaction, even if others commit changes.</li>
<li><strong><code>SERIALIZABLE</code></strong> =E2=80=94 Use when absolute =
correctness matters more than speed (e.g., financial reconciliation).</li>
<li><strong><code>READ UNCOMMITTED</code></strong> =E2=80=94 Rarely used. O=
nly for rough analytics where speed matters and stale/dirty data is accepta=
ble.</li>
</ul>
