</section><section class=3D"sec"><h2 id=3D"concurrency-control-in-dbms-ski"=
 data-toc=3D"33. Concurrency Control in DBMS SKI"><span class=3D"sec-n">33.=
</span> Concurrency Control in DBMS SKI</h2>
<p>=F0=9F=94=97 <strong>Reference:</strong> <a href=3D"https://www.tutorial=
spoint.com/dbms/dbms_concurrency_control.htm" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noop=
ener">Tutorialspoint =E2=80=94 DBMS Concurrency Control</a></p>
<p>Concurrency control is the mechanism that allows <strong>multiple transa=
ctions to execute simultaneously</strong> while still maintaining the ACID =
properties. Without it, concurrent transactions can corrupt data, produce w=
rong results, and leave the database in an inconsistent state.</p>
<div class=3D"markdown-alert markdown-alert-note"><p class=3D"markdown-aler=
t-title"><svg class=3D"octicon octicon-info mr-2" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 16" ver=
sion=3D"1.1" width=3D"16" height=3D"16" aria-hidden=3D"true"><path d=3D"M0 =
8a8 8 0 1 1 16 0A8 8 0 0 1 0 8Zm8-6.5a6.5 6.5 0 1 0 0 13 6.5 6.5 0 0 0 0-13=
ZM6.5 7.75A.75.75 0 0 1 7.25 7h1a.75.75 0 0 1 .75.75v2.75h.25a.75.75 0 0 1 =
0 1.5h-2a.75.75 0 0 1 0-1.5h.25v-2h-.25a.75.75 0 0 1-.75-.75ZM8 6a1 1 0 1 1=
 0-2 1 1 0 0 1 0 2Z"></path></svg>Note</p><p><strong>Why not just run trans=
actions one at a time?</strong> Serial execution is correct but extremely s=
low. In a banking system handling thousands of transactions per second, ser=
ial execution would mean each user has to wait for ALL previous users to fi=
nish. Concurrency control lets us run transactions in parallel <strong>safe=
ly</strong>.</p>
</div>
<hr>
<p>The five problems concurrency control must prevent (Dirty Read, Lost Upd=
ate, Incorrect Summary, Non-Repeatable Read, Phantom Read) are covered once=
, with examples, in <a href=3D"#concurrency-problems-without-proper-isolati=
on">Concurrency Problems</a> above.</p>
<hr>
