<h4 id=3D"the-4-necessary-conditions">The 4 Necessary Conditions</h4>
<p>A deadlock can occur <strong>only if all four hold simultaneously</stron=
g>. Break any one and deadlock becomes impossible =E2=80=94 that's exactly =
what prevention schemes do.</p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Condition</th>
<th>Meaning</th>
<th>In DBMS terms</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>1. Mutual Exclusion</strong></td>
<td>A resource can be held by only one transaction at a time</td>
<td>An exclusive (X) lock on a row can't be shared</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>2. Hold and Wait</strong></td>
<td>A transaction holding a resource requests another one and waits</td>
<td>T1 holds <code>X-Lock(A)</code> and waits for <code>X-Lock(B)</code></t=
d>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>3. No Preemption</strong></td>
<td>A resource can't be forcibly taken; it must be released voluntarily</td=
>
<td>The DBMS won't yank T1's lock away mid-transaction</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>4. Circular Wait</strong></td>
<td>A closed chain of transactions each waiting on the next</td>
<td>T1 =E2=86=92 T2 =E2=86=92 T3 =E2=86=92 T1</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
