</section><section class=3D"sec"><h2 id=3D"locking--concurrency-control-in-=
mysql" data-toc=3D"34. Locking &amp; Concurrency Control in MySQL"><span cl=
ass=3D"sec-n">34.</span> Locking &amp; Concurrency Control in MySQL</h2>
<p>When multiple transactions try to read and update the same row at the sa=
me time, you can end up with <strong>lost updates</strong>, <strong>dirty r=
eads</strong>, or <strong>inconsistent data</strong>. MySQL provides severa=
l mechanisms to handle this. Below are the main approaches, each illustrate=
d with real-world examples from an <strong>Inventory Management System</str=
ong> and a <strong>Banking Transaction System</strong>.</p>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"1-pessimistic-locking-select--for-update" data-toc=3D"1. Pessimis=
tic Locking (SELECT ... FOR UPDATE)">1. Pessimistic Locking (<code>SELECT .=
.. FOR UPDATE</code>)</h3>
<p><strong>How it works:</strong> You explicitly lock the row(s) when you r=
ead them. Any other transaction that tries to read the same row with <code>=
FOR UPDATE</code> (or tries to update it) will <strong>block and wait</stro=
ng> until the first transaction commits or rolls back. This is called &quot=
;pessimistic&quot; because you assume a conflict <em>will</em> happen, so y=
ou lock preemptively.</p>
<h4 id=3D"inventory-example--purchasing-the-last-item">Inventory Example =
=E2=80=94 Purchasing the Last Item</h4>
<p>Two customers try to buy the last unit of a product at the same time.</p=
>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Transaction A (Customer 1)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">START</span> TRANSACTION;

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Lock the row =E2=80=94 no one else can modi=
fy it until we commit</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> quantity <span class=3D"hljs-key=
word">FROM</span> products <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> produc=
t_id <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">1=
01</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FOR</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keywo=
rd">UPDATE</span>;
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Returns: quantity =3D 1</span>

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Quantity is enough, proceed with purchase</=
span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">UPDATE</span> products <span class=3D"hljs-key=
word">SET</span> quantity <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> quantity=
 <span class=3D"hljs-operator">-</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">1</span=
> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> product_id <span class=3D"hljs-=
operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">101</span>;
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">INSERT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">INT=
O</span> orders (customer_id, product_id, qty) <span class=3D"hljs-keyword"=
>VALUES</span> (<span class=3D"hljs-number">1</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-n=
umber">101</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">1</span>);

<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">COMMIT</span>;

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Transaction B (Customer 2) =E2=80=94 runs c=
oncurrently</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">START</span> TRANSACTION;

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- This BLOCKS here, waiting for Transaction A=
 to finish</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> quantity <span class=3D"hljs-key=
word">FROM</span> products <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> produc=
t_id <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">1=
01</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FOR</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keywo=
rd">UPDATE</span>;
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- After A commits, returns: quantity =3D 0</s=
pan>

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Quantity is 0 =E2=80=94 cannot fulfil, info=
rm customer</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ROLLBACK</span>;</code></pre></div>
<p><strong>What happens without the lock?</strong> Both transactions would =
read <code>quantity =3D 1</code> simultaneously, both would decrement it, a=
nd you'd end up with <code>quantity =3D -1</code> =E2=80=94 selling stock y=
ou don't have.</p>
<h4 id=3D"bank-example--transferring-money-between-accounts">Bank Example =
=E2=80=94 Transferring Money Between Accounts</h4>
<p>Transfer $500 from Account A to Account B.</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">START</span> TRANSACTION;

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Always lock in a consistent order (lower ID=
 first) to avoid deadlocks</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> balance <span class=3D"hljs-keyw=
ord">FROM</span> accounts <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> account=
_id <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">10=
01</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FOR</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keywo=
rd">UPDATE</span>;
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Returns: balance =3D 2000</span>

<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> balance <span class=3D"hljs-keyw=
ord">FROM</span> accounts <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> account=
_id <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">10=
02</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FOR</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keywo=
rd">UPDATE</span>;
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Returns: balance =3D 500</span>

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Check sufficient funds</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- 2000 &gt;=3D 500 =E2=9C=93</span>

<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">UPDATE</span> accounts <span class=3D"hljs-key=
word">SET</span> balance <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> balance <=
span class=3D"hljs-operator">-</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">500</span=
> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> account_id <span class=3D"hljs-=
operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">1001</span>;
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">UPDATE</span> accounts <span class=3D"hljs-key=
word">SET</span> balance <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> balance <=
span class=3D"hljs-operator">+</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">500</span=
> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> account_id <span class=3D"hljs-=
operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">1002</span>;

<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">INSERT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">INT=
O</span> transactions (from_acc, to_acc, amount) <span class=3D"hljs-keywor=
d">VALUES</span> (<span class=3D"hljs-number">1001</span>, <span class=3D"h=
ljs-number">1002</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">500</span>);

<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">COMMIT</span>;</code></pre></div>
<p><strong>Key tip:</strong> When locking multiple rows, always lock them i=
n a <strong>consistent order</strong> (e.g., by ascending <code>account_id<=
/code>). If Transaction A locks account 1001 then 1002, and Transaction B l=
ocks 1002 then 1001, they will <strong>deadlock</strong> =E2=80=94 each wai=
ting for the other to release.</p>
<h5 id=3D"what-are-start-transaction-commit-and-rollback">What are START TR=
ANSACTION, COMMIT, and ROLLBACK?</h5>
<p>Think of a <strong>transaction</strong> as a protective wrapper around a=
 group of SQL statements that says: &quot;Either <strong>all</strong> of th=
ese succeed together, or <strong>none</strong> of them happen.&quot;</p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Command</th>
<th>What it does</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>START TRANSACTION</code></td>
<td>Opens the wrapper. From this point, nothing you do is made permanent ye=
t =E2=80=94 it's all tentative. Other connections <strong>cannot see</stron=
g> your uncommitted changes (depending on isolation level).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>COMMIT</code></td>
<td>Closes the wrapper and <strong>saves everything permanently</strong>. A=
ll the <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code>, <code>DELETE</code> statem=
ents inside the transaction are now final and visible to everyone. Locks ar=
e released.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>ROLLBACK</code></td>
<td>Closes the wrapper and <strong>throws everything away</strong>. The dat=
abase goes back to exactly how it was before <code>START TRANSACTION</code>=
. Locks are released.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>Why does this matter?</strong> In the bank transfer above, you d=
ebit Account A and credit Account B. If MySQL crashes <em>after</em> the de=
bit but <em>before</em> the credit, without a transaction the money simply =
vanishes. With a transaction, either both happen or neither happens =E2=80=
=94 this is the <strong>Atomicity</strong> guarantee in ACID.</p>
<p><strong>What is auto-commit?</strong> By default, MySQL runs in <strong>=
auto-commit</strong> mode =E2=80=94 every single SQL statement is its own m=
ini-transaction that immediately commits. When you explicitly say <code>STA=
RT TRANSACTION</code>, you turn off auto-commit for that session until you =
<code>COMMIT</code> or <code>ROLLBACK</code>.</p>
<p>For <code>SAVEPOINT</code> and step-by-step examples of each command, se=
e <a href=3D"#commit-rollback--savepoint--in-detail">COMMIT, ROLLBACK &amp;=
 SAVEPOINT =E2=80=94 In Detail</a>.</p>
<h5 id=3D"how-to-achieve-this-in-spring-boot--jpa-hibernate">How to Achieve=
 This in Spring Boot + JPA (Hibernate)</h5>
<p>In a Spring application, you almost <strong>never</strong> write <code>S=
TART TRANSACTION</code> or <code>COMMIT</code> manually. Spring's <code>@Tr=
ansactional</code> annotation handles it for you.</p>
<p><strong>1. The <code>@Transactional</code> Annotation (Most Common)</str=
ong></p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">Java</span></div><pre><code class=3D=
"hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-meta">@Service</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">cla=
ss</span> <span class=3D"hljs-title class_">TransferService</span> {

    <span class=3D"hljs-meta">@Autowired</span>
    <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">private</span> AccountRepository accountRe=
pository;

    <span class=3D"hljs-meta">@Autowired</span>
    <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">private</span> TransactionRepository trans=
actionRepository;

    <span class=3D"hljs-meta">@Transactional</span>  <span class=3D"hljs-co=
mment">// =E2=86=90 This is your START TRANSACTION + COMMIT/ROLLBACK</span>
    <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword"=
>void</span> <span class=3D"hljs-title function_">transferMoney</span><span=
 class=3D"hljs-params">(Long fromId, Long toId, BigDecimal amount)</span> {

        <span class=3D"hljs-comment">// Lock rows using FOR UPDATE (via JPA=
 query)</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-type">Account</span> <span class=3D"hljs-variab=
le">from</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> accountRepository.=
findByIdForUpdate(fromId);
        <span class=3D"hljs-type">Account</span> <span class=3D"hljs-variab=
le">to</span>   <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> accountRepository.=
findByIdForUpdate(toId);

        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">if</span> (from.getBalance().compareTo=
(amount) &lt; <span class=3D"hljs-number">0</span>) {
            <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">throw</span> <span class=3D"hljs-k=
eyword">new</span> <span class=3D"hljs-title class_">InsufficientFundsExcep=
tion</span>(<span class=3D"hljs-string">&quot;Not enough balance&quot;</spa=
n>);
            <span class=3D"hljs-comment">// =E2=86=91 Any exception =3D aut=
omatic ROLLBACK, nothing is saved</span>
        }

        from.setBalance(from.getBalance().subtract(amount));
        to.setBalance(to.getBalance().add(amount));

        accountRepository.save(from);
        accountRepository.save(to);

        transactionRepository.save(<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">new</span> =
<span class=3D"hljs-title class_">BankTransaction</span>(fromId, toId, amou=
nt));

        <span class=3D"hljs-comment">// =E2=86=90 Method ends normally =3D =
automatic COMMIT</span>
    }
}</code></pre></div>
<p><strong>What Spring does behind the scenes:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Before <code>transferMoney()</code> runs =E2=86=92 Spring calls <code>S=
TART TRANSACTION</code></li>
<li>Your method executes all the JPA operations</li>
<li>Method returns normally =E2=86=92 Spring calls <code>COMMIT</code></li>
<li>Method throws a runtime exception =E2=86=92 Spring calls <code>ROLLBACK=
</code></li>
</ol>
<p><strong>2. The Repository =E2=80=94 <code>FOR UPDATE</code> in JPA</stro=
ng></p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">Java</span></div><pre><code class=3D=
"hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-meta">@Repository</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">int=
erface</span> <span class=3D"hljs-title class_">AccountRepository</span> <s=
pan class=3D"hljs-keyword">extends</span> <span class=3D"hljs-title class_"=
>JpaRepository</span>&lt;Account, Long&gt; {

    <span class=3D"hljs-comment">// The PESSIMISTIC_WRITE lock =3D SELECT .=
.. FOR UPDATE</span>
    <span class=3D"hljs-meta">@Lock(LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE)</span>
    <span class=3D"hljs-meta">@Query(&quot;SELECT a FROM Account a WHERE a.=
id =3D :id&quot;)</span>
    Account <span class=3D"hljs-title function_">findByIdForUpdate</span><s=
pan class=3D"hljs-params">(<span class=3D"hljs-meta">@Param(&quot;id&quot;)=
</span> Long id)</span>;

    <span class=3D"hljs-comment">// For OPTIMISTIC locking, use:</span>
    <span class=3D"hljs-comment">// @Lock(LockModeType.OPTIMISTIC)</span>
}</code></pre></div>
<p><strong>3. The Entity</strong></p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">Java</span></div><pre><code class=3D=
"hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-meta">@Entity</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-meta">@Table(name =3D &quot;accounts&quot;)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">cla=
ss</span> <span class=3D"hljs-title class_">Account</span> {

    <span class=3D"hljs-meta">@Id</span>
    <span class=3D"hljs-meta">@GeneratedValue(strategy =3D GenerationType.I=
DENTITY)</span>
    <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">private</span> Long id;

    <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">private</span> BigDecimal balance;

    <span class=3D"hljs-meta">@Version</span>  <span class=3D"hljs-comment"=
>// =E2=86=90 For optimistic locking (auto-managed version column)</span>
    <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">private</span> Integer version;

    <span class=3D"hljs-comment">// getters and setters</span>
}</code></pre></div>
<p><strong>4. Key <code>@Transactional</code> Options</strong></p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">Java</span></div><pre><code class=3D=
"hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">// Read-only transaction (optimizes per=
formance, no dirty checking)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-meta">@Transactional(readOnly =3D true)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">public</span> Account <span class=3D"hljs-titl=
e function_">getAccount</span><span class=3D"hljs-params">(Long id)</span> =
{ ... }

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">// Custom isolation level</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-meta">@Transactional(isolation =3D Isolation.SERIALIZAB=
LE)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">voi=
d</span> <span class=3D"hljs-title function_">criticalOperation</span><span=
 class=3D"hljs-params">()</span> { ... }

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">// Custom timeout (seconds) =E2=80=94 auto-rol=
lback if exceeded</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-meta">@Transactional(timeout =3D 5)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">voi=
d</span> <span class=3D"hljs-title function_">timeSensitiveOperation</span>=
<span class=3D"hljs-params">()</span> { ... }

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">// Specify which exceptions trigger rollback</=
span>
<span class=3D"hljs-meta">@Transactional(rollbackFor =3D Exception.class)</=
span>        <span class=3D"hljs-comment">// rollback on ALL exceptions</sp=
an>
<span class=3D"hljs-meta">@Transactional(noRollbackFor =3D MailException.cl=
ass)</span>   <span class=3D"hljs-comment">// don&#x27;t rollback for this =
one</span></code></pre></div>
<p><strong>5. JPA Lock Modes Mapped to MySQL</strong></p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>JPA Lock Mode</th>
<th>MySQL Equivalent</th>
<th>When to Use</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>PESSIMISTIC_WRITE</code></td>
<td><code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</code></td>
<td>You will modify the row =E2=80=94 block everyone else</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>PESSIMISTIC_READ</code></td>
<td><code>SELECT ... LOCK IN SHARE MODE</code></td>
<td>You need to ensure the row doesn't change, but you won't modify it</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>OPTIMISTIC</code></td>
<td>No SQL lock =E2=80=94 checks <code>@Version</code> on flush</td>
<td>Low contention, retry-friendly scenarios</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>OPTIMISTIC_FORCE_INCREMENT</code></td>
<td>No SQL lock =E2=80=94 increments <code>@Version</code> even on read</td=
>
<td>Force a version bump to signal &quot;this was touched&quot;</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h5 id=3D"common-mistakes-to-avoid-in-spring">Common Mistakes to Avoid in S=
pring</h5>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Mistake</th>
<th>Why It Fails</th>
<th>Fix</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Calling a <code>@Transactional</code> method from <strong>within the sa=
me class</strong></td>
<td>Spring proxies don't intercept self-calls =E2=80=94 no transaction is s=
tarted</td>
<td>Extract the method to a separate <code>@Service</code> class</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Catching exceptions inside the method silently</td>
<td>Spring never sees the exception, so it <strong>commits</strong> instead=
 of rolling back</td>
<td>Let exceptions propagate, or call <code>TransactionAspectSupport.curren=
tTransactionStatus().setRollbackOnly()</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Using <code>@Transactional</code> on a <strong>private</strong> method<=
/td>
<td>Spring AOP proxies can't intercept private methods</td>
<td>Make the method <code>public</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Long-running logic inside a transaction</td>
<td>Holds locks and DB connections for too long</td>
<td>Keep transactions short =E2=80=94 do non-DB work (API calls, file I/O) =
outside the <code>@Transactional</code> method</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>Pros:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Guarantees no conflicting updates =E2=80=94 strongest safety</li>
<li>Simple mental model: lock, read, write, commit</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Cons:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Blocks other transactions =E2=80=94 reduces throughput under high concu=
rrency</li>
<li>Risk of <strong>deadlocks</strong> if lock ordering is not consistent</=
li>
<li>Holding locks for long transactions hurts performance</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"2-optimistic-locking-application-level-version-check" data-toc=3D=
"2. Optimistic Locking (Application-Level Version =E2=80=A6">2. Optimistic =
Locking (Application-Level Version Check)</h3>
<p><strong>How it works:</strong> You do <strong>not</strong> lock the row =
when reading. Instead, you include a <code>version</code> (or <code>updated=
_at</code> timestamp) column. When updating, you check that the version has=
n't changed since you read it. If it has, someone else modified the row fir=
st =E2=80=94 your update affects 0 rows, and you know to retry. This is cal=
led &quot;optimistic&quot; because you assume conflicts are <strong>rare</s=
trong>.</p>
<p>This is the application-level form of <a href=3D"#4-optimistic-concurren=
cy-control-validation-based">Optimistic Concurrency Control</a>.</p>
<h4 id=3D"inventory-example--two-warehouse-staff-updating-stock">Inventory =
Example =E2=80=94 Two Warehouse Staff Updating Stock</h4>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Table structure</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- products (product_id, name, quantity, versi=
on)</span>

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Staff Member A reads the product</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> quantity, version <span class=3D=
"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> products <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</spa=
n> product_id <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-=
number">101</span>;
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Returns: quantity =3D 50, version =3D 3</sp=
an>

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Staff Member B also reads it (at the same t=
ime)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> quantity, version <span class=3D=
"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> products <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</spa=
n> product_id <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-=
number">101</span>;
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Returns: quantity =3D 50, version =3D 3</sp=
an>

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Staff A updates (adding 20 units from a shi=
pment)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">UPDATE</span> products
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SET</span> quantity <span class=3D"hljs-operat=
