<p><strong>Pros:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>No blocking =E2=80=94 great throughput under low-to-moderate contention=
</li>
<li>No deadlocks</li>
<li>Works well in distributed systems and APIs (version can travel in HTTP =
ETags)</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Cons:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Requires a <code>version</code> or <code>updated_at</code> column on ev=
ery table</li>
<li>Under <strong>high contention</strong>, many retries can happen =E2=80=
=94 worse performance than just locking</li>
<li>Application must handle the retry logic</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"3-atomic-updates-no-explicit-lock-needed" data-toc=3D"3. Atomic U=
pdates (No Explicit Lock Needed)">3. Atomic Updates (No Explicit Lock Neede=
d)</h3>
<p><strong>How it works:</strong> Instead of reading a value into the appli=
cation and then writing back a new value, you do the math <strong>directly =
in the SQL statement</strong>. Because a single <code>UPDATE</code> stateme=
nt is atomic in InnoDB, MySQL internally acquires a row lock for the durati=
on of that single statement, preventing a race condition =E2=80=94 and you =
don't need to manage locks yourself.</p>
<h4 id=3D"inventory-example--decrement-stock-on-purchase">Inventory Example=
 =E2=80=94 Decrement Stock on Purchase</h4>
