<li>Inherently safe against lost updates</li>
<li>Great performance</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Cons:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Only works for simple operations (increment, decrement, set to computed=
 value)</li>
<li>Cannot handle complex business logic that requires reading multiple col=
umns or rows before deciding what to write</li>
<li>You don't get the &quot;before&quot; value unless you use a follow-up <=
code>SELECT</code> or <code>RETURNING</code> (MySQL 8.1+)</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"4-lock-in-share-mode-shared--read-lock" data-toc=3D"4. LOCK IN SH=
ARE MODE (Shared / Read Lock)">4. <code>LOCK IN SHARE MODE</code> (Shared /=
 Read Lock)</h3>
<p><strong>How it works:</strong> Similar to <code>FOR UPDATE</code>, but i=
nstead of an exclusive lock, it places a <strong>shared lock</strong>. Mult=
iple transactions can hold a shared lock on the same row simultaneously (so=
 concurrent reads are fine), but no transaction can <strong>write</strong> =
to that row until all shared locks are released.</p>
<p>For how shared and exclusive locks interact, see the <a href=3D"#lock-co=
mpatibility-matrix">Lock Compatibility Matrix</a> under Concurrency Control=
.</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Use case: Validate that a referenced =
row exists and won&#x27;t be deleted</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- while you insert a child record</span>

<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">START</span> TRANSACTION;

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Shared lock =E2=80=94 others can also read,=
 but nobody can delete/update this row</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> accounts <span class=3D"hlj=
s-keyword">WHERE</span> account_id <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span>=
 <span class=3D"hljs-number">1001</span> LOCK <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">=
IN</span> SHARE MODE;

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Safe to insert a transaction referencing th=
is account</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">INSERT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">INT=
O</span> transactions (account_id, amount, type) <span class=3D"hljs-keywor=
d">VALUES</span> (<span class=3D"hljs-number">1001</span>, <span class=3D"h=
ljs-number">200</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;DEPOSIT&#x27;</sp=
an>);

<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">COMMIT</span>;</code></pre></div>
<p><strong>When to use:</strong> When you need to ensure a row <strong>exis=
ts and stays unchanged</strong> while you do related work, but you don't ne=
ed to modify that row yourself. It's lighter than <code>FOR UPDATE</code> b=
ecause it doesn't block other readers.</p>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"5-isolation-levels" data-toc=3D"5. Isolation Levels">5. Isolation=
 Levels</h3>
<p>MySQL's InnoDB engine supports all four standard isolation levels, which=
 control how much transactions &quot;see&quot; each other's uncommitted wor=
k. Choosing the right level is another way to manage concurrency. The level=
-vs-anomaly table, the <code>SET ... ISOLATION LEVEL</code> syntax and guid=
ance on which level to pick are in <a href=3D"#sql-isolation-levels">SQL Is=
olation Levels</a>. What is specific to InnoDB:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Default level is <code>REPEATABLE READ</code></strong> =E2=80=
=94 good for most applications.</li>
<li><strong>Phantoms under <code>REPEATABLE READ</code>:</strong> InnoDB's =
<code>REPEATABLE READ</code> uses gap locks to prevent most phantom reads, =
making it safer than the SQL standard requires.</li>
<li><strong><code>SERIALIZABLE</code>:</strong> every <code>SELECT</code> a=
utomatically behaves like <code>LOCK IN SHARE MODE</code> (section 4 above)=
.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"which-approach-should-you-pick" data-toc=3D"Which Approach Should=
 You Pick?">Which Approach Should You Pick?</h3>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Scenario</th>
<th>Recommended Approach</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Simple increment/decrement (stock count, balance)</td>
<td><strong>Atomic Update</strong> =E2=80=94 fastest and simplest</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Low-contention reads with occasional conflicts</td>
<td><strong>Optimistic Locking</strong> =E2=80=94 no blocking, retry on con=
flict</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>High-contention critical operations (payments, bookings)</td>
<td><strong>Pessimistic Locking</strong> (<code>FOR UPDATE</code>) =E2=80=
=94 guaranteed safety</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Validate a parent row exists before inserting child</td>
<td><strong><code>LOCK IN SHARE MODE</code></strong> =E2=80=94 lightweight =
read lock</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Entire transaction must see a perfectly consistent snapshot</td>
<td><strong><code>SERIALIZABLE</code> isolation level</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Mixed workload in a typical web app</td>
<td><strong><code>REPEATABLE READ</code></strong> (default) + <strong>atomi=
c updates</strong> where possible</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h4 id=3D"general-rule-of-thumb">General Rule of Thumb</h4>
<div class=3D"markdown-alert markdown-alert-note"><p class=3D"markdown-aler=
t-title"><svg class=3D"octicon octicon-info mr-2" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 16" ver=
sion=3D"1.1" width=3D"16" height=3D"16" aria-hidden=3D"true"><path d=3D"M0 =
8a8 8 0 1 1 16 0A8 8 0 0 1 0 8Zm8-6.5a6.5 6.5 0 1 0 0 13 6.5 6.5 0 0 0 0-13=
ZM6.5 7.75A.75.75 0 0 1 7.25 7h1a.75.75 0 0 1 .75.75v2.75h.25a.75.75 0 0 1 =
0 1.5h-2a.75.75 0 0 1 0-1.5h.25v-2h-.25a.75.75 0 0 1-.75-.75ZM8 6a1 1 0 1 1=
 0-2 1 1 0 0 1 0 2Z"></path></svg>Note</p><p>Start with <strong>atomic upda=
tes</strong> for simple operations. If the logic is too complex for a singl=
e statement, try <strong>optimistic locking</strong> first. Fall back to <s=
trong>pessimistic locking</strong> (<code>FOR UPDATE</code>) only when cont=
ention is high and retries are too costly. Use <strong>isolation levels</st=
rong> as a global safety net underneath all of these.</p>
</div>
