a.75.75 0 0 1-.75-.75Z"></path></svg>Tip</p><p><strong>Two meanings of &quo=
t;precompiler&quot; =E2=80=94 know both for interviews:</strong></p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Meaning</th>
<th>What It Refers To</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Classic DBMS textbook</strong></td>
<td>An <strong>embedded-SQL precompiler</strong> (Pro*C, SQLJ, ESQL/C). You=
 write <code>EXEC SQL SELECT ...</code> inside C/COBOL; the precompiler run=
s <em>before</em> the C compiler, replacing those blocks with database API =
calls. Largely historical =E2=80=94 modern apps use JDBC/ODBC/ORMs instead.=
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>MySQL practical</strong></td>
<td>The <strong>parse + preprocess phase</strong>, and specifically <strong=
>prepared statements</strong> =E2=80=94 where MySQL parses and plans a stat=
ement once, then executes it many times with different values.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
</div>
<h4 id=3D"prepared-statements--precompile-once-execute-many">Prepared State=
ments =E2=80=94 &quot;Precompile Once, Execute Many&quot;</h4>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- 1. PREPARE: parse + preprocess + plan=
 ONCE, store as a handle</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">PREPARE</span> stmt <span class=3D"hljs-keywor=
d">FROM</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;SELECT id, name FROM users=
 WHERE country =3D ? AND age &gt; ?&#x27;</span>;

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- 2. EXECUTE: reuse the plan, only the values=
 change</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SET</span> <span class=3D"hljs-variable">@c</s=
pan> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&=
#x27;IN&#x27;</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-variable">@a</span> <span class=
=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">25</span>;
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">EXECUTE</span> stmt <span class=3D"hljs-keywor=
d">USING</span> <span class=3D"hljs-variable">@c</span>, <span class=3D"hlj=
s-variable">@a</span>;

<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SET</span> <span class=3D"hljs-variable">@c</s=
pan> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&=
#x27;US&#x27;</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-variable">@a</span> <span class=
=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">40</span>;
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">EXECUTE</span> stmt <span class=3D"hljs-keywor=
d">USING</span> <span class=3D"hljs-variable">@c</span>, <span class=3D"hlj=
s-variable">@a</span>;      <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- no re-parsing</=
span>

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- 3. Clean up</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">DEALLOCATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword"=
>PREPARE</span> stmt;</code></pre></div>
<p>In application code you almost never write <code>PREPARE</code> by hand =
=E2=80=94 the driver does it:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">Java</span></div><pre><code class=3D=
"hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">// JDBC =E2=80=94 this IS a prepared st=
atement</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-type">PreparedStatement</span> <span class=3D"hljs-vari=
able">ps</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> conn.prepareStatem=
ent(
    <span class=3D"hljs-string">&quot;SELECT id, name FROM users WHERE coun=
try =3D ? AND age &gt; ?&quot;</span>);
ps.setString(<span class=3D"hljs-number">1</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-stri=
ng">&quot;IN&quot;</span>);
ps.setInt(<span class=3D"hljs-number">2</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number"=
>25</span>);
<span class=3D"hljs-type">ResultSet</span> <span class=3D"hljs-variable">rs=
</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> ps.executeQuery();</code><=
/pre></div>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Benefit</th>
<th>Why It Matters</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>SQL injection immunity</strong></td>
<td>Values travel <strong>separately</strong> from the SQL text =E2=80=94 t=
hey can never be parsed as SQL. This is the #1 reason to use them. See <a h=
ref=3D"#sql-injection">SQL Injection</a>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Skip re-parsing</strong></td>
<td>Parse + preprocess happen once per handle instead of once per execution=
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Smaller network payload</strong></td>
<td>After the first call, only the parameter values go over the wire</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Type safety</strong></td>
<td>The driver sends typed values instead of stringified SQL literals</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>Gotchas:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>A prepared statement is <strong>scoped to one connection</strong>. Conn=
ection pools re-prepare per physical connection.</li>
<li>MySQL's Connector/J defaults to <strong>client-side</strong> prepared s=
tatements. Set <code>useServerPrepStmts=3Dtrue</code> to get real server-si=
de <code>PREPARE</code>.</li>
<li>The plan is chosen at <code>PREPARE</code> time on some paths, so a pla=
n that suits <code>country =3D 'IN'</code> (500M rows) may be reused for <c=
ode>country =3D 'MC'</code> (200 rows). This is the classic <strong>paramet=
er-sniffing</strong> trade-off.</li>
<li>MySQL 8.0 <strong>removed the query cache</strong> entirely =E2=80=94 p=
repared statements save <em>parsing</em>, not <em>result</em> reuse.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"stage-4--the-cost-based-optimizer" data-toc=3D"Stage 4 =E2=80=94 =
The Cost-Based Optimizer">Stage 4 =E2=80=94 The Cost-Based Optimizer</h3>
<p>The optimizer's job: given one SQL statement, choose the <strong>cheapes=
t execution plan</strong> among many equivalent ones. MySQL's optimizer is =
<strong>cost-based</strong>, not rule-based =E2=80=94 it assigns a number t=
o each candidate plan and picks the smallest.</p>
<h4 id=3D"what-the-optimizer-actually-decides">What the Optimizer Actually =
Decides</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Decision</th>
<th>Options It Weighs</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Access method</strong> per table</td>
<td>Full table scan / index scan / index range scan / index lookup / coveri=
ng index</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Which index</strong> to use</td>
<td>Any usable index =E2=80=94 or none, if a scan looks cheaper</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Join order</strong></td>
<td>For <code>A JOIN B JOIN C</code>, which table is scanned first (<code>A=
=E2=86=92B=E2=86=92C</code>? <code>C=E2=86=92A=E2=86=92B</code>?)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Join algorithm</strong></td>
<td>Nested-Loop, Block Nested-Loop, or <strong>Hash Join</strong> (8.0.18+)=
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Subquery strategy</strong></td>
<td>Rewrite to a join / semi-join, or materialize into a temp table</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Sort strategy</strong></td>
<td>Read in index order (free) vs an explicit <code>filesort</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Aggregation strategy</strong></td>
<td>Loose index scan vs temporary table</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h4 id=3D"how-cost-is-estimated">How Cost Is Estimated</h4>
<p>Cost is computed from <strong>statistics</strong>, not from your data it=
self:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">text</span></div><pre><code class=3D=
"hljs">plan cost  =E2=89=88  (rows examined =C3=97 cpu_cost)  +  (pages rea=
d =C3=97 io_cost)</code></pre></div>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Source</th>
<th>What It Provides</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Index cardinality</strong></td>
<td>Estimated number of distinct values per index =E2=80=94 drives selectiv=
ity guesses</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>ANALYZE TABLE t;</code></td>
<td>Recomputes those statistics. Run it after bulk loads/deletes.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Histograms</strong> (8.0+)</td>
<td><code>ANALYZE TABLE t UPDATE HISTOGRAM ON col;</code> =E2=80=94 real va=
lue distributions for <strong>non-indexed</strong> columns; fixes skew mise=
stimates</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>mysql.server_cost</code> / <code>mysql.engine_cost</code></td>
<td>Tunable cost constants (e.g. <code>io_block_read_cost</code>, <code>mem=
ory_block_read_cost</code>)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Look at what the optimizer thinks it =
knows</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SHOW</span> INDEX <span class=3D"hljs-keyword"=
>FROM</span> orders;              <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Cardinali=
ty column</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> information_schema.STATISTI=
CS <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> TABLE_NAME <span class=3D"hljs=
-operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;orders&#x27;</span>=
;</code></pre></div>
<div class=3D"markdown-alert markdown-alert-warning"><p class=3D"markdown-a=
lert-title"><svg class=3D"octicon octicon-alert mr-2" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 16"=
 version=3D"1.1" width=3D"16" height=3D"16" aria-hidden=3D"true"><path d=3D=
"M6.457 1.047c.659-1.234 2.427-1.234 3.086 0l6.082 11.378A1.75 1.75 0 0 1 1=
4.082 15H1.918a1.75 1.75 0 0 1-1.543-2.575Zm1.763.707a.25.25 0 0 0-.44 0L1.=
698 13.132a.25.25 0 0 0 .22.368h12.164a.25.25 0 0 0 .22-.368Zm.53 3.996v2.5=
a.75.75 0 0 1-1.5 0v-2.5a.75.75 0 0 1 1.5 0ZM9 11a1 1 0 1 1-2 0 1 1 0 0 1 2=
 0Z"></path></svg>Warning</p><p>Cardinality in InnoDB is <strong>sampled an=
d approximate</strong>, not exact. That's why a stale-statistics table can =
suddenly get a terrible plan after a big data change =E2=80=94 and why <cod=
e>ANALYZE TABLE</code> sometimes &quot;magically fixes&quot; a slow query.<=
/p>
</div>
<h4 id=3D"optimizer-rewrites-transformations">Optimizer Rewrites (Transform=
ations)</h4>
<p>Before costing, the optimizer rewrites your query into an equivalent-but=
-friendlier form:</p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Transformation</th>
<th>Example</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Constant folding</strong></td>
<td><code>WHERE price &gt; 10 * 5</code> =E2=86=92 <code>WHERE price &gt; 5=
0</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Constant propagation</strong></td>
<td><code>WHERE a =3D 5 AND b =3D a</code> =E2=86=92 <code>WHERE a =3D 5 AN=
D b =3D 5</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Impossible-WHERE detection</strong></td>
<td><code>WHERE 1 =3D 0</code> =E2=86=92 plan becomes &quot;Impossible WHER=
E&quot;, table never read</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Subquery =E2=86=92 semi-join</strong></td>
<td><code>WHERE id IN (SELECT user_id FROM orders)</code> =E2=86=92 a semi-=
join</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Derived table merging</strong></td>
<td><code>FROM (SELECT ...) d</code> merged into the outer query when possi=
ble</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Index Condition Pushdown (ICP)</strong></td>
<td>Pushes <code>WHERE</code> filters down into the storage engine so non-m=
atching rows are discarded <em>before</em> being read into the server</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Multi-Range Read (MRR)</strong></td>
<td>Sorts row-IDs from an index before fetching, converting random I/O into=
 sequential I/O</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong><code>ORDER BY</code> elimination</strong></td>
<td>If an index already returns rows in the requested order, skip the sort =
entirely</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h4 id=3D"seeing-the-plan">Seeing the Plan</h4>
<p><code>EXPLAIN</code> shows the plan this optimizer picked; <code>EXPLAIN=
 ANALYZE</code> runs it and adds actual rows and time. When <strong>estimat=
ed</strong> and <strong>actual</strong> rows differ by orders of magnitude,=
 the optimizer costed the plan with bad statistics, which is the usual root=
 cause of a bad plan. Sample tables, outputs and a step-by-step reading gui=
de are in <a href=3D"#explain--reading-query-execution-plans">EXPLAIN =E2=
=80=94 Reading Query Execution Plans</a>.</p>
<h4 id=3D"watching-the-optimizer-think--optimizer_trace">Watching the Optim=
izer Think =E2=80=94 <code>optimizer_trace</code></h4>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SET</span> optimizer_trace <span class=
=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;enabled=3Do=
n&#x27;</span>;
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> ... ;                          <=
span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- run the query</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> information_schema.OPTIMIZE=
R_TRACE\G
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SET</span> optimizer_trace <span class=3D"hljs=
-operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;enabled=3Doff&#x27;=
</span>;</code></pre></div>
<p>The trace is a JSON document listing every plan considered, the cost ass=
igned to each, and <strong>why</strong> the rejected ones lost. This is the=
 definitive answer to &quot;why isn't it using my index?&quot;.</p>
<h4 id=3D"overriding-the-optimizer">Overriding the Optimizer</h4>
<p>Use these as a <strong>last resort</strong> =E2=80=94 a hint you add tod=
ay becomes a wrong hint after the data grows.</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Index hints (older syntax, applies to=
 one table)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> orders USE INDEX (idx_creat=
ed)    <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> ...;
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> orders FORCE INDEX (idx_cre=
ated)  <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> ...;   <span class=3D"hljs=
-comment">-- &quot;scan is basically never allowed&quot;</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> orders IGNORE INDEX (idx_st=
atus)  <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> ...;

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Optimizer hints (8.0+, comment syntax, more=
 granular)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-comment">/*+=
 JOIN_ORDER(o, c) */</span>        ... <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</s=
pan> orders o <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">JOIN</span> customers c ...;
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-comment">/*+=
 NO_INDEX(orders idx_status) */</span> ...;
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-comment">/*+=
 MAX_EXECUTION_TIME(1000) */</span> ...;   <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- =
kill after 1s</span>

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Toggle whole optimizer features for a sessi=
on</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SET</span> SESSION optimizer_switch <span clas=
s=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;index_cond=
ition_pushdown=3Doff&#x27;</span>;</code></pre></div>
<h4 id=3D"-why-doesnt-mysql-use-my-index">=E2=9D=93 Why Doesn't MySQL Use M=
y Index?</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Cause</th>
<th>Fix</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Low selectivity</strong> =E2=80=94 the index matches too large =
a fraction of rows</td>
<td>Nothing to fix; a scan genuinely is cheaper. See <a href=3D"#cardinalit=
y--selectivity--how-duplicate-values-affect-an-index">Cardinality &amp; Sel=
ectivity</a>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Stale statistics</strong></td>
<td><code>ANALYZE TABLE t;</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Data skew</strong> =E2=80=94 <code>status=3D'active'</code> is =
99% of rows but average cardinality says otherwise</td>
<td>Add a histogram: <code>ANALYZE TABLE t UPDATE HISTOGRAM ON status;</cod=
e></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Function wrapping the column</strong> =E2=80=94 <code>WHERE YEA=
R(created_at) =3D 2024</code></td>
<td>Rewrite as a range: <code>WHERE created_at &gt;=3D '2024-01-01' AND cre=
ated_at &lt; '2025-01-01'</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Type mismatch</strong> =E2=80=94 <code>WHERE varchar_col =3D 12=
3</code></td>
<td>Compare like types; an implicit cast disables the index</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Leading-column violation</strong> on a composite index</td>
<td>See <a href=3D"#leftmost-prefix-rule">Leftmost-Prefix Rule</a></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Leading wildcard</strong> =E2=80=94 <code>LIKE '%abc'</code></t=
d>
<td>Only <code>LIKE 'abc%'</code> can use a B+ tree; consider <code>FULLTEX=
T</code></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"quick-fire-qa-2" data-toc=3D"Quick-Fire Q&amp;A">Quick-Fire Q&amp=
;A</h3>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Question</th>
<th>Answer</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Which stage catches a typo in a keyword?</td>
<td>The <strong>parser</strong> (syntax)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Which stage catches a typo in a column name?</td>
<td>The <strong>preprocessor</strong> (semantic)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Is MySQL's optimizer rule-based or cost-based?</td>
<td><strong>Cost-based</strong> =E2=80=94 it prices candidate plans and pic=
ks the cheapest</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>What does <code>PREPARE</code> actually save?</td>
<td>Re-parsing and re-planning =E2=80=94 <strong>not</strong> result cachin=
g (the query cache is gone in 8.0)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Why are prepared statements the fix for SQL injection?</td>
<td>Values are sent <strong>separately</strong> from the SQL text, so they =
can never be parsed as SQL</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>How do you see estimated vs actual row counts?</td>
<td><code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> (MySQL 8.0.18+)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>How do you find out <em>why</em> an index was rejected?</td>
<td><code>optimizer_trace</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>First thing to try when a good plan suddenly goes bad?</td>
<td><code>ANALYZE TABLE</code> =E2=80=94 stale statistics are the usual cul=
prit</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>=F0=9F=94=97 Next: <a href=3D"#how-to-optimize-a-sql-query">How to Optim=
ize a SQL Query</a> turns these mechanics into a practical checklist.</p>
