's expensive, group commit and the durability tuning knobs =E2=80=94 is cov=
ered once in <a href=3D"#mechanism-2-fsync--actually-getting-to-disk">Mecha=
nism 2: <code>fsync()</code> =E2=80=94 Actually Getting to Disk</a>.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"why-we-need-it--the-latency-gap" data-toc=3D"Why We Need It =E2=
=80=94 The Latency Gap">Why We Need It =E2=80=94 The Latency Gap</h3>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Operation</th>
<th>Typical latency</th>
<th>Relative</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Read from RAM (buffer pool hit)</td>
<td>~100 ns</td>
<td><strong>1=C3=97</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Read from NVMe SSD</td>
<td>~50=E2=80=93100 =C2=B5s</td>
<td>~1,000=C3=97 slower</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Random read from spinning HDD (seek)</td>
<td>~10 ms</td>
<td>~100,000=C3=97 slower</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Three compounding reasons the buffer pool pays off:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Locality</strong> =E2=80=94 real workloads are wildly skewed. A=
 tiny &quot;hot&quot; subset (recent orders, active users, the upper levels=
 of every B+ tree) serves most queries. Cache that subset and almost every =
read is a hit.</li>
<li><strong>Index pages stay resident</strong> =E2=80=94 a B+ tree's root a=
nd internal nodes are touched by <em>every</em> lookup. Keeping them in mem=
ory turns a 4-level index traversal from 4 disk reads into 1 (or 0).</li>
<li><strong>Write batching</strong> =E2=80=94 random page writes are the mo=
st expensive thing a disk does. Deferring and coalescing them is what lets =
a DBMS sustain thousands of writes per second.</li>
</ol>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"what-if-we-had-no-buffer-pool" data-toc=3D"What If We Had No Buff=
er Pool?">What If We Had No Buffer Pool?</h3>
<p>Suppose every read and write went straight to disk:</p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Consequence</th>
<th>Why</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Every row read =3D a disk I/O</strong></td>
<td>A query scanning 10,000 rows would do thousands of physical reads inste=
ad of a handful</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Index lookups become brutal</strong></td>
<td>A 4-level B+ tree =3D 4 separate disk reads <em>per row lookup</em>, ev=
ery single time</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Every <code>UPDATE</code> =3D a random 16 KB disk write</strong=
></td>
<td>Updating one row 100 times =3D 100 physical writes to the same block</t=
d>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Throughput collapses</strong></td>
<td>From ~10=E2=81=B5=E2=80=9310=E2=81=B6 page accesses/sec to ~10=C2=B2=E2=
=80=9310=E2=81=B4 =E2=80=94 three to four orders of magnitude</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Joins and sorts become impractical</strong></td>
<td>A nested-loop join re-reads the inner table's pages repeatedly =E2=80=
=94 with no cache, each pass hits the disk again</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Concurrency gets worse too</strong></td>
<td>Transactions hold locks while waiting on disk, so lock wait times explo=
de and deadlocks become far more likely</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>The disk wears out faster</strong></td>
<td>On SSDs, the write amplification from un-coalesced writes directly shor=
tens device life</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>Concrete arithmetic:</strong> a query touching 1,000 pages =E2=
=80=94</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">text</span></div><pre><code class=3D=
"hljs">  With buffer pool (99% hit rate):  990 =C3=97 100 ns  +  10 =C3=97 =
100 =C2=B5s  =E2=89=88  1.1 ms
  With no buffer pool:             1000 =C3=97 100 =C2=B5s                 =
 =E2=89=88  100 ms

  ~90=C3=97 slower =E2=80=94 and that's on fast SSD. On an HDD it would be =
~10 seconds.</code></pre></div>
