p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> without a buffer pool, a database is just a=
 slow file system. It's the component that makes the difference between &qu=
ot;a query is a memory operation&quot; and &quot;a query is a disk operatio=
n.&quot;</p>
</div>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"eviction--which-page-gets-thrown-out" data-toc=3D"Eviction =E2=80=
=94 Which Page Gets Thrown Out?">Eviction =E2=80=94 Which Page Gets Thrown =
Out?</h3>
<p>The pool is finite, so when a new page must come in, an existing one mus=
t go. If the victim is <strong>dirty</strong>, it must be flushed to disk f=
irst; if <strong>clean</strong>, it can simply be dropped.</p>
<p><strong>Plain LRU</strong> (evict the Least Recently Used page) is the s=
tarting point, but it has a fatal flaw: <strong>one big table scan evicts t=
he entire hot working set</strong>, because thousands of pages that will ne=
ver be read again get inserted at the &quot;most recent&quot; end.</p>
