ages enter at the midpoint, eviction from the tail, promotion on re-access"=
 loading=3D"lazy"></figure>
<p>A one-off full scan's pages enter the old sublist, are never re-read, an=
d get evicted from there =E2=80=94 leaving the hot young pages untouched. P=
ostgreSQL solves the same problem differently, with a clock-sweep algorithm=
 and a small ring buffer for sequential scans.</p>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"sizing--monitoring" data-toc=3D"Sizing &amp; Monitoring">Sizing &=
amp; Monitoring</h3>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Engine</th>
<th>Typical setting</th>
<th>Note</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>MySQL / InnoDB</td>
<td><code>innodb_buffer_pool_size</code> =3D <strong>70=E2=80=9380% of RAM<=
/strong> on a dedicated server</td>
<td>InnoDB bypasses the OS cache, so it wants the memory itself</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>PostgreSQL</td>
<td><code>shared_buffers</code> =3D <strong>~25% of RAM</strong></td>
<td>Postgres deliberately leans on the OS page cache too</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
