trong> &quot;The buffer pool is the DBMS's cache of disk pages. Reads check=
 it first =E2=80=94 a hit is served from RAM in nanoseconds, a miss costs a=
 physical page read. Writes modify the page in memory, marking it dirty, an=
d durability comes from <code>fsync</code>ing the WAL at commit, not from w=
riting the data page; dirty pages are flushed in the background at checkpoi=
nts. Without it every row access would be a disk I/O and throughput would d=
rop by orders of magnitude. The main tuning knobs are its size and the evic=
tion policy =E2=80=94 InnoDB uses a midpoint-insertion LRU specifically so =
a full table scan can't evict the hot working set.&quot;</p>
