<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th><strong>Asynchronous</strong> (default)</th>
<th><strong>Semi-synchronous</strong></th>
<th><strong>Group Replication</strong> (InnoDB Cluster)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Source waits for</strong></td>
<td>Nothing</td>
<td>=E2=89=A51 replica to confirm it <strong>received</strong> the events a=
nd wrote them to its relay log (<strong>not</strong> applied)</td>
<td>A <strong>majority</strong> of members to agree (Paxos-based) on the tr=
ansaction's order and certify it</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Extra commit latency</strong></td>
<td>None</td>
<td>+1 network round trip</td>
<td>+1 consensus round trip</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Data loss if the source dies</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F Possible: transactions committed but not yet shipped=
</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 None, <strong>if</strong> the acknowledging replica is promot=
ed</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 None, as long as a majority survives</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Replica can still lag?</strong></td>
<td>Yes</td>
<td><strong>Yes</strong>: &quot;received&quot; =E2=89=A0 &quot;applied&quot=
;</td>
<td>Yes: other members apply asynchronously</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Failover</strong></td>
<td>External tool (Orchestrator, etc.) or manual</td>
<td>External tool</td>
<td><strong>Automatic</strong> primary election inside the group</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Minimum setup</strong></td>
<td>1 source + 1 replica</td>
<td>1 source + =E2=89=A51 replica</td>
<td>3 members (tolerates 1 failure)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>Semi-sync details that interviewers like:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong><code>AFTER_SYNC</code> (default, &quot;lossless&quot;):</stron=
g> the source waits for the replica's ACK <em>before</em> committing in Inn=
oDB. No other client can see a change that might be lost.</li>
<li><strong><code>AFTER_COMMIT</code> (older):</strong> it commits first, t=
hen waits. Other sessions may briefly see data that a crash could still los=
e.</li>
<li>=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F <strong>Silent fallback:</strong> if no replica ACKs=
 within <code>rpl_semi_sync_source_timeout</code> (default <strong>10 s</st=
rong>), the source <strong>falls back to async</strong> and keeps going. It=
 switches back once a replica catches up. Monitor <code>Rpl_semi_sync_sourc=
e_status</code>, or you may be running async without knowing it.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"read-replicas--readwrite-splitting" data-toc=3D"Read Replicas &am=
p; Read/Write Splitting">Read Replicas &amp; Read/Write Splitting</h3>
<p>Most apps read far more than they write, so add replicas and <strong>rou=
te reads to them</strong> (the right half of the diagram above):</p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Where the routing happens</th>
<th>How</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Application</strong></td>
<td>Two connection pools / datasources: <code>writeDb</code> =E2=86=92 sour=
ce, <code>readDb</code> =E2=86=92 replicas</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Proxy</strong></td>
<td>ProxySQL / MySQL Router split by query type (<code>SELECT</code> =E2=86=
=92 replicas) and health-check the nodes (<a href=3D"#proxysql--the-mysql-a=
ware-proxy">ProxySQL below</a>)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Driver</strong></td>
<td>Some drivers support &quot;read-only&quot; connections that go to repli=
cas</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Mark replicas <code>super_read_only =3D ON</code> so nothing writes to t=
hem by accident.</p>
