 0Z"></path></svg>Warning</p><p><strong>Pinning is the catch.</strong> Mult=
iplexing only works while a client doesn't need &quot;its own&quot; backend=
 connection. Open <strong>transactions</strong>, <strong>temp tables</stron=
g>, <strong>session variables</strong> or <code>LAST_INSERT_ID()</code> pin=
 a backend connection to that client. A client holding hundreds of open tra=
nsactions can use up the pool. MySQL is still capped by <code>mysql_servers=
.max_connections</code>, but other clients wait in the queue. Keep transact=
ions short.</p>
</div>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>1,000 client connections</th>
<th>Plain MySQL</th>
<th>Behind ProxySQL</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>MySQL sees</td>
<td>1,000 threads (or errors past <code>max_connections</code>)</td>
<td>A capped pool, e.g. 20=E2=80=93100</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>When the limit is hit</td>
<td>Immediate &quot;Too many connections&quot; for everyone</td>
<td>Request <strong>queues</strong>; errors only after the timeout, and onl=
y for the noisy user if per-user limits are set</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Per-user isolation</td>
<td><code>MAX_USER_CONNECTIONS</code> per account</td>
<td>Per user <strong>and</strong> per backend server</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<blockquote>
<p>PostgreSQL has the same problem, made worse by <strong>one process per c=
onnection</strong>. There the standard fix is <strong>PgBouncer</strong> in=
 transaction-pooling mode, plus per-role <code>CONNECTION LIMIT</code>s.</p=
>
</blockquote>
<h4 id=3D"proxysql-vs-haproxy">ProxySQL vs HAProxy</h4>
<p><strong>HAProxy</strong> is a general-purpose, very fast load balancer f=
or TCP and HTTP. In front of MySQL it works at the <strong>TCP level (layer=
 4)</strong>: it forwards connections without understanding the SQL inside =
them.</p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th><strong>ProxySQL</strong></th>
<th><strong>HAProxy</strong> (for MySQL)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Understands SQL?</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 Yes (layer 7, MySQL protocol)</td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C No: just forwards TCP connections (layer 4)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Read/write split</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 Automatic, per query (query rules)</td>
<td>=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F Only by <strong>port</strong>: e.g. <code>:3307</cod=
e> =E2=86=92 source, <code>:3308</code> =E2=86=92 replicas. The <strong>app=
 must choose</strong> which port to use</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Connection pooling</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 Multiplexes many app connections onto few DB connections</td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C Each app connection =3D one DB connection</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Health checks</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 MySQL-aware: <code>read_only</code>, replication lag</td>
<td>=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F Basic (<code>option mysql-check</code> =3D can I con=
nect?); lag checks need an external script</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Follows failover</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 Automatically, via the <code>read_only</code> flag</td>
<td>=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F Via health checks or scripts; it can't tell which se=
rver is the writer</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Query caching / rewrite / firewall / stats</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 Yes</td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C No</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Speed / overhead</strong></td>
<td>Fast, but parses every query</td>
<td>=E2=9A=A1 Extremely fast and lightweight</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Protocols</strong></td>
<td>MySQL (and compatible)</td>
<td>Any TCP or HTTP service</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Best for</strong></td>
<td>MySQL replication setups that need smart routing and pooling</td>
<td>Simple TCP balancing, e.g. in front of Galera / InnoDB Cluster or repli=
cas when the app already splits reads and writes</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
