<ol>
<li>User changes their display name =E2=86=92 <code>UPDATE users =E2=80=A6<=
/code> goes to the <strong>source</strong> =E2=86=92 &quot;Saved!&quot;</li=
>
<li>The page reloads =E2=86=92 <code>SELECT =E2=80=A6 FROM users</code> goe=
s to a <strong>replica</strong> that is 2 s behind.</li>
<li>The user sees the <strong>old name</strong> and thinks the save failed.=
</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Fixes</strong>, from simplest to most precise:</p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Fix</th>
<th>How it works</th>
<th>Trade-off</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Read from the source after writing</strong></td>
<td>For N seconds after a user's write (timestamp in session/cookie), send =
<em>that user's</em> reads to the source</td>
<td>Simple; extra load on the source</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Critical reads always go to the source</strong></td>
<td>E.g. &quot;my profile&quot;, &quot;my order status&quot;, checkout</td>
<td>Only the important paths pay the cost</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Wait for my GTID on the replica</strong></td>
<td>After the write, remember its GTID; before reading, run <code>SELECT WA=
IT_FOR_EXECUTED_GTID_SET('&lt;gtid&gt;', 1);</code> on the replica (returns=
 once applied, or times out after 1 s =E2=86=92 fall back to the source)</t=
d>
<td>Exact; needs plumbing. ProxySQL can do it automatically (&quot;GTID cau=
sal reads&quot;)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Group Replication consistency</strong></td>
<td><code>SET SESSION group_replication_consistency =3D 'BEFORE';</code> ma=
kes a read wait until the member has applied everything before it</td>
<td>Built in; adds latency to those reads</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>Related anomaly =E2=80=94 time going backwards:</strong> two rea=
ds from two replicas with different lag can show a newer value and then an =
older one. Fix it by <strong>pinning a user's session to one replica</stron=
g> (monotonic reads).</p>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"failover--when-the-source-dies" data-toc=3D"Failover =E2=80=94 Wh=
en the Source Dies">Failover =E2=80=94 When the Source Dies</h3>
<p><strong>Failover</strong> =3D promoting a replica to be the new source a=
nd pointing everyone at it. It can be <strong>manual</strong> (a DBA runs t=
he steps) or <strong>automatic</strong> (a tool detects the failure and doe=
s it in seconds).</p>
<h4 id=3D"candidate-master-designated-failover-replica">Candidate Master (D=
esignated Failover Replica)</h4>
<p>A <strong>candidate master</strong> is a replica <strong>chosen in advan=
ce</strong> to become the new source if the current one dies. The term come=
s from the failover tool MHA (<code>candidate_master=3D1</code>); other too=
ls call it a <em>promotion rule</em> or <em>member weight</em>.</p>
<p><strong>Why choose in advance?</strong> Not every replica should ever be=
come the source:</p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Replica</th>
<th>Should it be promoted?</th>
<th>Why</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Same hardware, same region, up to date</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 <strong>Candidate master</strong></td>
<td>Can take the full write load immediately</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Reporting / analytics replica</td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C</td>
<td>Weaker hardware or heavy queries running on it</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Delayed replica (e.g. 1 hour behind on purpose, for recovery)</td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C Never</td>
<td>It's missing an hour of data by design</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Cross-region replica</td>
<td>=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F Only for disaster recovery</td>
<td>Writes from the main region would suddenly be slow</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Replica with replication filters</td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C</td>
<td>It doesn't hold all the data</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>What makes a good candidate master:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Same hardware and MySQL version</strong> as the source, so it c=
an carry the full write load.</li>
<li><strong>Binary log enabled</strong> (<code>log_bin</code>, and <code>lo=
g_replica_updates</code> so it also logs the changes it applies). After pro=
motion, the other replicas replicate <strong>from it</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>GTIDs on</strong>, so the other replicas can re-point automatic=
ally.</li>
<li><strong>It's the semi-sync acknowledger</strong>: if it ACKs the source=
's commits, it's guaranteed to have every acknowledged transaction, so prom=
oting it loses no data.</li>
<li><strong>Low lag, no heavy queries, no filters</strong>, and in a <stron=
g>different availability zone</strong> from the source, so one zone outage =
can't take both.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>How tools mark it:</strong></p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Tool</th>
<th>Setting</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>MHA</strong></td>
<td><code>candidate_master=3D1</code> on the preferred replica, <code>no_ma=
ster=3D1</code> on replicas that must never be promoted</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Orchestrator</strong></td>
<td>Promotion rules per host: <code>prefer</code>, <code>neutral</code>, <c=
ode>prefer_not</code>, <code>must_not</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Group Replication</strong></td>
<td><code>group_replication_member_weight</code> (0=E2=80=93100): the highe=
st-weight healthy member wins the election</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
