L routes writes to A, and the old source is fenced off" title=3D"Failover: =
before, the app goes through ProxySQL to the source, with replica A (candid=
ate master) and replica B replicating from it; the source crashes; after fa=
ilover, replica A is promoted to source, replica B now replicates from A, P=
roxySQL routes writes to A, and the old source is fenced off" loading=3D"la=
zy"></figure>
<ol>
<li><strong>Detect.</strong> The tool notices the source is unreachable. To=
 avoid a false alarm from a network blip, it checks from <strong>several pl=
aces</strong>. Orchestrator, for example, also asks the replicas whether <e=
m>they</em> lost the source too. Typical detection time: 5=E2=80=9310 s.</l=
i>
<li><strong>Choose the new source.</strong> The <strong>candidate master</s=
trong>, if it's healthy and up to date. Otherwise the replica with the <str=
ong>most complete GTID set</strong> (fewest missing transactions).</li>
<li><strong>Catch up.</strong> The chosen replica applies everything still =
in its relay log. If other replicas received transactions it doesn't have, =
those are copied to it first (tools like MHA do this), so nothing that reac=
hed any replica is lost.</li>
<li><strong>Promote.</strong> Make it writable:<div class=3D"code"><div cla=
ss=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span clas=
s=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"hljs">STOP REPLICA;
RESET REPLICA <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ALL</span>;                 <spa=
n class=3D"hljs-comment">-- it no longer replicates from anyone</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SET</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">GLOBAL=
</span> super_read_only <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> OFF;
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SET</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">GLOBAL=
</span> read_only <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> OFF;</code></pre=
></div>
</li>
<li><strong>Re-point the other replicas</strong> to the new source (GTIDs m=
ake this one command each):<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><spa=
n class=3D"dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span=
></div><pre><code class=3D"hljs">CHANGE REPLICATION SOURCE <span class=3D"h=
ljs-keyword">TO</span> SOURCE_HOST <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span>=
 <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;replica-a&#x27;</span>, SOURCE_AUTO_POSI=
TION <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">1=
</span>;
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">START</span> REPLICA;</code></pre></div>
</li>
<li><strong>Redirect the application</strong> to the new source, using one =
of:
<ul>
<li><strong>ProxySQL:</strong> automatic, because it sees <code>read_only =
=3D OFF</code> and moves the server into the writer hostgroup.</li>
<li><strong>Virtual IP (VIP):</strong> move the floating IP to the new sour=
ce.</li>
<li><strong>DNS / service discovery</strong> (e.g. Consul): update the reco=
rd. Watch DNS TTLs and client caching.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Fence the old source.</strong> Make sure it can never accept wr=
ites again if it comes back: power it off, cut its network, or force <code>=
super_read_only =3D ON</code> at startup. Later, rebuild it as a replica of=
 the new source.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Typical timeline (automatic):</strong> crash at <strong>t =3D 0<=
/strong> =E2=86=92 detected <strong>~5=E2=80=9310 s</strong> =E2=86=92 prom=
oted and replicas re-pointed <strong>~15 s</strong> =E2=86=92 traffic switc=
hed <strong>~20=E2=80=9330 s</strong>. Applications should <strong>retry</s=
trong> failed writes during that window.</p>
<h4 id=3D"manual-vs-automatic-failover">Manual vs Automatic Failover</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th><strong>Manual</strong></th>
<th><strong>Automatic</strong></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Who decides</strong></td>
<td>A DBA, after investigating</td>
<td>A tool, by rules</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Downtime</strong></td>
<td>Minutes to hours (someone has to be paged and respond)</td>
<td>Seconds to about a minute</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Risk</strong></td>
<td>Slow, but a human can spot false alarms</td>
<td>A false detection can trigger a needless failover, or split brain if fe=
ncing fails</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Tools</strong></td>
<td>Runbook + the SQL above</td>
<td>Orchestrator, MHA (older), MySQL InnoDB Cluster (Group Replication + My=
SQL Router), managed cloud (Amazon RDS Multi-AZ, Aurora)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h4 id=3D"what-can-go-wrong">What Can Go Wrong</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Risk</th>
<th>What happens</th>
<th>Prevention</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Data loss (async)</strong></td>
<td>Transactions the old source committed but never shipped are gone after =
promotion</td>
<td>Semi-sync with the candidate master as acknowledger, or Group Replicati=
on</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Split brain</strong></td>
<td>Old source wakes up and both nodes accept writes =E2=86=92 divergent da=
ta</td>
<td>Fencing (power off / revoke its network), <code>super_read_only</code> =
on start, majority-based tools</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Errant transactions</strong></td>
<td>A replica has a GTID the new source doesn't (someone wrote to a replica=
)</td>
<td><code>super_read_only</code> on every replica</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Flapping</strong></td>
<td>A server that keeps going up and down triggers repeated failovers</td>
<td>Tools block a second automatic failover for a cool-down period</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
