a.75.75 0 0 1-.75-.75Z"></path></svg>Tip</p><p><strong>&quot;How does MySQL=
 replication work, and what problems does it bring?&quot;</strong>
&quot;The source writes every committed transaction to its binlog. Each rep=
lica's receiver thread copies those events into a relay log, and applier th=
reads replay them. By default it's asynchronous, so replicas lag and a sour=
ce crash can lose the last transactions. Semi-sync waits for one replica to=
 acknowledge receipt, and Group Replication waits for a majority. Read repl=
icas scale reads but not writes, and lag causes read-your-own-writes bugs, =
which I'd fix by reading from the source right after a write or waiting for=
 the write's GTID on the replica. On failover a tool like Orchestrator prom=
otes the pre-chosen candidate master (or the most up-to-date replica by GTI=
D), re-points the other replicas, and ProxySQL follows the new writer autom=
atically. The old source is fenced to avoid split brain.&quot;</p>
</div>
<hr>
<hr>
</section><section class=3D"sec"><h2 id=3D"database-partitioning" data-toc=
=3D"41. Database Partitioning"><span class=3D"sec-n">41.</span> Database Pa=
rtitioning</h2>
<p>When a single table grows to hundreds of millions (or billions) of rows,=
 or has dozens of columns with different access patterns, even well-indexed=
 queries start to slow down =E2=80=94 because the table simply doesn't fit =
efficiently into memory or disk I/O anymore. <strong>Partitioning</strong> =
is the strategy of splitting a large table into smaller, more manageable pi=
eces.</p>
<p>There are two fundamentally different ways to partition a table:</p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Strategy</th>
<th>What It Splits</th>
<th>Analogy</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Vertical Partitioning</strong></td>
<td><strong>Columns</strong> =E2=80=94 divides the table into narrower tabl=
es</td>
<td>Tearing a wide spreadsheet into separate sheets by column groups</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Horizontal Partitioning</strong></td>
<td><strong>Rows</strong> =E2=80=94 divides the table into smaller tables w=
ith the same columns</td>
<td>Splitting a phone book into volumes A=E2=80=93M and N=E2=80=93Z</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"vertical-partitioning" data-toc=3D"Vertical Partitioning">Vertica=
l Partitioning</h3>
<p>Vertical partitioning means <strong>splitting a table's columns</strong>=
 into two or more separate tables. Each resulting table has the <strong>sam=
e number of rows</strong> but <strong>fewer columns</strong>. The tables ar=
e linked by the same primary key so you can <code>JOIN</code> them back whe=
n needed.</p>
<h4 id=3D"why-do-it">Why Do It?</h4>
<p>Not all columns are accessed equally. In a typical <code>users</code> ta=
ble, you might read <code>name</code> and <code>email</code> on every page =
load, but <code>bio</code> (a large <code>TEXT</code> column) and <code>pro=
file_picture_url</code> only when someone visits a profile page. Keeping ev=
erything in one table means:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Row size is large</strong> =E2=86=92 fewer rows fit per InnoDB =
page (16 KB) =E2=86=92 more disk reads for common queries</li>
<li><strong>Buffer pool is wasted</strong> =E2=80=94 hot, small columns sha=
re pages with cold, large columns</li>
<li><strong>Full scans are slower</strong> =E2=80=94 even reading just <cod=
e>name</code> and <code>email</code> has to skip over the bulky <code>bio</=
code> column in every row</li>
</ul>
<h4 id=3D"example--e-commerce-product-table">Example =E2=80=94 E-Commerce P=
roduct Table</h4>
<p>Suppose you have a single <code>products</code> table:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keywor=
d">TABLE</span> products (
    id           <span class=3D"hljs-type">INT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-k=
eyword">PRIMARY</span> KEY AUTO_INCREMENT,
    name         <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class=3D"hl=
js-number">200</span>),
    price        <span class=3D"hljs-type">DECIMAL</span>(<span class=3D"hl=
js-number">10</span>,<span class=3D"hljs-number">2</span>),
    category_id  <span class=3D"hljs-type">INT</span>,
    stock        <span class=3D"hljs-type">INT</span>,
    <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- These columns are accessed rarely (only=
 on the product detail page)</span>
    description  TEXT,             <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- ~2 KB av=
erage</span>
    specs_json   JSON,             <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- ~1 KB av=
erage</span>
    warranty     TEXT,             <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- ~500 byt=
es average</span>
    reviews_avg  <span class=3D"hljs-type">DECIMAL</span>(<span class=3D"hl=
js-number">3</span>,<span class=3D"hljs-number">2</span>),
    created_at   DATETIME
);</code></pre></div>
<p><strong>Problem:</strong> Every query that lists products (homepage, sea=
rch results, category pages) reads just <code>name</code>, <code>price</cod=
e>, <code>category_id</code>, and <code>stock</code> =E2=80=94 but InnoDB l=
oads the <strong>full row</strong> including the heavy <code>description</c=
ode>, <code>specs_json</code>, and <code>warranty</code> columns. For 10 mi=
llion products, this wastes enormous amounts of buffer pool memory.</p>
<h4 id=3D"after-vertical-partitioning">After Vertical Partitioning</h4>
<p>Split into two tables =E2=80=94 <strong>frequently accessed columns</str=
ong> and <strong>rarely accessed columns</strong>:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Table 1: Hot data (accessed on every =
page =E2=80=94 listing, search, cart)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TAB=
LE</span> products_core (
    id           <span class=3D"hljs-type">INT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-k=
eyword">PRIMARY</span> KEY AUTO_INCREMENT,
    name         <span class=3D"hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class=3D"hl=
js-number">200</span>),
    price        <span class=3D"hljs-type">DECIMAL</span>(<span class=3D"hl=
js-number">10</span>,<span class=3D"hljs-number">2</span>),
    category_id  <span class=3D"hljs-type">INT</span>,
    stock        <span class=3D"hljs-type">INT</span>,
    reviews_avg  <span class=3D"hljs-type">DECIMAL</span>(<span class=3D"hl=
js-number">3</span>,<span class=3D"hljs-number">2</span>),
    created_at   DATETIME
);

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Table 2: Cold data (accessed only on the pr=
oduct detail page)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TAB=
LE</span> products_detail (
    product_id   <span class=3D"hljs-type">INT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-k=
eyword">PRIMARY</span> KEY,    <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Same PK as p=
roducts_core</span>
    description  TEXT,
    specs_json   JSON,
    warranty     TEXT,
    <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FOREIGN</span> KEY (product_id) <span clas=
s=3D"hljs-keyword">REFERENCES</span> products_core(id)
);</code></pre></div>
