<h4 id=3D"delete-vs-drop-partition--side-by-side">DELETE vs DROP PARTITION =
=E2=80=94 Side by Side</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Aspect</th>
<th><code>DELETE WHERE date &lt; X</code></th>
<th><code>DROP PARTITION</code></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Speed for 10M rows</strong></td>
<td>Minutes to hours</td>
<td>Milliseconds</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Lock behavior</strong></td>
<td>Row-level locks =E2=80=94 blocks other writes to those rows</td>
<td>Metadata lock only =E2=80=94 near-instant, minimal blocking</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Undo log usage</strong></td>
<td>Generates undo entries for every deleted row (massive)</td>
<td>Zero undo log entries</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Redo log usage</strong></td>
<td>Writes every change to redo log</td>
<td>Only metadata change logged</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Disk space reclaimed?</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C No =E2=80=94 space stays allocated in tablespace until <code>=
OPTIMIZE TABLE</code></td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 Yes =E2=80=94 the <code>.ibd</code> file is deleted, OS recla=
ims the space immediately</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Replication impact</strong></td>
<td>Every deleted row is replicated to replicas (slow)</td>
<td>Only the <code>ALTER TABLE</code> statement is replicated (instant)</td=
>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Impact on running queries</strong></td>
<td>Holds locks, can cause timeouts for concurrent operations</td>
<td>Brief metadata lock, negligible impact</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Rollback possible?</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 Yes =E2=80=94 can <code>ROLLBACK</code> before <code>COMMIT</=
code></td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C No =E2=80=94 DDL is auto-committed and irreversible</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h4 id=3D"benefits-of-partition-based-ttl-rotation">Benefits of Partition-B=
ased TTL Rotation</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Benefit</th>
<th>Explanation</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Instant deletion</strong></td>
<td>Dropping a partition removes millions/billions of rows in milliseconds =
=E2=80=94 it's a file delete, not a row-by-row operation</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Zero lock contention</strong></td>
<td>Unlike <code>DELETE</code>, it doesn't hold row locks =E2=80=94 your ap=
plication keeps reading and writing normally during the drop</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>No undo/redo log bloat</strong></td>
<td>A <code>DELETE</code> of 100M rows generates gigabytes of undo logs; <c=
ode>DROP PARTITION</code> generates almost none</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Disk space actually freed</strong></td>
<td><code>DELETE</code> leaves &quot;holes&quot; in the tablespace that req=
uire <code>OPTIMIZE TABLE</code> to reclaim; <code>DROP PARTITION</code> de=
letes the physical file</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Replication-friendly</strong></td>
<td>Only the DDL statement travels to replicas, not millions of row-level d=
elete events</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Predictable performance</strong></td>
<td>The time to drop a partition is constant regardless of how many rows it=
 contains =E2=80=94 1 row or 100 million rows, it's the same speed</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Natural data organization</strong></td>
<td>Queries filtering by date automatically benefit from partition pruning =
=E2=80=94 even your regular <code>SELECT</code> queries get faster</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Simple archival</strong></td>
<td>Before dropping, you can <code>SELECT INTO OUTFILE</code> from the part=
ition to archive to cold storage (S3, HDFS, etc.)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h4 id=3D"demerits-and-gotchas">Demerits and Gotchas</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Demerit</th>
<th>Explanation</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Partition key must be in every unique index</strong></td>
<td>MySQL enforces that the partition key column must be part of the primar=
y key and every unique index =E2=80=94 this can force you to change your sc=
hema. For example, you can't have <code>PRIMARY KEY (id)</code> alone; you =
need <code>PRIMARY KEY (id, created_at)</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Foreign keys not supported</strong></td>
<td>MySQL does <strong>not</strong> support foreign key constraints on part=
itioned tables. If your table has FK relationships, you'll need to enforce =
referential integrity in your application code</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>No rollback</strong></td>
<td><code>DROP PARTITION</code> is a DDL operation =E2=80=94 it's auto-comm=
itted and <strong>irreversible</strong>. If you accidentally drop the wrong=
 partition, the data is gone (unless you have a backup or replica)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Partition management overhead</strong></td>
<td>Someone (or something) must create future partitions and drop old ones =
on schedule. If the cron job fails and no new partition is created, inserts=
 into <code>p_future</code> will work but partition pruning degrades. If <c=
ode>p_future</code> doesn't exist and no partition covers the date, inserts=
 <strong>fail</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Cross-partition queries are slower</strong></td>
<td>Queries that don't include the partition key in <code>WHERE</code> must=
 scan <strong>all</strong> partitions =E2=80=94 potentially slower than a s=
ingle well-indexed table</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Limited number of partitions</strong></td>
<td>MySQL supports up to ~8,192 partitions per table. For daily partitions =
with 7-day retention, this is never an issue (only 8-9 partitions). But for=
 fine-grained partitions (hourly) over long retention periods, you could hi=
t the limit</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>ALTER TABLE is heavier</strong></td>
<td>Adding/modifying columns on a partitioned table is slower because MySQL=
 must modify every partition's data file. Tools like <code>pt-online-schema=
-change</code> have partition-specific limitations</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Query planner limitations</strong></td>
<td>Partition pruning only works when the <code>WHERE</code> clause directl=
y references the partition key with constants or simple expressions. Comple=
x expressions like <code>WHERE DATEDIFF(CURDATE(), created_at) &gt; 7</code=
> may not trigger pruning =E2=80=94 you should use <code>WHERE created_at &=
lt; DATE_SUB(CURDATE(), INTERVAL 7 DAY)</code> instead</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h4 id=3D"best-practices-for-production-ttl-rotation">Best Practices for Pr=
oduction TTL Rotation</h4>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Always keep a <code>p_future</code> (or <code>MAXVALUE</code>) p=
artition</strong> =E2=80=94 this is your safety net. If the cron job that c=
reates new partitions fails, inserts still succeed (they land in <code>p_fu=
ture</code>) instead of throwing errors</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Create partitions 2-3 days ahead</strong> =E2=80=94 don't wait u=
ntil midnight to create tomorrow's partition. Pre-create a few days in adva=
nce so clock skew, timezone differences, or missed cron runs don't cause fa=
ilures</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Monitor the rotation job</strong> =E2=80=94 set up alerts if the=
 partition rotation doesn't run. A common failure mode: partitions stop bei=
ng created, all new data goes into <code>p_future</code>, and partition pru=
ning stops working (silently degraded performance)</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Test the partition drop with <code>EXPLAIN</code> first</strong>=
 =E2=80=94 before automating, verify that your queries actually benefit fro=
m pruning:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs">EXPLAIN <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hlj=
s-operator">*</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> events <span =
class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> created_at <span class=3D"hljs-operator=
">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;2025-02-24&#x27;</span>;
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Check the &quot;partitions&quot; column =E2=
=80=94 it should show only ONE partition, not all of them</span></code></pr=
e></div>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Archive before dropping</strong> =E2=80=94 if there's any chance=
 you'll need the old data later, export it before dropping:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Export the partition&#x27;s data to a=
 file before dropping</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> events <span class=3D"hljs-=
keyword">PARTITION</span> (p20250218)
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">INTO</span> OUTFILE <span class=3D"hljs-string=
">&#x27;/tmp/events_20250218.csv&#x27;</span>
FIELDS TERMINATED <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">BY</span> <span class=3D"hlj=
s-string">&#x27;,&#x27;</span> LINES TERMINATED <span class=3D"hljs-keyword=
">BY</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;\n&#x27;</span>;

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Now safe to drop</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ALTER</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TABL=
E</span> events <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">DROP</span> <span class=3D"hlj=
s-keyword">PARTITION</span> p20250218;</code></pre></div>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Use <code>RANGE</code> partitioning (not <code>HASH</code>)</str=
ong> =E2=80=94 hash partitions can't be dropped selectively by date because=
 rows are distributed by hash value, not by time. Only range (or list) part=
itioning allows you to drop a specific time window</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"partitioning-vs-multiple-tables--are-they-the-same" data-toc=3D"P=
artitioning vs Multiple Tables =E2=80=94 Are They the Same?">Partitioning v=
s Multiple Tables =E2=80=94 Are They the Same?</h3>
<p>At first glance, partitioning looks identical to just creating separate =
tables (<code>events_day1</code>, <code>events_day2</code>, etc.). Both spl=
it data into smaller chunks. But they are <strong>fundamentally different</=
strong> =E2=80=94 partitioning is managed <strong>by MySQL internally</stro=
ng>, while multiple tables are managed <strong>by you</strong> in your appl=
ication code. This difference matters enormously.</p>
<h4 id=3D"the-manual-approach-multiple-tables">The Manual Approach (Multipl=
e Tables)</h4>
<p>Without partitioning, you'd create a new table for each day and route qu=
eries yourself:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- You manually create one table per day=
</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TAB=
LE</span> events_20250218 (...);
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TAB=
LE</span> events_20250219 (...);
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TAB=
LE</span> events_20250220 (...);
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- ...and so on</span>

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Your APPLICATION must decide which table to=
 query</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- To find events from Feb 19:</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> events_20250219 <span class=
=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> event_type <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D=
</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;click&#x27;</span>;

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- To find events across multiple days, YOU mu=
st UNION them manually:</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> events_20250218 <span class=
=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> event_type <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D=
</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;click&#x27;</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">UNION</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ALL<=
/span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> events_20250219 <span class=
=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> event_type <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D=
</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;click&#x27;</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">UNION</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ALL<=
/span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> events_20250220 <span class=
=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> event_type <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D=
</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;click&#x27;</span>;

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- To delete old data:</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">DROP</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TABLE=
</span> events_20250218;</code></pre></div>
<h4 id=3D"the-partitioning-approach-mysql-managed">The Partitioning Approac=
h (MySQL Managed)</h4>
<p>With partitioning, there's <strong>one logical table</strong> and MySQL =
handles everything behind the scenes:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- One table, MySQL manages the internal=
 split</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TAB=
LE</span> events (...) <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">PARTITION</span> <span =
class=3D"hljs-keyword">BY</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">RANGE</span> =
(TO_DAYS(created_at)) (...);

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Your application queries ONE table =E2=80=
=94 same as any normal table</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> events <span class=3D"hljs-=
keyword">WHERE</span> event_type <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> <=
span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;click&#x27;</span> <span class=3D"hljs-key=
word">AND</span> created_at <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> <span =
class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;2025-02-19&#x27;</span>;
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- MySQL automatically routes this to the corr=
ect partition (partition pruning)</span>

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Multi-day query =E2=80=94 SAME simple query=
, no UNION needed</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">*<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> events
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> event_type <span class=3D"hljs-op=
erator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;click&#x27;</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">AND</span> created_at <span class=3D"hljs-keyw=
ord">BETWEEN</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;2025-02-18&#x27;</spa=
n> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">AND</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x2=
7;2025-02-20&#x27;</span>;
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- MySQL scans only the relevant partitions au=
tomatically</span>

<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Delete old data:</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ALTER</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">TABL=
E</span> events <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">DROP</span> <span class=3D"hlj=
s-keyword">PARTITION</span> p20250218;</code></pre></div>
<h4 id=3D"why-partitioning-is-better-than-multiple-tables">Why Partitioning=
 Is Better Than Multiple Tables</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Aspect</th>
<th>Multiple Tables (Manual)</th>
<th>Partitioning (MySQL Managed)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Query transparency</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C Application must know which table to query. Every query needs=
 table-routing logic.</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 Application queries <strong>one table name</strong> =E2=80=94=
 MySQL routes internally. Zero application code changes.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Cross-partition queries</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C You must write <code>UNION ALL</code> across all tables manua=
lly. Adding a new day means changing the query.</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 Just <code>SELECT * FROM events WHERE date BETWEEN x AND y</c=
ode> =E2=80=94 MySQL unions internally and prunes automatically.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Schema changes</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C <code>ALTER TABLE</code> must run on <strong>every</strong> t=
able separately. Add a column? Run it N times.</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 One <code>ALTER TABLE events ADD COLUMN ...</code> =E2=80=94 =
MySQL applies it to all partitions.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Indexes</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C Must create and maintain indexes on <strong>each</strong> tab=
le individually.</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 One <code>CREATE INDEX</code> =E2=80=94 applies to all partit=
ions.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Application complexity</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C Huge =E2=80=94 your app needs logic to: pick the right table,=
 build UNIONs, create new tables on schedule, handle edge cases around midn=
ight.</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 Minimal =E2=80=94 your app doesn't even know partitions exist=
. It's completely transparent.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Aggregations</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C <code>COUNT(*)</code> across all days requires UNION of all t=
ables.</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 <code>SELECT COUNT(*) FROM events</code> =E2=80=94 MySQL aggr=
egates across all partitions automatically.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Foreign keys from other tables</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C Impossible =E2=80=94 other tables can't FK to a table that ch=
anges name every day.</td>
<td>=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F MySQL doesn't support FK on partitioned tables eithe=
r, but at least the table name is stable for references in application code=
.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>ORM / Framework support</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C ORMs (JPA, Hibernate, Django ORM) don't know how to route to =
dynamic table names. You'd need raw SQL everywhere.</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 ORMs work normally =E2=80=94 they just see one table <code>ev=
ents</code>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Tooling compatibility</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C Monitoring, backup tools, migration tools all need to handle =
N tables.</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 All tools see one table =E2=80=94 <code>mysqldump events</cod=
e>, <code>EXPLAIN SELECT</code>, etc. work as expected.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Dropping old data</strong></td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 <code>DROP TABLE events_20250218</code> =E2=80=94 equally fas=
t.</td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 <code>ALTER TABLE events DROP PARTITION p20250218</code> =E2=
=80=94 equally fast.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h4 id=3D"example-the-pain-of-multiple-tables-in-real-code">Example: The Pa=
in of Multiple Tables in Real Code</h4>
<p>Imagine you manually manage separate tables in a Spring Boot application=
:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">Java</span></div><pre><code class=3D=
"hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">// =E2=9D=8C MANUAL TABLE ROUTING =E2=
=80=94 your code becomes a mess</span>

<span class=3D"hljs-meta">@Service</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">cla=
ss</span> <span class=3D"hljs-title class_">EventService</span> {

    <span class=3D"hljs-meta">@Autowired</span>
    <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">private</span> JdbcTemplate jdbc;

    <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">public</span> List&lt;Event&gt; <span clas=
s=3D"hljs-title function_">getEvents</span><span class=3D"hljs-params">(Loc=
alDate from, LocalDate to)</span> {
        <span class=3D"hljs-comment">// YOU must figure out which tables to=
 query</span>
        List&lt;String&gt; tableNames =3D <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">new<=
/span> <span class=3D"hljs-title class_">ArrayList</span>&lt;&gt;();
        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">for</span> (<span class=3D"hljs-type">=
LocalDate</span> <span class=3D"hljs-variable">d</span> <span class=3D"hljs=
-operator">=3D</span> from; !d.isAfter(to); d =3D d.plusDays(<span class=3D=
"hljs-number">1</span>)) {
            tableNames.add(<span class=3D"hljs-string">&quot;events_&quot;<=
/span> + d.format(DateTimeFormatter.BASIC_ISO_DATE));
        }

        <span class=3D"hljs-comment">// YOU must build a UNION ALL query dy=
namically</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-type">String</span> <span class=3D"hljs-variabl=
e">sql</span> <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> tableNames.stream()
            .map(t -&gt; <span class=3D"hljs-string">&quot;SELECT * FROM &q=
uot;</span> + t + <span class=3D"hljs-string">&quot; WHERE event_type =3D ?=
&quot;</span>)
            .collect(Collectors.joining(<span class=3D"hljs-string">&quot; =
UNION ALL &quot;</span>));

        <span class=3D"hljs-comment">// What if a table doesn&#x27;t exist =
yet? You need error handling.</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-comment">// What if the date range spans 30 day=
s? That&#x27;s 30 UNIONs.</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">return</span> jdbc.query(sql, mapper, =
<span class=3D"hljs-string">&quot;click&quot;</span>);
    }
}</code></pre></div>
<p>With partitioning, the same code is trivial:</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">Java</span></div><pre><code class=3D=
"hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">// =E2=9C=85 WITH PARTITIONING =E2=80=
=94 normal JPA, zero routing logic</span>

<span class=3D"hljs-meta">@Repository</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">int=
erface</span> <span class=3D"hljs-title class_">EventRepository</span> <spa=
n class=3D"hljs-keyword">extends</span> <span class=3D"hljs-title class_">J=
paRepository</span>&lt;Event, Long&gt; {

    <span class=3D"hljs-comment">// Just a regular query =E2=80=94 MySQL ha=
ndles partition routing</span>
    List&lt;Event&gt; <span class=3D"hljs-title function_">findByEventTypeA=
ndCreatedAtBetween</span><span class=3D"hljs-params">(
        String eventType, LocalDate from, LocalDate to
    )</span>;
}</code></pre></div>
<h4 id=3D"when-multiple-tables-actually-make-sense">When Multiple Tables Ac=
tually Make Sense</h4>
<p>There are rare cases where separate tables are preferred over partitioni=
ng:</p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Scenario</th>
<th>Why Separate Tables Work Better</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Each table has a different schema</strong></td>
<td>Partitions must all share the same columns. If each &quot;slice&quot; h=
as different columns, you need separate tables.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Tables belong to different databases/servers</strong></td>
<td>Partitioning is within a single MySQL instance. If you're sharding acro=
ss servers, you need separate tables (or databases).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Per-tenant isolation</strong></td>
<td>In multi-tenant systems, giving each tenant their own table (or databas=
e) provides stronger isolation than partitions.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>You need foreign keys</strong></td>
<td>Partitioned tables don't support FK. If FK constraints are critical, se=
parate tables with FK may be better.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<div class=3D"markdown-alert markdown-alert-important"><p class=3D"markdown=
-alert-title"><svg class=3D"octicon octicon-report mr-2" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 =
16" version=3D"1.1" width=3D"16" height=3D"16" aria-hidden=3D"true"><path d=
=3D"M0 1.75C0 .784.784 0 1.75 0h12.5C15.216 0 16 .784 16 1.75v9.5A1.75 1.75=
 0 0 1 14.25 13H8.06l-2.573 2.573A1.458 1.458 0 0 1 3 14.543V13H1.75A1.75 1=
.75 0 0 1 0 11.25Zm1.75-.25a.25.25 0 0 0-.25.25v9.5c0 .138.112.25.25.25h2a.=
75.75 0 0 1 .75.75v2.19l2.72-2.72a.749.749 0 0 1 .53-.22h6.5a.25.25 0 0 0 .=
25-.25v-9.5a.25.25 0 0 0-.25-.25Zm7 2.25v2.5a.75.75 0 0 1-1.5 0v-2.5a.75.75=
 0 0 1 1.5 0ZM9 9a1 1 0 1 1-2 0 1 1 0 0 1 2 0Z"></path></svg>Important</p><=
p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> Partitioning gives you the performance bene=
fits of splitting data across smaller chunks <strong>without</strong> the a=
pplication complexity of managing multiple tables. Your app queries one tab=
le, MySQL does the routing. Always prefer partitioning over manual table sp=
litting unless you have a specific reason not to.</p>
</div>
<hr>
<hr>
</section><section class=3D"sec"><h2 id=3D"sharding--splitting-one-database=
-across-many-servers" data-toc=3D"42. Sharding =E2=80=94 Splitting One Data=
base Across Many =E2=80=A6"><span class=3D"sec-n">42.</span> Sharding =E2=
=80=94 Splitting One Database Across Many Servers</h2>
<p>=F0=9F=94=97 <strong>Quiz yourself after reading:</strong> <a href=3D"..=
/CoreConcepts/scalability-question-bank.md">Scalability question bank</a> (=
Q16, 17, 31, 32, 38, 47, 57) =C2=B7 <a href=3D"../CoreConcepts/consistent-h=
ashing-question-bank.md">Consistent hashing question bank</a></p>
<p><strong>Sharding</strong> splits the rows of one logical table across <s=
trong>many independent database servers</strong> (shards). Each shard holds=
 only a slice of the data, and all shards share the <strong>same schema</st=
rong>. A <strong>shard key</strong> (e.g. <code>user_id</code>) decides whi=
ch shard a row lives on, and a router (your app, a proxy like Vitess, or th=
e database itself) sends each query to the right shard.</p>
