<hr>
<h3 id=3D"sharding-vs-partitioning-vs-replication" data-toc=3D"Sharding vs =
Partitioning vs Replication">Sharding vs Partitioning vs Replication</h3>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th><strong><a href=3D"#database-partitioning">Partitioning</a></strong></t=
h>
<th><strong>Sharding</strong></th>
<th><strong><a href=3D"#replication-in-mysql--read-replicas-lag--failover">=
Replication</a></strong></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>What it does</strong></td>
<td>Splits a table into pieces <strong>inside one server</strong></td>
<td>Splits a table across <strong>many servers</strong></td>
<td><strong>Copies</strong> the whole data set to more servers</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Each node holds</strong></td>
<td>(one server holds all pieces)</td>
<td>A <strong>different slice</strong> of the rows</td>
<td>The <strong>same</strong> rows</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Scales</strong></td>
<td>Query speed (pruning), easy deletes</td>
<td><strong>Writes + storage + reads</strong></td>
<td><strong>Reads</strong> only</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>They're usually <strong>combined</strong>: each shard is a primary with =
its own replicas.</p>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"benefits-vs-drawbacks-1" data-toc=3D"Benefits vs Drawbacks">Benef=
its vs Drawbacks</h3>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>=E2=9C=85 Benefits</th>
<th>=E2=9D=8C Drawbacks</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Scales writes</strong>: each shard's primary takes only its sha=
re of the writes. Replicas can't do this</td>
<td><strong>Much more complexity</strong>: routing, many servers, more fail=
ure modes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Scales storage</strong>: data bigger than any single machine ca=
n still fit</td>
<td><strong>Queries without the shard key</strong> must ask every shard (<s=
trong>scatter-gather</strong>): slower and costlier</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Smaller data per server</strong>: indexes and the working set f=
it in RAM, so queries get faster</td>
<td><strong>No cross-shard JOINs</strong>: rows you want to join may live o=
n different servers</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Fault isolation</strong>: one shard down affects only its slice=
 of users, not everyone</td>
<td><strong>No cross-shard ACID transactions</strong>: need workarounds (Sa=
gas, 2PC)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Data placement</strong>: e.g. EU users on EU servers for latenc=
y or data-residency law</td>
<td><strong>Unique constraints and auto-increment IDs</strong> only work pe=
r shard</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Cheaper hardware</strong>: many normal servers instead of one h=
uge one</td>
<td><strong>Uneven load (hot shards)</strong> and <strong>resharding</stron=
g> (moving data later) are painful</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td></td>
<td><strong>Operations multiply</strong>: backups, monitoring and schema ch=
anges across every shard</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
