<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Strategy</th>
<th>How the shard is picked</th>
<th>=E2=9C=85 Good</th>
<th>=E2=9D=8C Bad</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Range</strong></td>
<td>Key ranges: <code>1=E2=80=931M =E2=86=92 shard 1</code>, <code>1M=E2=80=
=932M =E2=86=92 shard 2</code></td>
<td>Fast range queries on the key</td>
<td><strong>Hot shard</strong> when keys keep growing (all new rows hit the=
 last range)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Hash</strong></td>
<td><code>hash(key) mod N</code></td>
<td><strong>Even</strong> spread</td>
<td>Range queries hit every shard; adding a shard moves most keys</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Consistent hashing</strong></td>
<td>Keys and shards on a hash ring</td>
<td>Adding a shard moves only <strong>~1/N</strong> of the keys</td>
<td>Slightly more complex (<a href=3D"../CoreConcepts/consistent-hashing-qu=
estion-bank.md">deep dive</a>)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Directory</strong></td>
<td>A lookup table maps <code>key =E2=86=92 shard</code></td>
<td>Fully flexible (e.g. a big customer on its own shard)</td>
<td>Extra lookup per request; the directory must never go down</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Geo</strong></td>
<td>By region: <code>EU users =E2=86=92 EU shard</code></td>
<td>Low latency, data-residency laws</td>
<td>Regions differ a lot in size</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>Why plain <code>mod N</code> hurts:</strong> going from 4 to 5 s=
hards changes the result of <code>hash(key) mod N</code> for ~80% of keys, =
so almost all data moves. Consistent hashing, or hashing into a <strong>fix=
ed number of logical shards</strong> (e.g. 1,024) mapped onto servers, keep=
s it to ~20%: you move whole logical shards, never individual rows.</p>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"choosing-the-shard-key" data-toc=3D"Choosing the Shard Key">Choos=
ing the Shard Key</h3>
<p>A good shard key:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Appears in almost every query</strong> (<code>WHERE user_id =3D=
 ?</code>), so the query goes to <strong>one</strong> shard.</li>
<li><strong>Has high cardinality</strong> (millions of values; <code>countr=
y</code> or <code>status</code> won't do).</li>
<li><strong>Spreads rows and traffic evenly</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Keeps related data together</strong> (a user's orders, items an=
d payments on one shard =E2=86=92 local joins and transactions).</li>
<li><strong>Never changes</strong> (changing it means moving the row).</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Example: an e-commerce <code>orders</code> table</strong></p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Candidate</th>
<th>Verdict</th>
<th>Why</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>user_id</code></td>
<td>=E2=9C=85 Best default</td>
<td>&quot;My orders&quot; hits one shard; a user's data stays together</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>order_id</code> (hashed)</td>
<td>=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F</td>
<td>Even spread, but &quot;all orders of user 42&quot; asks every shard</td=
>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>created_at</code></td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C</td>
<td>All new orders go to one shard =E2=86=92 hot shard</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>country</code></td>
<td>=E2=9D=8C</td>
<td>Few values, very skewed</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>For keys built from two columns (<code>user_id</code> + <code>order_id</=
code>), see <a href=3D"#multikey--composite-sharding-eg-user_id--order_id">=
Multikey / Composite Sharding</a> below.</p>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"common-problems--fixes" data-toc=3D"Common Problems &amp; Fixes">=
Common Problems &amp; Fixes</h3>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Problem</th>
<th>Fix</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Query without the shard key</strong> (e.g. find user by email)<=
/td>
<td>Add the key to the query, or keep a small global lookup table (<code>em=
ail =E2=86=92 user_id</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>JOIN across shards</strong></td>
<td>Shard related tables by the same key; copy small reference tables (coun=
tries, currencies) to every shard</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Transaction across shards</strong></td>
<td>Design so one business action stays on one shard; otherwise a <strong>S=
aga</strong> (local steps + undo steps) or 2PC</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Unique values across shards</strong> (email)</td>
<td>A global uniqueness table, written first</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Unique IDs</strong></td>
<td>Snowflake-style IDs or UUID v7 instead of <code>AUTO_INCREMENT</code> (=
<a href=3D"#uuid-vs-auto-increment-integer-as-primary-key">trade-offs</a>)<=
/td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Hot shard / hot key</strong> (celebrity, giant tenant)</td>
<td>Hash instead of range; move that key to its own shard; cache its reads<=
/td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Shards fill up</strong></td>
<td>Reshard: copy data to new shards, stream ongoing changes, then switch r=
outing. Tools like Vitess automate this</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Reports over all data</strong></td>
<td>Copy data into a data warehouse and run analytics there</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
