a.75.75 0 0 1-.75-.75Z"></path></svg>Tip</p><p><strong>&quot;How would you =
shard this database?&quot;</strong>
&quot;Only once writes or data size outgrow one primary. Indexes, a bigger =
box, replicas and caching come first. I'd pick a shard key that's in almost=
 every query, has high cardinality and spreads load evenly. For orders that=
's <code>user_id</code>, so each user's data and transactions stay on one s=
hard. I'd hash it into fixed logical shards so adding servers is easy, and =
give each shard its own replicas. The trade-offs are scatter-gather for que=
ries without the key, no cross-shard joins or transactions (I'd use a looku=
p table or a Saga), and the need for globally unique IDs.&quot;</p>
</div>
