<hr>
</section><section class=3D"sec"><h2 id=3D"multikey--composite-sharding-eg-=
user_id--order_id" data-toc=3D"43. Multikey / Composite Sharding (e.g. user=
_id + =E2=80=A6"><span class=3D"sec-n">43.</span> Multikey / Composite Shar=
ding (e.g. <code>user_id</code> + <code>order_id</code>)</h2>
<p><strong>Sharding</strong> splits one logical table across many physical =
databases or nodes. A <strong>shard key</strong> (or partition key) decides=
 which node stores a row (basics, strategies and trade-offs: <a href=3D"#sh=
arding--splitting-one-database-across-many-servers">Sharding</a>). <strong>=
Multikey sharding</strong> means the routing rule uses <strong>more than on=
e logical attribute</strong> =E2=80=94 often written as a composite such as=
 <code>(user_id, order_id)</code> =E2=80=94 but the critical detail is <str=
ong>how</strong> those keys are combined: one column may define <em>which s=
hard</em>, another may define <em>order within the shard</em>, or both may =
be hashed together (which changes query patterns completely).</p>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"two-different-meanings-of-user_id--order_id" data-toc=3D"Two Diff=
erent Meanings of &quot;user_id + order_id&quot;">Two Different Meanings of=
 &quot;<code>user_id</code> + <code>order_id</code>&quot;</h3>
<h4 id=3D"a-good-pattern--shard-by-user_id-only-order_id-is-local-identity-=
-sort-key">A) Good pattern =E2=80=94 <strong>Shard by <code>user_id</code> =
only</strong>; <code>order_id</code> is local identity + sort key</h4>
<p>All rows for a given user live on <strong>one</strong> shard. <code>orde=
r_id</code> (or <code>created_at</code>) is used for uniqueness and <code>O=
RDER BY</code>, not for picking the shard.</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">text</span></div><pre><code class=3D=
"hljs">shard_id =3D hash(user_id) % NUM_SHARDS</code></pre></div>
<p><strong>Effect on &quot;last 5 orders for user 42&quot;:</strong> One ro=
und-trip to the correct shard. The database can use an index on <code>(user=
_id, created_at DESC)</code> or <code>(user_id, order_id DESC)</code> and s=
top after 5 rows.</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Router sends this to shard =3D hash(4=
2) % N only</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> order_id, total, created_at
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> orders
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> user_id <span class=3D"hljs-opera=
tor">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">42</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ORDER</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">BY</=
span> created_at <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">DESC</span>
LIMIT <span class=3D"hljs-number">5</span>;</code></pre></div>
<p>This is the usual design for an <strong>order history</strong> feature.<=
/p>
<h4 id=3D"b-risky-pattern--shard-by-a-function-of-both-eg-hashuser_id-order=
_id">B) Risky pattern =E2=80=94 <strong>Shard by a function of both</strong=
> (e.g. <code>hash(user_id, order_id)</code>)</h4>
<p>Each <em>order</em> may land on a different shard even for the same user=
. No single shard has &quot;all orders for user 42.&quot;</p>
<p><strong>Effect on &quot;last 5 orders for user 42&quot;:</strong> You mu=
st <strong>scatter-gather</strong>: run the same query (or index lookup) on=
 <strong>every</strong> shard, collect up to 5 rows from each, then <strong=
>merge and sort</strong> in the application or a coordinator to produce the=
 true &quot;last 5 globally.&quot; Cost grows with shard count (latency, CP=
U, open connections).</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Per shard k (app or proxy runs this N=
 times)</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> order_id, total, created_at
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">FROM</span> orders
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> user_id <span class=3D"hljs-opera=
tor">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">42</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ORDER</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">BY</=
span> created_at <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">DESC</span>
LIMIT <span class=3D"hljs-number">5</span>;
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- Then: merge all N result sets, sort by crea=
ted_at, take top 5</span></code></pre></div>
<p>So <strong><code>user_id</code> + <code>order_id</code> in the shard fun=
ction</strong> is rarely what you want for <strong>per-user lists</strong>;=
 it can make sense when your hot path is always <strong>point lookups</stro=
ng> by both keys (e.g. &quot;fetch this exact order&quot;) and you accept e=
xpensive user-scoped scans.</p>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"example-schema-on-one-shard-same-sql-shape-everywhere" data-toc=
=3D"Example Schema on One Shard (same SQL shape =E2=80=A6">Example Schema o=
n One Shard (same SQL shape everywhere)</h3>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keywor=
d">TABLE</span> orders (
    user_id     <span class=3D"hljs-type">BIGINT</span> <span class=3D"hljs=
-keyword">NOT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">NULL</span>,
    order_id    <span class=3D"hljs-type">BIGINT</span> <span class=3D"hljs=
-keyword">NOT</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">NULL</span>,      <span c=
lass=3D"hljs-comment">-- unique per user, or globally unique (Snowflake/UUI=
D)</span>
    total       <span class=3D"hljs-type">DECIMAL</span>(<span class=3D"hlj=
s-number">10</span>,<span class=3D"hljs-number">2</span>),
    created_at  <span class=3D"hljs-type">TIMESTAMP</span>(<span class=3D"h=
ljs-number">3</span>) <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">NOT</span> <span class=
=3D"hljs-keyword">NULL</span>,
    <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">PRIMARY</span> KEY (user_id, order_id),
    KEY idx_user_created (user_id, created_at <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">=
DESC</span>)
);</code></pre></div>
<ul>
<li><strong>Shard by <code>user_id</code>:</strong> Primary key and seconda=
ry index both start with <code>user_id</code> =E2=86=92 efficient <strong>s=
ingle-shard</strong> access for that user.</li>
<li>If the <strong>physical</strong> DB is sharded, the router adds <code>A=
ND user_id =3D ?</code> so each query is sent to one node.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"sql-patterns-after-sharding" data-toc=3D"SQL Patterns After Shard=
ing">SQL Patterns After Sharding</h3>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Need</th>
<th>Shard by <code>user_id</code></th>
<th>Shard by <code>hash(user_id, order_id)</code></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Last 5 orders for user</td>
<td>One shard; <code>WHERE user_id =3D ? ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 5</=
code></td>
<td>All shards + merge</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Order by id for known user</td>
<td>One shard; <code>WHERE user_id =3D ? AND order_id =3D ?</code></td>
<td>Must know shard or query all (if only order_id known, <strong>bad</stro=
ng>)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Admin: recent orders globally</td>
<td>Expensive anyway; may need <strong>OLAP</strong> / search index / separ=
ate read model</td>
<td>Same problem</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>Lookup by <code>order_id</code> alone</strong> when the shard ke=
y is only <code>user_id</code>: you need either a <strong>secondary routing=
 table</strong> (<code>order_id =E2=86=92 user_id</code> or <code>order_id =
=E2=86=92 shard_id</code>), a <strong>global index</strong> service, or <st=
rong>duplicate storage</strong> (e.g. order details keyed by <code>order_id=
</code> on a different sharding scheme). Otherwise the system does not know=
 which shard to hit.</p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- If you maintain order_id -&gt; user_i=
d in Redis or a tiny lookup table:</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- 1) SELECT user_id FROM order_index WHERE or=
der_id =3D 9001;</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- 2) SELECT * FROM orders WHERE user_id =3D ?=
 AND order_id =3D 9001;  -- single shard</span></code></pre></div>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"practical-takeaways" data-toc=3D"Practical Takeaways">Practical T=
akeaways</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Align the shard key with your hottest queries.</strong> For &qu=
ot;my order history,&quot; that is almost always <strong><code>user_id</cod=
e> (or <code>tenant_id</code>)</strong>, not <code>hash(user_id, order_id)<=
/code>.</li>
<li><strong><code>user_id</code> + <code>order_id</code> as a composite pri=
mary key</strong> is normal; it does <strong>not</strong> mean both must pa=
rticipate in the <strong>shard</strong> function.</li>
<li><strong>Multikey sharding</strong> that hashes both dimensions often op=
timizes <strong>even spread</strong> or <strong>point reads</strong> at the=
 cost of <strong>range/list queries</strong> =E2=80=94 document that trade-=
off in design reviews.</li>
<li><strong>Scatter-gather</strong> for &quot;last N per user&quot; is corr=
ect but costly; cap shard count impact with caching, a <strong>per-user rec=
ent orders</strong> cache, or a <strong>CQRS</strong> read model fed by eve=
nts.</li>
</ol>
<hr>
<hr>
</section><section class=3D"sec"><h2 id=3D"innodb-configuration-flags--chea=
t-sheet" data-toc=3D"44. InnoDB Configuration Flags =E2=80=94 Cheat Sheet">=
<span class=3D"sec-n">44.</span> InnoDB Configuration Flags =E2=80=94 Cheat=
 Sheet</h2>
<p>=F0=9F=94=97 <strong>Reference:</strong> <a href=3D"https://dev.mysql.co=
m/doc/refman/8.0/en/innodb-parameters.html" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopen=
er">MySQL Docs =E2=80=94 InnoDB Startup Options and System Variables</a></p=
>
<p>InnoDB's behaviour is controlled by <strong>system variables</strong> (&=
quot;flags&quot;), set in <code>my.cnf</code> or at runtime. This is a quic=
k reference for the ones that matter most: what each <strong>controls</stro=
ng>, its <strong>default</strong>, and <strong>when to change it</strong>. =
The =F0=9F=94=97 links go to the section of these notes that explains the m=
echanism behind it.</p>
<p><strong>How to see and change them:</strong></p>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">SQL</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SHOW</span> VARIABLES <span class=3D"hlj=
s-keyword">LIKE</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">&#x27;innodb_buffer_pool=
%&#x27;</span>;              <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- current values=
</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SET</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">GLOBAL=
</span> innodb_lock_wait_timeout <span class=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> <=
span class=3D"hljs-number">10</span>;               <span class=3D"hljs-com=
ment">-- dynamic flags: change at runtime</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">SET</span> PERSIST innodb_io_capacity <span cl=
ass=3D"hljs-operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"hljs-number">2000</span>;  =
                <span class=3D"hljs-comment">-- 8.0+: change AND survive a =
restart</span></code></pre></div>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">ini</span></div><pre><code class=3D"=
hljs"><span class=3D"hljs-comment"># my.cnf: static flags (marked =F0=9F=94=
=92 below) can only be set here, then restart MySQL</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-section">[mysqld]</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-attr">innodb_buffer_pool_size</span> =3D <span class=3D=
"hljs-number">24</span>G</code></pre></div>
<div class=3D"markdown-alert markdown-alert-note"><p class=3D"markdown-aler=
t-title"><svg class=3D"octicon octicon-info mr-2" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 16" ver=
sion=3D"1.1" width=3D"16" height=3D"16" aria-hidden=3D"true"><path d=3D"M0 =
8a8 8 0 1 1 16 0A8 8 0 0 1 0 8Zm8-6.5a6.5 6.5 0 1 0 0 13 6.5 6.5 0 0 0 0-13=
ZM6.5 7.75A.75.75 0 0 1 7.25 7h1a.75.75 0 0 1 .75.75v2.75h.25a.75.75 0 0 1 =
0 1.5h-2a.75.75 0 0 1 0-1.5h.25v-2h-.25a.75.75 0 0 1-.75-.75ZM8 6a1 1 0 1 1=
 0-2 1 1 0 0 1 0 2Z"></path></svg>Note</p><p>Defaults below are <strong>MyS=
QL 8.0</strong>. <strong>MySQL 8.4 LTS changed several of them</strong>: e.=
g. <code>innodb_adaptive_hash_index</code> is OFF, <code>innodb_change_buff=
ering</code> is <code>none</code>, <code>innodb_io_capacity</code> is 10000=
, and <code>innodb_flush_method</code> is <code>O_DIRECT</code> on Linux. A=
lways check <code>SHOW VARIABLES</code> on your own server. =F0=9F=94=92 =
